  {
    "title": "PULLING IT TOGETHER - Gilberto's season-high 33 points, fourth-quarter surge fuel Lions past Warriors",
    "date": "2024-01-03",
    "author": "William Haufe; bhaufe@chespub.com",
    "content": "PULLING IT TOGETHER - Gilberto's season-high 33 points, fourth-quarter surge fuel Lions past Warriors\n\n January 3, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: William Haufe; bhaufe@chespub.com | Page: 28 | Section: Sports \n \n 675\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE - With the graduation of KJ Smothers, Nate Ford and Amir Coles, and with senior big man Jai Roy yet to play this season, Queen Anne's County High boys' head basketball coach Jeff Hollis thought it might take a while for his team's experience and youth to come together.If Tuesday night was any indication, Hollis' crew is coming together just fine.\nVince Gilberto scored a season-high 33 points, including 17 in a 29-16 fourth-quarter surge that cemented a 78-56 Queen Anne's County victory over Easton.\n\"We lost players last year, but this year we got a lot of young players,\" Gilberto said.\n\"We got to bring them up, bring them together, being the captains and seniors of this team. And we brought them together tonight, and we got the win as a team.\"\nThe two-time defending North Bayside champion-Lions (6-1 overall, 5-0 North) held a slim 15-12 lead after the first quarter. Gilberto then started heating up, scoring Queen Anne's last 10 points of the second quarter, giving the home team a 31-21 halftime lead.\n\"As a shooter I get a lot of shots up during the week,\" said Gilberto, who finished with six 3-pointers. \"So I just come out here, played confident; confident in my game, confident in my teammates. I guess it was a hot night.\"\nBut Hollis didn't necessarily think his senior sharpshooter was on fire.\n\"I don't think it was a hot night for him,\" Hollis said.\n\"He ran to all the right spots.\nHe got to where he was supposed to get, and his teammates found him. And honestly that's what I expect.\"\nHollace Branch's inside bucket extended Queen Anne's lead to 33-21 with 27 seconds elapsed in the third quarter. But Kahlil Batson's follow and a 3-pointer from Marshall Bailey (team-high 21 points) pulled Easton (4-3, 3-1) within 33-26 with less than a minute gone in the period.\nBut every time the Warriors got their deficit to under double digits, the Lions responded.\nCollin Woolford (14 points) hit a layup then swished consecutive 3-pointers to give Queen Anne's a 43-31 lead with 3:36 left in the third.\nEaston again whittled the lead to seven before Gilberto splashed a 3 from deep in the corner for a 46-37 lead.\nThe Lions carried a ninepoint advantage into the fourth, but two free throws from Diontaye Copper and back-toback baskets inside the paint from Zymier Ramirez pulled Easton with 51-46 with 6:20 left.\nBut Amari Baynard (13 points) converted a dish from Jadereon Thompson for a layup.\nGilberto followed with a layup, then buried two 3-pointers for a 61-46 lead that prompted an Easton timeout. But the Warriors couldn't make a serious dent in their deficit the rest of the way, as the Lions outscored Easton 27-10 over the final 5:17.\n\"We've been trying to do it all year,\" Hollis said of Queen Anne's finding the open man.\n\"Once again we started with a young team but now they're starting to come together.\n\"And I've been harping on the defense all year and today we actually came to play defense,\" Hollis continued. \"I like what we did today.\"\nCAMBRIDGE-SD 86 N. DORCHESTER 37\nCAMBRIDGE - LaShaun Moody and Jaden Harris each scored 16 points as the Vikings routed their county rival.\nSemaj Pinder added 13 points for Cambridge-South Dorchester.\nJae'zeon Cephas' 13 points led North Dorchester.\nGIRLS' BASKETBALL\nQUEEN ANNE'S 62 EASTON 30\nCENTREVILLE - Freshman Bea Holthaus had 20 points and 16 rebounds to lead the unbeaten Lions.\nLaila Coles had 16 points, seven steals and five assists for Queen Anne's.\nSerenity Velez led Easton with 13 points.\nCAMBRIDGE-SD 57 N. DORCHESTER 21\nSHILOH - LeAsia Todd scored 33 points to go with 18 rebounds, four blocks and one steal to lead the Vikings.\nDamiya Henry had 10 points, four rebounds and four steals for Cambridge-SD.\nNORTH CAROLINE 39, KENT 27\nWORTON - Malaya Phillips had 14 points and Ashleigh Lawry finished with nine to lead the Bulldogs. \n \n Caption: Queen Anne's County senior Vince Gilberto (2) glides in for two of his game-high 33 points during the second half of Tuesday night's 78-56 victory over Easton.\n\n Easton's Tymire Thomas (35) tries for a put-back over Queen Anne's Jimmy Holthaus during Tuesday night's first half. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
