  {
    "title": "Warriors quietly move past Vikings",
    "date": "2024-01-11",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Warriors quietly move past Vikings\n\n January 11, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 924\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — There were no fans in the bleachers which created somewhat of an unusual atmosphere for a midseason game.\"It was definitely different,\" Easton High senior Sisi Sama said. \"It was quiet.\"\n\"It was really weird,\" Cambridge-South Dorchester junior standout LeAsia Todd added. \"I felt like we were just at practice honestly.\"\nDue to the altercations during the Cambridge-SD at Easton football game on Oct. 27, Thursday's girls basketball game between the two schools at Easton was played without fans.\nBut Warrior head coach Lesley Staehli had a pre-game message for her team.\n\"We just knew that we had to come out here and be as normal as possible, if that makes sense,\" Staehli said.\nIt seemed to make sense to the Warriors, who used a 13-0 second-half surge to gain separation before holding off a late Vikings' comeback bid en route to a 41-33 victory.\n\"With playing the national anthem at the beginning to announcing (the starting lineups) with no one in the stands, we just wanted to be as normal as possible, and just play our game,\" said Staehli after Easton improved to 4-6 overall, 4-3 North Bayside. \"I told them coming into the game, 'We've got to play Easton ball.' We've been struggling and we come out tonight and I think this is going to be a turnaround for us for the season.\"\nCambridge-SD (5-3, 4-3) led only once in the first quarter, when Destiny Johnson (team-high 12 points) splashed a 3-pointer with 2:11 left in the period for a 6-5 Vikings lead.\nEaston closed the opening period on a 6-0 run, and maintained a slim lead through the majority of the second quarter. Destiny Johnson and Janaya Stanley connected from beyond the 3-point arc to pull Cambridge-SD even at 20-20 with 22 seconds left in the half. But Kate Adelman (10 points) hit a mid-range shot with 11 seconds remaining, nudging the Warriors to a 22-20 halftime lead.\nThat advantage was gone midway through the third period, as Tejanae Ballin made a free throw before Todd (nine points) made a foul shot and converted a put-back for a 24-22 Vikings' lead. Staehli called time with 4:45 to go in the third, then watched guard Serenity Velez (game-high 18 points) hit a 3-pointer to start the Warriors' decisive 13-0 spree that ended with Velez scoring a transition layup.\n\"We just told them to remember to play strong like they did in the first half,\" Staehli said. \"They were outrebounding us and outplaying us. But when we ran the floor in transition and we made them work they got tired. And we had to capitalize on them getting tired, 'cause if not we'd be in trouble.\"\nDown 35-24, Cambridge-SD began pressing the Warriors, and ended an almost 8-minute scoring drought when Kassidy Young hit a bank shot with 4:09 left in the game. Todd's 3-point play pulled Cambridge-SD within 35-29. Destiny Johnson drilled her fourth and final 3-pointer, shaving the Warriors lead to 35-32 with 2:07 left.\nBut the Vikings couldn't get the ball into the low post to Todd down the stretch, frustrating head coach Shamya Johnson while allowing Easton to slowly pad its lead over the final minutes.\n\"They weren't even attempting a good pass to her,\" Coach Johnson said of her team not working the ball inside to the 6-foot-3 Todd. \"She's got to work extra hard because you don't want to feed her the ball. That's crazy to me. We had the whole advantage.\n\"As soon as they get four or five points we're hanging our heads, not realizing the game is still just one or two possessions,\" Shamya Johnson continued. \"You've got to finish the game and have the basketball IQ to know there was nobody on the floor that was as tall as LeAsia. And not only did you have LeAsia, you also had Azeyera (Johnson) who can post up. They weren't getting either one of them the ball.\"\nAdelman scored a layup with 1:40 remaining to end Easton's near 4-minute scoring lull. Velez hit a pair of free throws to push Easton's lead to 39-33 with 34.5 seconds left before Adelman made two free throws for the 41-33 final.\n\"We wish there were fans so they could see our potential because before this game we haven't really played to our full potential,\" said Siang, who finished with 11 points and 16 rebounds. \"We had a lot of chemistry and I think we just played together as a team today. I just tried to do whatever I could for the team.\"\nThe Vikings still figure to be a favorite in the Class 1A East Regional playoffs, but their coach knows they need to regain the form that helped them start the season 4-1.\n\"Energy was off,\" Johnson said. \"But I told them 'Regardless, if you've got 100 fans in the stands or zero, you're supposed to give it your all on that court for 32 minutes. The crowd can't determine how much heart you're going to give a game.'\"\nN. Caroline 53 Col. Richardson 28\nAMERICAN CORNER — Selaya Garrison scored 19 points and Malayah Phillips finished with 10 as the Bulldogs improved to 5-3, 3-4.\nQueen Anne's 48, Kent Island 45\nSTEVENSVILLE — Freshman Bea Holthaus scored 20 points and snared 11 rebounds as the Lions topped their county rival to take over sole possession of first place in the North Bayside.\nLaila Coles added 11 points for Queen Anne's County (8-0, 7-0).\nSenior Lilli Dauses led Kent Island (6-2, 6-1) with 15 points.\nBoys Basketball\nQueen Anne's 63, Kent Island 57\nCENTREVILLE — Vince Gilberto had 21 points and Jimmy Holthaus finished with 10 to lead the Lions (8-2, 7-1). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
