  {
    "title": "LIONS PIN DOWN HISTORY",
    "date": "2024-01-13",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "LIONS PIN DOWN HISTORY\n\n January 13, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 981\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — John Waters, Queen Anne's County High's girls head wrestling coach, wondered if his team recognized it was wrestling with history.Then again, maybe Queen Anne's was more focused on just winning than getting its name in the record books.\nThe Lions got both Friday night, stringing together five consecutive pinfalls before Kaylynn Bryant delivered a first-period fall in the second-to-last match that clinched Queen Anne's 48-36 victory over Winters Mill in the first girls dual match in the history of the Maryland Public Secondary Schools Athletic Association, where all 14 weight classes competed without a forfeit.\n\"I don't think they realized the magnitude of the match; that Winters Mill was one of the first true girls teams,\" Waters said of his team. \"They don't forfeit weight classes.\"\nOn this night, neither did Queen Anne's.\n\"They come in every day; they work,\" Waters said of his team. \"And it's just like, 'OK, cool. It's time to wrestle.' I'm extremely proud of them.\"\nWinters Mill defeated Queen Anne's a year ago in a dual meet in Centreville. The two met again in the finals of Maverick Duals at Manchester Valley in December, with Queen Anne's earning a 48-36 victory — the same score as Friday — ending the Falcons' two-year win streak.\nFriday's third clash had the early markings of another close finish.\nQueen Anne's Annalise Michael — one of seven first-year wrestlers in the Lions' starting lineup Friday — rallied from a 12-5 deficit to pin Nijae Tate in 4:21 of the 100-pound match.\n\"Started out with Annalise; that was a big swing match that we won,\" Waters said. \"She fought off her back a couple of times and ended up getting the pin. I think that provided a lot of energy for the girls and they fed off it.\"\nWinters Mill tied the match 6-all at 105, Alexa Hresko earning a first-period fall over Celeste Molina. Queen Anne's needed just 1:19 to move back in front, when Ally Conley, who won a state title in 2022, caught Carli Ireland in a headlock and hit a throw for a fall at 110. The Falcons again answered, as Christina Padilla (115) pinned Bevyn Branham in 1:03.\nThe Lions then reeled off five consecutive pins to build a commanding 30-point cushion.\nBree Conard — granddaughter of Hall of Fame coach Dan Zottarelli, who guided Kent County High's boys to back-to-back 2A-1A state titles in 2001-02, and was in attendance Friday — was taken down in the first period, but worked off bottom to register a fall in 2:28 over Jayla Shropshire at 120, giving the Lions a lead they never relinquished at 18-12.\nFinley Patchett (125), another first-year wrestler, rolled Taylor Scholl to her back for a pin in 1:22. Queen Anne's Delaney Gray followed suit at 130, pinning Hailee Smith in 1:21 for a 30-12 lead. Linda Vail continued the roll, with a fall over Maggie Morley in 3:38 of the 135-pound match. Winters Mill's Kayla Goodwin (140) looked ready to end that streak, when she built a 4-1 lead on Keira Corcoran in the first period. But Corcoran scored an early reverse in the second en route to pinning Goodwin in 3:22.\n\"Tonight we got the matchups that we wanted and they were better,\" said Winters Mill head coach John Lowe, a St. Michaels High graduate who coached Waters at McDaniel College from 1997-99. \"All props to coach Waters. He does a great job.\"\nBut Waters, who won a state title at 103 pounds for Queen Anne's in 1996, deflected any credit afterwards.\n\"I'm happy for all the girls,\" Waters said. \"I'm extremely happy for (boys head) coach (Dave) Stricker. I just sat in the corner. If it wasn't for coach Stricker and his vision a couple of years ago, we wouldn't have the girls team.\n\"It just so happened that he's coaching one team and we needed a coach for the other team,\" Waters continued. \"This is a team effort. I'm just one of the coaches. We have several coaches that are at practice every day that help these girls, and boys, wrestle. It's great for the program.\"\nWinters Mill ended the Lions' run at 145, when Kimora Harrid pinned Lilly Hammond in the first period, and began whittling away at its deficit. The Falcons' Gabbie McLeod followed with a first-period fall over Morgan Morris at 155. Maiah Sweet (165) moved Winters Mill within 42-30 with a first-period pin of Addie Dickens.\nThe Falcons had a chance to draw even with two matches remaining, but those hopes ended when Bryant pinned Morgan Tolle with 7 seconds remaining in the first period of the 190-pound match, pushing the Lions' lead to 48-30.\n\"We had a couple of girls, they got their first varsity matches tonight and stepped in for some girls that were injured, and some that were sick,\" Waters said. \"To not have our true lineup and to come in and do what we did. … Pretty impressive. I give all the credit to the girls.\"\nWinters Mill's Jade Love (235) capped the match with a first-period pin of Maggie Rooney.\n\"They won it tonight. But we're going to focus on March now,\" Lowe said in reference to this year's state tournament, where a girls' team champion will be crowned for the first time in MPSSAA history. \"We've got some stuff to work on before March.\"\nStricker, who watched his boys team defeat Winters Mill, 42-34, before losing to Southern of Anne Arundel, 42-33, snuck a couple glances at the girls match between the two boys dual bouts.\n\"We're probably the two biggest teams in the state of Maryland,\" Stricker said of Queen Anne's and Winters Mill, who figure to get competition from Francis Scott Key, La Plata and Manchester Valley at the state tournament. \"To go toe-to-toe three times (with Winters Mill), it's what you want to grow the sport. They're exciting matches. Hope we can convince somebody up top that we can have a girls state duals at some point.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
