  {
    "title": "KINGS AND QUEENS",
    "date": "2024-01-18",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "KINGS AND QUEENS\n\n January 18, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1226\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n SNOW HILL — It was a first for Cambridge-South Dorchester High's boys track program and head coach Clint Falduto.But it was the last conference indoor title run for Kent Island's girls team, at least under head coach Justin Holland.\nTori Willis Jr., won two individual titles to lead the Vikings to their first boys team crown indoors in school history, and Gabrielle Bernhard struck gold twice and set a new standard Wednesday, pacing the Buccaneers to their fifth girls title in six years during the Bayside Conference indoor track and field championships at the Worcester County Recreation Center.\n\"I was nervous,\" said Holland of the girls' competition, which ended with Kent Island totaling 96½ points — 23 ahead of runner-up Parkside.\nHolland, who is set to retire from coaching after this season, had reason to be nervous.\nHistorically a power in the middle- and long-distance events, the Bucs did not score a point in the 1,600 or 3,200 meters Wednesday, had Kaitlyn Stevens place fifth for their only points in the 800, and were an uncharacteristic fifth in the 4x800 relay.\n\"This is not the stereotypical Kent Island track team,\" Holland said. \"The four-by-eight (relay), I can't remember us not either winning, or you know, (placing) top three in the four-by-eight. I'm not used to this.\n\"And then when it comes to the (800), that's when we usually win the meet,\" Holland continued. \"It's tight and then we go to the 800 and we triple score in the eight, and we seal the deal, because everybody knows we're going to be in the top four in the four-by-four relay. It was interesting.\"\nBernhard helped make up for those rare deficiencies, leading a 1-2-4 finish in both the 500 meters and pole vault.\n\"I'm happy that she switched over from cheerleading to track,\" Holland said of Bernhard, who did cheer and ran indoors last year, but is running track only this year.\nLike many of Wednesday's competitors, Bernhard ran all her events in a fairly tight time frame. She placed second to Cambridge-SD's Enazajah Young (8.76 seconds) in the 55 hurdles, clocking a 9.03. She came right back and took fourth (45.26) in the 300. Two events later, she was back on the track, running the 500.\n\"Last year she was in the four-by-two (relay), but I had to drop her all the time because the four-by-two and the pole vault were all in the same (time) spot,\" Holland said. \"So this year she's been scoring individually in the three and the five. And she wasn't a 500 runner. But she ran cross country this year so she knew she was in shape. So I said, 'Let's try the 500.' Of course that's the one she won.\"\nBut it took some doing, as she rallied past sophomore teammate Reece Heyliger over the final 50 meters to win in 1:24.59. Heyliger, who overcame a false start in the 55 to run strong legs for the second-place 4x200 relay and first-place 4x400 relay, was second in 1:24.78, followed by teammate Siena Koch (1:27.66) in fourth.\nBernhard, who early in the season broke Fiona Mulligan's school indoor pole vault record when she cleared 11 feet, 1 inch, capped her day by winning the pole vault with a new conference record effort of 11-2. The senior was followed by teammates Siena Koch (8 foot) in second and Nora Koch (6-6) in fourth.\nParkside (1:53.95) won the 4x200, but Kent Island's team of Kendra Coffey, Siena Koch, Heyliger and Seryna Stephan was second in 1:57.12. The Bucs punctuated the ninth girls indoor championship of Holland's career in the 4x400 relay, as Heyliger, Coffey, Stephan and Stevens won in 4:31.45.\nThe defending Class 1A state champion in the girls 55 meters, Cambridge-SD's Young held off Parkside's RaNiya Corbin (7.56) to win the conference crown in 7.45, before coming back to win the 55 hurdles in 8.76.\n\"Enazajah is a talent that's hard to match,\" Falduto said. \"She's just incredibly athletic. We're trying to build her stamina up for some more different events other than the short ones. This could be a great opportunity for her future after high school. Just excited to see how she continues to control this season.\"\nFalduto was excited to see what his boys team could do this season, and Wednesday the Vikings showed why, rolling to their first Bayside title indoors with 134½ points — 74 points ahead of runner-up Kent Island.\n\"They're stacked,\" Holland said of Cambridge-SD.\nAt the top of that stack is Willis, who started his day by winning the 55 meters in 6.576 — nipping Parkside's Nasir Bolden (6.578) — then leading a 1-2-3 Vikings sweep in the 55 hurdles. Willis took gold in 8.07 seconds followed by teammates Antonio Blueford (second, 8.121) and Jaydene Smith (third, 8.129).\n\"We've been training Tori to prepare for a day like this, knowing that come the state meet it would not be a similar type (of situation),\" Falduto said in reference to the tight time schedule. \"We knew coming into the Bayside meet and the competition we had, that he had a great opportunity to be a medalist in all four events.\"\nA junior, Willis placed second in the 300 (37.10) to Snow Hill's Logan Hurney (36.88) before closing his day with a third-place in the 500 (1:13.71), completing a 34-point day.\nCompeting indoors for the first time, sophomore Majestic Jones gave Cambridge-SD an added boost, winning the high jump with a leap of 5-10.\nThen there was Viking junior Tekai Drummond, who logged runner-up finishes in the 1,600 (4:55.50), 3,200 (10:49.39) and 800 (2:09.11), and ran a leg on the third-place 4x400 relay.\n\"Tekai has been probably the backbone of our team,\" Falduto said. \"He was a sprinter last year, rarely did an 800 here or there, but as he's moved now to the mile, two mile. What he has done has really changed the dynamics of our team.\n\"We had a goal set on having a championship year,\" Faldutot said of his boys team. \"Starting off the postseason with the indoor Bayside championship is a huge success for our team, and where they're at, and where they're heading. We had a goal set and we accomplished it for the boys. Everybody showed up in my opinion so I was very excited about that.\"\nQueen Anne's County junior Ben Marks looks comfortable and in command en route to winning the boys 1,600 meters (4:51.13) and 800 (2:03.25).\n\"We didn't want him to run too hard,\" Queen Anne's head coach Natasha Dawson said of Marks, who's been focusing on the 1,600 this indoor season. \"He hasn't run the 800 competitively this season. So we needed to make sure he got a good time so that sets him up for a good heat in regions.\"\nEaston's Jed Smith led from start to finish in the boys 3,200. The senior began widening his lead with six laps remaining and won in 10:41.28. Smith joined Colin Smith, Jacob Camper and William Orndoff to give the Warriors victory in the boys 4x800 relay (8:49.39).\nThe Warriors got a second individual champion, as senior Sumayah Wilkins won the high jump, clearing the bar at 5 feet. Easton's Marvin Foster was second in the boys shot put (42-10½).\nNorth Caroline junior Morgan Knott outdueled Snow Hill's Allie Suarezapecheche to win the girls 800 in 2:36.43. Knott also teamed with sophomores Seanna Rupp, Reece Bisesi and Linda Ridgell for victory in the 4x800 relay (10:49.95). Ridgell was runner-up in the 3,200 (12:46.96). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 1,
    "day": 18,
