  {
    "title": "STEP BY STEP",
    "date": "2024-01-27",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "STEP BY STEP\n\n January 27, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 763\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — James Gardner's instructions to freshman Matthew Brunner were simple.\"The last thing I told him before he got out on the mat: Wrestle hard. Stay aggressive. And stay off your back,\" Easton High's head wrestling coach said.\nBrunner checked all those boxes in what was perhaps the biggest match of his young career, pinning St. Michaels' Oliver Cummings midway through the first period of Friday's final bout to seal a 39-30 victory that extended the Warriors' win streak to three.\n\"That's exactly what we needed,\" Gardner said of Brunner's fall which helped raise Easton's record to 3-8 overall and in the Bayside Conference. \"I'm really happy with his outcome and his progression.\"\nSt. Michaels (0-7, 0-7) never trailed until the ninth match, when Easton's Hayden Allen won by forfeit at 157 pounds, nudging the Warriors to a 21-18 lead. The Saints regained the lead at 24-21 in the next match, as Blake Foy built a 4-0 first-period advantage before pinning Kameron Popal in 3:34 of the 165-pound bout.\nBut the Warriors won the three of the remaining four bouts, starting with senior Matt Svehla's 15-second fall over Andrew Moorehead at 175.\n\"He's been one of my most consistent wrestlers this year,\" Gardner said of Svehla, who has yet to lose a conference match this season. \"He's still working, still trying to get better on a daily basis.\n\"Tonight he just knew what he needed to get done,\" Gardner added of Svehla. \"He knew we needed more than a win. We needed a pin because I tell them every point matters. I just appreciate him as a young man and a wrestler. I wish I had him for another year.\"\nSvehla's pin moved Easton into the lead at 27-24. Liam Lewis widened that margin to 33-24 in a swing match, when he pinned Max Reed in 1:23 at 190.\n\"We definitely needed the match with Liam Lewis,\" Gardner said. \"He's normally at 170, but we pushed him up to 190. That was big.\"\nWill Bridges picked up a forfeit at 215, drawing St. Michaels within 33-30, and giving the Saints a chance to either tie or win heading into the final match at 285.\nBut Brunner followed Gardner's instructions, getting a takedown of Cummings before turning his opponent to his back for a fall with 1:02 remaining in the first period.\n\"I knew I just had to go out there and do my job to get the win,\" Brunner said. \"At first I wasn't thinking I was going to get a pin. But I went in there confident and ended up getting the pin.\n\"It feels amazing,\" said Brunner, who after having his arm raised in victory received a big smile and handshake from Gardner before getting surrounded by his celebrating teammates. \"When it's down to you and you go out there and get a win, it's a big one.\"\nSt. Michaels took a 6-0 lead in the opening match, as Will Coale led 4-0 after one period, watched his lead get trimmed to 4-2 by John Seymour 40 seconds into the second before earning a reversal and notching a fall with 3 seconds left in the second period at 106.\nEaston cut that deficit in half via Gabe Trise's 14-8 decision over Hudson Jones at 113. St. Michaels' Andrew Sanchez (120) answered with a 6-1 win over Jakob Barnhart, restoring the Saints' six-point cushion.\nThen came four consecutive forfeits. Easton's Shawn Arnold won a forfeit at 126 to make it 9-9. St. Michaels' Jacob Jenkins took a forfeit at 132, pushing the Saints back to a 15-9 lead. After a double forfeit at 138, Easton's Adonis Schmitt won by forfeit at 144, leaving the match even at 15-all.\nSt. Michaels' took its final lead at 150. After a scoreless first, Easton's Teddy Antal hit a reversal for a 2-0 lead. But St. Michaels' Lane Cappa took over from there. Despite two blood stoppages, Cappa rallied for a 4-2 lead, watched Antal escape, then scored three near-fall points for a 7-3 lead. Antal closed with a takedown with 28 seconds remaining in the match, but couldn't get any closer, as Cappa hit a switch for a 9-5 final and an 18-15 Saints lead.\n\"We're trying to get there,\" said St. Michaels' first-year head coach Steve Mister, one of the most outstanding wrestlers to ever come through the Saints' program. \"We're going through growing pains, but everybody does. We'll get there … sooner than later. But we did what we came to do.\"\nEach team won four matches that were wrestled. Easton had three pinfalls to St. Michaels' two, and also picked up one more forfeit win than its county rival. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 1,
    "day": 27,
