  {
    "title": "Warriors struggle, surge past Bucs",
    "date": "2024-01-27",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Warriors struggle, surge past Bucs\n\n January 27, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 716\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Two players forgot their jerseys.Another forget his stick, and still another was short one sock.\nThen Easton High's ice hockey team fell behind by two goals in the first period Friday night to Kent Island.\nThat combination was enough for Warrior head coach Tim Nowak to issue a warning to his team.\n\"I said, 'You better figure it out because next week when we play Severna Park we'll be down 10-0, it won't be two,\" Nowak said.\nEaston figured it out, and quickly, as Ethan Finlay scored a hat trick and older brother Ty Finlay had three assists and a goal — all in the second period — sparking the Warriors to a 10-4 victory at the Talbot County Community Center.\n\"I was like, 'Guys, you have to be ready,'\" Nowak said. \"You can't just show up and say, 'OK, we're playing hockey today.'\"\nKent Island, now 1-7 in the Maryland Student Hockey League Eastern Conference, came out with a new look in the first period and it paid dividends. Jacob Keever converted a pass from Jayden Calisto 4:37 into the game for a 1-0 Bucs lead. Kent Island continued to keep the puck in the Easton end for much of the first period, and widened its lead to 2-0 with just over 3 minutes left when Aidan Mahaffey set up Blake Vane's goal.\n\"We had a different strategy,\" Bucs head coach Doug Wackowski said. \"We were playing defense first and that was very successful through the first period and into the second. Unfortunately, kids get tired and Easton's a good team. They've got a full bench and things started going their way.\"\nBut before things started going Easton's way, Kent Island had impressed Nowak.\n\"I said to our guys, 'Look how they're playing,'\" Nowak said. \"'They're playing like structured. They're passing. You guys are trying to skate through everybody.' They (Kent Island) totally dominated us. And it wasn't just the fact they were up 2-0. They won every puck battle in the first period. I was like, 'Are we going to start doing something?'\"\nBlanked by Erick Long for almost 14 minutes, the Warriors finally tucked one past Kent Island's goalie with 1:08 left in the first period as David Rogers scored on Kaleb Fluharty's assist.\nThen the Finlays found their groove.\nThe Ty-to-Ethan connection produced the tying goal 5:06 into the second period. Just 50 seconds later they connected again, putting Easton in front to stay at 3-2. Long stopped everything directed his way over the next 8-plus minutes. But with 21 seconds left in the period Shane Gunshenan and Ty Finlay set up Ethan's third goal. Ty then punctuated Easton's four-goal period with an unassisted goal 7 seconds later for a 5-2 Warrior lead.\nEaston fattened its lead to 7-2 in the third as Fluharty took a Ty Finlay pass, steamed down right wing and snapped a wrist shot past Long. Just 26 seconds later Finlay scored his second goal — compliments of a Fluharty feed — against Nicole Seiler, who had taken over in net for Long.\nKent Island got back on the scoreboard with 9:29 remaining when Vane took an Abigail Mahaffey and James Biles passing combination and beat Easton goalie James Shaw (19 saves), starting his first game of the season.\nEaston again responded with a quick one-two punch. Paxton Echternach scored off a Ryan LaCorte pass 21 seconds after Vane's goal. Less than a minute later, Echternach set up Nick Walters' score for a 9-3 Easton lead.\nThe Warriors stretched their lead to 10-3 when Bradyn Speakman and Walters set up Carson Clow's goal. Vane then fed Aidan Mahaffey on a breakaway goal for the 10-4 final with under 3 minutes remaining.\nEaston, which has won three four, is 3-3 in conference play and sitting behind South River (6-1, Annapolis/Arundel/Chessie (6-1), Severna Park (7-1) and Broadneck (6-2) in the Eastern Conference. The top two teams in the conference will earn byes into the state tournament, while seeds 3 through 6 will meet in the playoffs in a bid to qualify for states.\n\"We're close,\" said Nowak, whose team lost by two goals to Annapolis/Arundel/Chessie and surrendered a goal in the final minute of an eventual 4-3 loss to Broadneck. \"We're so close to being a top echelon team. But the little things are keeping us from that.\"\nNotes: Long and Seiler combined for 25 saves. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 1,
    "day": 27,
