  {
    "title": "Buccaneers fall to Decatur, bounce back against Tech",
    "date": "2024-02-01",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Buccaneers fall to Decatur, bounce back against Tech\n\n February 1, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1052\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n STEVENSVILLE — The streak — which unofficially reached 61 consecutive victories against Bayside Conference opponents Wednesday night — is not the overwhelming driving force.No, what really pushes Stephen Decatur High's wrestling team is the idea it won't be good enough after graduating its state champions and finishers from the previous year.\n\"The kids know we haven't lost a Bayside match in awhile,\" Seahawks head coach Josh August said. \"But I feel like each year, we have like really good guys, state champs that graduated and go off to college, and then the guys that are left feel like they have something to prove. Because when you have a state champ, or two state champs leave, (people) think that you are not going to be as good. I think our guys use that to motivate themselves, keep on carrying on with the wins and making states.\"\nDecatur kept carrying on Wednesday night, routing non-conference foe Harford Vo-Tech, 61-9, before defeating Kent Island, 44-23, to extend its Bayside dominance while remaining undefeated heading Friday's showdown with unbeaten Rising Sun.\n\"I thought we wrestled really good against Harford Tech,\" said August, after the Seahawks improved to 13-0 overall, 10-0 Bayside. \"Some of our guys stepped up big, went up a couple weights for the Kent Island match. Not a lot of guys do that. We got guys wanting to wrestle big matches. Overall, I think the team is clicking pretty good.\"\nOverall, Kent Island head coach Tommy Sitar was happy with how his team battled against Decatur, which routed the Buccaneers, 60-18 at the Iron Duals in early January.\n\"We won only three matches against them,\" Sitar said of the Iron Horse dual against Decatur. \"(Tonight) we won six matches, doubled our output, which is nice.\"\nPerhaps the nicest part of the Decatur match for Kent Island was after losing the first three weight classes to Juan Hinojosa (113), Liam Hugues (120) and Reid Caimi (126) by pin to fall behind 18-0, the Bucs (10-3, 8-1) won the next four weights to pull within 18-14.\n\"They definitely looked strong,\" Sitar said of Silas Brobst (132), Greg Couch (138), Cody Sandefer (144) and Ashton Main (150). \"I was impressed with the way they were able to dictate to the other wrestlers what was going to happen.\"\nBrobst start the mini-rally when he went into overtime tied 1-1 with Aaron August then scored a takedown with 26 seconds left in the 1-minute overtime for a 3-1 sudden victory.\n\"Silas wrestled his best match definitely on his feet,\" Sitar said. \"The way he was moving and setting him up was awesome.\"\nKent Island's next three wrestlers would each score the first takedown in their respective matches. Couch scored a late takedown for a 9-1 major decision over Jake Saullo. Sandefer built a 10-0 lead over two periods en route to a 12-3 major over Alex Riehl. Main capped the run with a 10-3 decision over Braydon Bell at 150.\nDespite having its lead trimmed to four, Coach August was pleased with how his team wrestled through the middleweights.\n\"The score looked closer than the last time we went against Kent Island,\" Josh August said. \"They're a solid team every year. We came away with the victory. It was a little close in the middle, but they are strong right there. One of our things was our guys weren't getting pinned. Our guys that were outmatched were hanging in there, wrestling the full six minutes.\"\nThe Seahawks seized full control from there, packaging three pinfalls and two major decisions over the next five bouts for a 44-23 lead.\nDecatur's Amarian Manuel earned a 16-8 major over Brandon Adler at 157. Parker Intrieri pinned Chris Kougoures in 47 seconds at 165. Gavin Solito — wrestling up from 157 — put together a 9-1 major over Giuseppe Mellinger at 175. Kole Kohut (190) pinned Brian Potter in 1:49, and Nate McDaniel pinned Nick Morey with 28 seconds remaining in the 215-pound match.\n\"I think it's an expectation by them,\" Josh August said of his team's mindset. \"It's an expectation that they wrestle to the level to get there, not that they are just going to get there.\"\nKent Island came back to win the last two bouts, Greg Coffey winning a 4-2 decision over Price Hill at 285, and Gavin Lindner pinning Jaxon Archer in 3:08 at 106.\nThe 21-point difference was the closest any Bayside team has come to Decatur this season.\nThe Bucs fell behind 7-0 to Harford Vo Tech, but then regrouped to win 10 of the remaining 12 bouts, which included pinfalls from Couch (138), Adler (157), Kougoures (166), Potter (190), Morey (215), Coffey (285) and Lindner (106).\n\"I talked to the guys after the Decatur match 'cause it appeared we started at a weight (120) where had kind of a letdown,\" Sitar said. \"I let them know, 'We're peaking. We haven't peaked. Decatur was not the peak. We don't stop now just because we wrestled a team tough. We keep on peaking.\"\nWedged in the Bucs' rally was Sitar's decision to insert Potter, a 175-pound backup, in at 190 and bump Morey from 190 to 215.\n\"At the end of the match we had a situation where we were going to be (potentially) down 15 with five bouts left, or we could be down potentially 15 with three bouts left,\" Sitar said. \"So I opted with the strategy of going down 15 with three bouts, and bumped our 190 (Morey) to 215, where I was 100 percent sure he could get a pinfall. And our backup 175-pounder, Brian Potter ended up getting a pin over Jack Ryder of Harford Tech, who I guess was ranked in the state.\"\nBrobst won a 13-3 major decision at 132 and Sandefer got a 7-3 decision at 144 to help the Bucs take a 13-7 lead. Tech's Ryan Sewell pinned Main at 150 to tie the match at 13-all before Kent Island won the next seven straight matches, which included a 6-2 decision by Mellinger at 175.\n\"It's nice to immediately erase it from the forefront,\" Sitar said of coming back and beating Harford Tech after the Decatur loss.\nNotes: Kent Island's three losses this season have come against Decatur, which is bidding for a fifth consecutive state dual-meet title — excluding the COVID-19 canceled 2021 season — two-time defending 1A state dual-meet champ and unbeaten South Carroll, and Class 4A Broadneck (9-3). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 1,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
      "explanation": "The article reports on high school wrestling matches, scores, and team performance, which is clearly a sports news story."
    },
    "llm_classification_meta": {
      "model": "gpt-oss:120b",
      "llm_failed": false
    },
    "entities_people": "[\"Silas Brobst\"]",
    "metadata_sport": "wrestling",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
