  {
    "title": "Queen Anne's rallies, stuns Cambridge-SD",
    "date": "2024-02-01",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Queen Anne's rallies, stuns Cambridge-SD\n\n February 1, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 743\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CAMBRIDGE — It may not have been the best half of basketball Queen Anne's County High's boys team has played this season, but it was pretty close.Hollace Branch's three-point play with 1.5 seconds remaining Thursday night capped a 17-point comeback, as the Lions pulled out a 58-57 victory to stun unbeaten Cambridge-South Dorchester.\n\"Man, we played as a team,\" Queen Anne's head coach Jeff Hollis said as tears welled up in his eyes. \"They played hard. That's probably the hardest we've played all year.\"\nCambridge-SD (13-1 overall, 11-1 North Bayside) led 36-21 at halftime and bumped that lead to 17 early in the third quarter.\nQueen Anne's (12-4, 11-2), which was without leading scorer Vince Gilberto, went on a 14-4 run that was capped by Jadereon Thompson's layup with 3:09 left in the third. Cambridge-SD head coach Shawn Tucker called time, and the Vikings responded as Semay Pinder and LaShaun Moody (11 points) hit consecutive baskets, extending the lead to 44-35.\nThe Vikings maintained that nine-point edge at 48-39 heading into the fourth quarter. Cambridge-SD looked to be regaining control, as Moody dished to Koby Ennals for a basket, converted a steal into a layup, before Pinder scored a layup for a 54-41 lead with over 5 minutes remaining.\nBut the North Bayside leaders scored only three more points the rest of the game while Queen Anne's was piecing together a 17-point rally over the final 4:39, with seemingly someone different scoring every time down the floor.\nDJ Smith started the rally with a bucket. Nickai Gross made a free throw. Branch scored on a left-handed drive before Amari Baynard's layup pulled Queen Anne's within 54-48 with 3:36 to play.\nCambridge-SD's Pinder missed a pair of free throws.\nQueen Anne's kept closing.\nBaynard buried a 3-pointer with 2:10 left, drawing the Lions within 54-51. After Cambridge-SD was whistled for a moving screen, Jimmy Holthaus (game-high 14 points) scored, making it a one-point game at 54-53 with 1:34 left.\nAnthony Hughes (13 points) scored on a follow shot with 1:18 to go, nudging the Vikings' lead to 56-53. Moody converted one of two free throws with 1:06 left, making it 57-53.\nBut Smith drove down the right side of the lane for a basket with 41.8 seconds left, shaving the Vikings lead to 57-55. Baynard blocked a shot by Hughes, but Moody retrieved the ball and was fouled. But the senior guard missed both free throws with 15.8 seconds left.\nQueen Anne's got the ball back and worked it inside to Branch who scored the tying basket and was fouled. With 1.5 seconds left, the Lions senior made the free throw to complete the three-point play.\nCambridge-SD inbounded the ball, but could not get a shot off.\n\"Unfortunately, we're a terrible first-half team,\" Hollis said. \"The second half, they brought the energy and it made a difference and they started believing. You could tell.\nQueen Anne's defense forced multiple turnovers during a chaotic fourth quarter.\n\"We've been working on defense all year,\" Hollis said. \"And finally something must have finally hit today. I don't know. But yeah, this was definitely their best game on defense by far this year.\n\"We were telling them we were getting ready for the playoffs and every game means something, and they took it to heart today,\" Hollis continued. \"I'm really proud of these guys.\"\nWoolford finished with 13 points for Queen Anne's.\nEASTON 88 NORTH CAROLINE 50\nRIDGELY — Marshall Bailey scored 36 points as the Warriors remained in contention for the North Bayside title.\nZymier Ramirez had 16 points and Kyree Acree had 10 for the Warriors (12-4, 11-2).\nST. MICHAELS 56 KENT COUNTY 46\nST. MICHAELS — Nakhi Miller finished with 30 points as the Saints upended the Trojans.\nCOL. RICHARDSON 82, KENT ISLAND 61\nAMERICAN CORNER — Dreden Lake scored a team-high 22 points to help boost the Colonels.\nJazir Dickerson finished with 20 points and Makiah Batson hd 17 for Colonel Richardson.\nKam Green led Kent Island with 20 points and Robert Butler had 15.\nGIRLS BASKETBALL\nQUEEN ANNE'S 56, CAMBRIDGE-SD 34\nCAMBRIDGE — Freshman Laila Coles had 23 points, 10 steals and nine assists to pace the North Bayside-leading Lions. Bea Holthaus had eight points and 12 rebounds for Queen Anne's.\nNORTH CAROLINE 48 EASTON 3\nEASTON — Selaya Garrison finished with 22 points and Malaya Phillips added nine points to lead the Bulldogs. Jasiyah Henry had eight points.\nSisi Sama led Easton with 17 points.\nKENT ISLAND 61, COL. RICHARDSON 22\nSTEVENSVILLE — Sydney Riska recorded another double-double with 16 points and 12 rebounds as the Buccaneers rolled. Lilli Dauses scored 13 points for Kent Island. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
