  {
    "title": "TITLE WAVE",
    "date": "2024-02-03",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "TITLE WAVE\n\n February 3, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 832\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CAMBRIDGE — Bayside Conference swim teams be warned. Kent Island High may be preparing for an extended stay at the top.\"The kind of drought of young swimmers that some of the teams are experiencing. … like Easton, they lost a lot of seniors and really did not fill those spots,\" Kent Island head coach Corey Coulbourn said. \"We, knock on wood, we are fortunate enough this year not to experience that.\"\nThat influx of youth was on display at the Pauline F. and W. David Robbins Family YMCA Saturday, as Kent Island freshmen combined for five individual titles and were part of two winning relays, leading the Buccaneers to a sweep of the boys and girls titles during the Bayside swimming championships.\n\"We only had two senior girls swimming here today,\" Coulbourn said of Marissa White and Maddie Hull. \"They both earned us tons of points. We're also really fortunate to have a lot of young girls, including those freshmen who are coming to us from a club swimming background. So we've really been able to maintain the skills that they've already come to us with.\"\nKent Island's girls totaled 442 points, outdistancing runner-up Stephen Decatur by 54 points.\nLeading that effort was freshman Maddie Parks, who joined classmates Kara Seymour and Clare O'Keefe, and senior White to win the meet-opening 200-yard medley relay in 1:59.36. Parks went on to win the 100 freestyle (59.02) — edging White who placed second in 59.49 — and the 100 backstroke (1:05.46) by more than 2 seconds.\nBolstering the Bucs were Seymour, who won the 200 individual medley in 2:27.82 — almost 7 full seconds ahead of second-place Kendall Bagley of Decatur (2:34.70) — and O'Keefe, first in the 50 freestyle sprint (25.83).\nBut the Bucs' most impressive performance came in the 200 freestyle relay, when confusion as to who was swimming the lead leg had the team not even in the water until most of the competition was at the other end of the pool.\n\"It was just miscommunication,\" Coulbourn said. \"When we were brainstorming entries, I typed it up in a different order than what it got entered in the computer. We made some changes based on the events swimmers had right after the relay. So we just moved some people around. So what the girls had looked at on the wall was different from what (the) clerk of course was telling them.\"\nDespite that late start, Seymour, Parks, White and O'Keefe finished third (1:59.64) behind first-place Decatur (1:57.74) and second-place Queen Anne's (1:59.37).\n\"I'm super impressed that they made up that time to the degree that they did,\" Coulbourn said. \"One of our parents was watching the clock and said that they left about 15 seconds late. So to make up that. … we have an amazing group of freshmen, and three of them are on that relay.\"\nEaston, the two-time defending girls champion, finished third behind Maddie Weems, who won the 100 butterfly (1:03.53), the 100 breaststroke (1:09.62) before teaming with Isabella Westerfield, Katheryne Kelsey and Claire Morris to destroy the field in the 400 freestyle relay (3:59.20), winning by 15 seconds.\nThe Warriors also got a strong performance from Westerfield, who was second in the 50 free (26.11) and first in the 500 freestyle (5:47.64) — winning by 15 seconds. Westerfield anchored Easton's second-place 200 medley team, and gave the Warriors a lead they never surrendered in the 400 free relay. Mae Jennings' first-place effort in the 200 freestyle (1:59.20) bolstered the Warriors' point total.\nKent Island's boys also got a lift from their freshmen, as Jacob Reeder won the 200 IM (2:17.35), and joined classmate Jack Da Rosa, junior Giovanni Aguirre and senior John Mangold to win the 200 freestyle relay in 1:38.75.\n\"Similar situation with the boys,\" Coulbourn said in regard to Kent Island's youth. \"We only have one senior boy, our captain, John Mangold, who was on all three relays. And then we lucked out with our freshman crew this year. We have seven or eight freshman boys, and I think six or seven were here today. It's just amazing.\"\nKent Island won the boys' title with 358 points, with Decatur again taking second with 318.\nThe lone two-time winner in individual competition on the boys' side was Kent County's Henry Martinez, who won the 50 freestyle (23.08) and the 100 free (50.49).\nDecatur's Daniel Karcheuski was first in the 200 freestyle, but was disqualified, making Easton's Brady Tipton the champion with a time of 2:00.43. Karcheuski, who swam the second leg on the Seahawks' winning 200 medley relay, rebounded, winning the 100 breaststroke in a meet-record 1:03.05, before swimming a huge anchor leg to rally Decatur to victory in the 400 free relay (3:44.51).\nNorth Bayside swimmers brought home two other titles, as Queen Anne's County's William Meyer won the 100 butterfly (1:00.71) by 2 seconds, and Cambridge-South Dorchester's Hayden Hudson pulled away to win the 500 free (5:39.47) by 16 seconds.\nStephen Decatur's Chase Ponton won the 100 backstroke in 1:02.26.\nKent Island finished second in the boys and girls competitions last year. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
