  {
    "title": "SEEING DOUBLE",
    "date": "2024-02-07",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "SEEING DOUBLE\n\n February 7, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 851\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Katie Murphy has seen it all season.\"They tried to shut Morgan (Quade) down. They tried to shut Avery (McCall) down,\" Saints Peter and Paul High's girls head basketball coach said of Worcester Prep trying to clamp down on her top two leading scorers. \"We needed everybody to step up and Lucy did it.\"\nSophomore Lucy Rankin stepped up like never before Wednesday, notching a career-high 16 points to go with 10 rebounds for her first-ever double-double, as the Sabres romped to a 49-15 victory over Worcester Prep in an Eastern Shore Independent Athletic Conference clash on senior night.\n\"She really stepped up, 'cause when that stuff happens we need the other three (starters) to step up,\" Quade said of Rankin. \"She did really good with that tonight.\"\nRankin hit two free throws to give the Sabres their first points and a 2-2 tie, scored a layup midway through the first quarter to give Sts. Peter & Paul a lead it steadily widened, then made another foul shot to cap a 17-5 first quarter for the home team.\n\"Our coaches told us that this would happen,\" said Rankin of the Mallards trying to limit Quade and McCall. \"So they said I needed to step up and move the ball and drive to the basket more. Once I started to get more comfortable I loosened up a bit and started to just go with the flow. This is a surprise to me.\"\nRankin scored on a layup and a put-back as the Sabres outscored Worcester, 15-0, in the second quarter to take a commanding 32-5 lead into the locker room.\n\"She really came through,\" Murphy said of Rankin, who had nine first-half points. \"And everybody played the role they needed to play today.\"\nOne night after losing to ESIAC rival Delmarva Christian by 10, the Sabres cranked up the defensive pressure on Worcester early in the first quarter. Sts. Peter & Paul pressed Worcester full court, creating multiple turnovers while holding the Mallards scoreless the final 14 minutes of the first half.\n\"We've been fine tuning the press and changing different roles and different positions, and making sure that they're cutting off that middle person,\" Murphy said. \"They did it pretty well today. And they were jumping for balls and getting the steals.\"\nDespite Worcester's defensive efforts, Quade and McCall each finished in double figures. Quade, the Sabres' lone senior, finished with 14 points, eight rebounds and six steals. A sophomore, McCall scored all 12 of her points in the first half, and totaled four rebounds, three steals and three blocked shots.\nWorcester ended an 18-plus minute scoring drought with just under 4 minutes remaining in the third quarter, and matched the Sabres in scoring during a 7-7 third. But Sts. Peter & Paul continued to play solid half-court defense in the fourth, limiting the visitors to just one basket from the field — that coming with 4 minutes left when Sydney Mize hit a 3-pointer.\n\"I think today we moved the ball quicker,\" said Quade, comparing the Sabres' loss Tuesday to Delmarva Christian to Wednesday's rout. \"I think that's what kind of killed us last night. They were trapping us and we weren't moving it fast enough to get it around their traps, and if we did we would have found the open person really easily. Today I think we definitely moved it better. And honestly with them taking me and Avery out it kind of opened it up for the other three (Rankin, Sam Murphy and Annie Albright).\"\nWith another potential showdown with Delmarva Christian in the playoffs looming, Coach Murphy was pleased with how her team rebounded from Tuesday's loss.\n\"Our defense is doing very well right now,\" Murphy said. \"Every ball is a Sabre ball. They are supposed to go after it. I'm very happy with our underclassmen because they took the lead from our starters and they were after every ball, causing a bunch of jump balls. It's probably the most jump balls we've had in a game so far. Boxing out, rebounding, looking for the open player, making sure everybody was covered, they also did very well.\"\nKENT ISLAND 39 STEPHEN DECATUR 33\nBERLIN — Sydney Riska had 13 points and 15 rebounds Wednesday, leading the Buccaneers past the Seahawks.\nMakayla Kropfelder added 10 points for Kent Island.\nBOYS BASKETBALL\nSSPP 72 WORCESTER PREP — Robbie Cunningham had 17 points and JJ Rardin 16 Wednesday, as the Sabres rolled past the Mallards.\nOn Tuesday, Braydan Smith drilled eight 3-pointers for a 24-point effort, leading Saints Peter & Paul to a 62-53 victory over Key School in a Maryland Interscholastic Athletic Association C Conference game. Garett Hemingway finished with 18.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 65 NORTH DORCHESTER 41\nCENTREVILLE — Vince Gilberto scored 16 points to eclipse the 1,000-point mark for his career Tuesday while pacing the Lions past the Eagles.\nJimmy Holthaus had 11 for Queen Anne's County.\nCOLONEL 80 KENT COUNTY 70\nWORTON — Dreden Lake scored 29 points Tuesday, leading the Colonels.\nJazir Dickerson had 22 points and Makiah Batson finished with 20 for Colonel Richardson.\nJysir Valentine led the Trojans with 20 points. Larry Steward had 14 for Kent County, and Dwah Harris and Jonah Somerville each had 13. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
