  {
    "title": "Vikings win title after scare",
    "date": "2024-02-10",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Vikings win title after scare\n\n February 10, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 934\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n LANDOVER — Cambridge-South Dorchester High's boys indoor track and field team was on its way to a third consecutive Class 1A East Region championship Friday night.Then confusion stepped in.\nBoys head coach Clint Falduto and assistant Lois Narr couldn't figure out how the Vikings' lead over Largo had suddenly evaporated to almost nothing after the second-to-last race on the boys 1A schedule — the 800 meters.\n\"Lois was like, 'We should be up by so much further.' And I was like, 'I know. I don't know what happened,'\" Falduto said.\nAs Falduto and Narr continued trying to understand how their comfortable lead had suddenly gone poof, the boys 800 for the Class 4A East meet — which was being held simultaneously with the 1A meet at the Prince George's Sports and Learning Complex — was being run.\nThat's when Cambridge-SD's Tekai Drummond approached Falduto and Narr with the answer.\n\"Tekai came up and said, 'That kid that just beat me, he's in 4A,'\" Falduto said. \"And we're like, 'What?'\"\nFalduto and Narr went to the officials' table where it was confirmed a 4A runner — wearing the same hip number as a Largo runner — had run the wrong race, and that his points had initially been counted toward Largo's point total. As officials corrected the mistake, Cambridge-SD was getting ready to run its heat of the boys 4x400 relay.\nThough Largo won the 4x400, Antonio Blueford, Tori Willis, Tekai Drummond and Teshar Drummond's runner-up finish in the event capped a third regional title for the Vikings, who totaled 91 points to the Lions' 84.\n\"They were beating us at one point,\" Falduto said of Largo. \"My head was spinning. Oh my goodness, it was exciting. So, so exciting. Freaking bizarre.\n\"The boys have worked so tremendously hard,\" Falduto continued. \"This group has been together for a good solid three years, the majority of them. And knowing the competition around the state, they knew it was possible. But they knew it was going to be a lot of hard work.\"\nCambridge-SD's bid for a third straight 1A East crown became even harder because a number of key team members were unable to compete, including Jayden Smith, who was sidelined by an illness.\nDespite those losses, the Vikings scored in 10 of the 11 events.\nLeading that effort was Willis, who won the 55-meter hurdles in a personal-record 7.87 seconds, placed second in the 55-meter sprint (6.55), and ran a leg on the second-place 4x800 relay, joining Christian Dockins and the Drummonds for a time of 8:54.92.\n\"Fifty-five-meter hurdles, I really think it was just push and get out, and tried to keep my pace and keep my energy going through the hurdles,\" said Willis, who took the lead after clearing the second hurdle. \"I just wanted to make sure I qualified and made it to states again. Once I get a good start, I know I'm going to push through the hurdles and get the win.\"\nEdwind Torres of Chesapeake Science Point was second in the hurdles with an 8.11, followed by Cambridge-SD's Blueford in third (8.28).\n\"Fifty-five, there's room to improve,\" said Willis, who clocked a 6.55, finishing second to Friendly's Donte Long (6.48). Kent County's Tyrell Fennell was third in 6.58.\nIn addition to their second-places in the 4x800 and 4x400 relays, Cambridge-SD was also second in the 4x200, as Teshar Drummond, Kay'lon Marine, William Jackson and Jakuez combined for a 1:34.55.\nThe Vikings were second to Largo in all three relays.\nFourth in the controversial 800, Tekai Drummond, who was running sprints a year ago, finished second in the 1,600 with a 4:49.61.\nBolstering the Vikings' fortunes were the second- and third-place finishes by Jackson (11-5) and Preston Adkins (8-11) in the pole vault.\nCambridge-SD swept the hurdles titles, as Enazajah Young broke her own school record to win the girls title in 8.28, beating Largo's Ayniah Fersner (8.56) and Lailah Fersner (8.65).\n\"By the third hurdle,\" said Young, when asked when she thought she had the hurdles won. \"When you race you can hear people's footsteps. I didn't hear nothing. I was like, 'You got to push through the rest.'\"\nThe defending state champion in the 55 meters, Young got off to a slow start in the sprint after a teammate had been disqualified for a false start. Young finished fourth in 7.34, but qualified for the state championships on Feb. 20.\n\"She watched her teammate false start right before,\" Cambridge-SD girls head coach Stacey Brown said of Young in the 55. \"It's nerve racking when you're out there. You false start one time and you miss states. I think that goes through a lot of their heads when they're out there. They want to come out of those blocks fast, but they know one false start and they're done.\"\nYoung finished sixth in the 300 with a 44.81.\nThe only other North Bayside athlete to earn a first place in the girls competition was Kent County's Ke'yare Hawkins, who won the shot put with a heave of 35 feet, 3¼ inches.\nThe Trojans earned a second regional champion, as Larry Steward, competing in just his third competition of the season, won the boys high jump, clearing the bar at 6-2.\n\"I was pretty confident coming in because I know I can jump high,\" Steward said. \"I'm not so confident in my technique in jumping. I surprised myself with 6-2. I tried to get 6-5, but I plan on getting that at states.\"\nColonel Richardson's Jackson Jean was second in the high jump at 5-10, followed by Kent County's Rah'zir Wilson in fourth (5-10).\nColonel Richardson also had a runner-up finish in the girls high jump, with Addison Chandler clearing 4-6. Mardela's Lexie Guy won with a 4-10. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
