  {
    "title": "EASTON-CR",
    "date": "2024-02-13",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "EASTON-CR\n\n February 13, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 863\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n AMERICAN CORNER — Any residue from Saturday night's loss to James M. Bennett at the Peake Challenge was gone.And Easton High's boys basketball team made sure this wasn't going to be a bump in the road as it tried to re-establish its momentum with the postseason approaching.\nThe Warriors buried five 3-pointers in the first quarter and sophomore guard Marshall Bailey scored a game-high 22 points Tuesday night, pacing Easton to a 73-65 victory over Colonel Richardson.\n\"We want to establish like a mindset and try to come out and punch 'em in the mouth first and see what happens, and then adjust after that,\" Easton head coach Dionte Hynson said after the Warriors improved to 14-5 overall, 13-2 North Bayside. \"I think we came out and played real well defensively, got up and down a little bit, and that got our offense flowing a little bit.\"\nSo did the three-ball.\nJa'Kai Brooks (18 points) drilled the first of his two 3-pointers in the opening minute and Easton never trailed. Brooks popped another trey midway through the first quarter for an 8-2 Easton lead. Bailey, who had three 3-pointers, canned a 3 for an 11-4 lead. Diontaye Copper (13 points) drained one of his three 3-pointers with 1:40 left in the first period before Bailey connected on Easton's fifth of the quarter for a 21-10 lead after one period.\n\"Our guards were shooting the ball real well,\" Hynson said.\nThe Warriors extended their lead in the second quarter behind strong inside play from Tymire Thomas and Kahlil Batson, who each took entry passes and made quick spin moves inside the paint for buckets and a 40-24 halftime cushion.\n\"The halfcourt offense was really flowing I thought,\" Hynson said. \"We hit a couple high-lows too. We made some really nice passes. Our bigs, they were hitting the high-lows. Even Tymire came off the bench with his high-low.\"\nThree-pointers from Makiah Batson (12 points) and Manny Wallace (13 points) helped Colonel Richardson (11-8, 7-8) trim its 16-point halftime deficit to 10 with 3:46 remaining in the third quarter.\nBut Easton outscored the Colonels, 14-5, over the final 3-plus minutes of the third, including a 6-point frenzy in the last 30 seconds. Bailey turned a steal into a layup and was fouled. Though Bailey missed the free throw, Zymier Ramirez grabbed the rebound and scored on a put-back. Bailey then made a steal on the inbounds pass and laid the ball in for a 59-40 lead heading into the fourth quarter.\n\"We just lost our focus there a little at the end of the quarter,\" Colonels head coach Brad Plutschak said. \"We just couldn't get right. I was hoping if we could get it inside 10 we could see what could happen. But we had given up some pretty open looks there in the first quarter. We were chasing points all night at that's tough.\"\nColonel creeped back within 15, but back-to-back 3-pointers from Copper gave the Warriors a 65-47 lead with just under 6 minutes left in the game.\nUp 19, Hynson pulled most of his starting lineup with a little over 2 minutes remaining. Colonel scored the game's final 10 points to make the final more respectable.\n\"We played a good team on Saturday,\" Hynson said of Bennett. \"We didn't play that bad but we got some stuff straightened out. I thought we bounced back real nice. I told them this is the last week (of the regular season) and we've got to get ready for the playoffs. I think we're headed in the right direction.\"\nEaston is scheduled to host North Bayside-leading Cambridge-South Dorchester (19-1, 15-1) on Thursday.\nNotes: Jazir Dickerson had a team-high 15 points for Colonel Richardson, which had its four-game win streak snapped.\nCAMBRIDGE-SD 115 ST. MICHAELS 49\nCAMBRIDGE — LaShaun Moody scored a season-high 41 points and Sterling Perkins had 26 to power the Vikings to their second victory over the Saints in as many nights Tuesday.\nJaden Harris had 16 for Cambridge-SD and Thomas Wilson 10.\nNakhi Miller led St. Michaels with 30 points.\nPerkins had 28 points in Monday's 87-51 win at St. Michaels. Anthony Hughes and Semaj Pinder had 15 points apiece and Wilson contributed 13 points.\nJohnny Mautz led the Saints with 19.\nSSPP 71, PARK 52\nBALTIMORE — Garett Hemingway scored 28 points and Brayden Smith had 12 Tuesday, as Saints Peter and Paul (13-8, 6-3) rolled in a Maryland Interscholastic Athletic Association C Conference game.\nRobbie Cunningham finished with 10.\nHemingway had 33 points and Smith 13 in Monday's 69-60 victory over Delmarva Christian.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 81 NORTH CAROLINE 65\nRIDGELY — Senior Vince Gilberto had a team-high 27 points and the Lions raised their record to 15-4, 13-2.\nCollin Woolford had 18 points and Jimmy Holthaus scored 10.\nGIRLS BASKETBALL\nKENT ISLAND 54 SOUTHERN 50\nSTEVENSVILLE — On Monday, Makayla Kropfelder scored a game-high 18 points as the Bucs topped Southern-Anne Arundel. Lilli Dauses had 17 points and Lacey Dauses had 10. Riska missed a double-double by one point, finishing with nine points and 14 rebounds.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 49 NORTH CAROLINE 37\nCENTREVILLE — Freshman Laila Coles had 16 points, eight assists and three steals as the Lions improved to 18-1, 15-0.\nFreshman Bea Holthaus had 12 points and 12 boards for Queen Anne's and RJ Ensor added eight points, 10 rebounds and three steals. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 13,
