  {
    "title": "Defense fuels Cambridge-SD past Warriors",
    "date": "2024-02-15",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Defense fuels Cambridge-SD past Warriors\n\n February 15, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 739\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Cambridge-South Dorchester High's boys basketball team arrived in Easton Thursday night having already won the North Bayside.The way the Vikings played, you would have thought the division title was still on the line.\nSophomore Semaj Pinder scored a team-high 19 points and four others finished in double figures, as Cambridge-SD, behind an overwhelming defense, rolled to a 79-60 victory over the Warriors.\nThe Vikings (19-1 overall, 15-1 North Bayside) now prepare to play Stephen Decatur for the Bayside Conference championship next Tuesday at the Wicomico County Civic Center in Salisbury.\n\"Old fashion butt kicking,\" Easton head coach Dionte Hynson said after the Warriors' second most lopsided loss of the season. \"Congratulations to them. They were the better team tonight. I thought they brought a whole lot of energy. We didn't match their energy tonight. First quarter, came out, played well. And then after that.\"\nEaston (14-6, 13-3) led 12-5 with a little over 3 minutes remaining in the first quarter. Cambridge-SD sophomore Sterling Perkins (17 points) converted a steal into a layup. Darren Belizare turned another steal into a layup. Pinder then buried a 3-pointer to forge a 12-12 tie heading into the second quarter.\nCambridge-SD continued to hound the Warriors defensively and took control of the game, outscoring Easton, 21-11 in the second quarter.\n\"We got a chip on our shoulder,\" said Cambridge-SD head coach Shawn Tucker, who was minus starting point guard LaShaun Moody. \"We're the underdogs we feel like every game we're going in. There's so much negativity that goes with these C-SD boys. And they just fight and fight. They're resilient. And they represent me. I'm wild. I'm passionate. I'm full of energy.\"\nSo were the Vikings.\nEaston shaved a seven-point deficit to 25-21 when sophomore guard Marshall Bailey (18 points) hit a pair of free throws with 2:36 left in the second quarter. But Perkins and junior Koby Ennals (12 points) drained back-to-back 3-pointers before a Kamarion Hall's bucket capped an 8-0 run, that helped the Vikings to a 33-23 halftime lead.\n\"I give all props to my assistant coaches, also my defensive coach, Ryan Waters,\" Tucker said. \"He has those guys locked in on defense. And we've been pressuring teams for the last seven games. We're just relentless on that.\"\nAfter letting a 17-point third-quarter lead get away in a 58-57 loss to Queen Anne's on Feb. 1, Cambridge-SD has reeled off seven straight wins.\nEaston scored the last five points of the third quarter to pull within 50-41 at the end of the third quarter. But the Warriors wouldn't get any closer, as the Vikings outscored their longtime rival, 29-19, in the fourth quarter.\n\"They were shooting the heck out of the ball tonight,\" Hynson said of the Vikings. \"Their zero (Perkins) and their number 12 (Pinder), they were shooting the ball real well.\n\"Our defense was horrible,\" Hynson added. \"We were just sitting there watching their shooters. And we were giving up too many straight line drives to the basket. We'll learn from it. Like I told them, it's over and done with. Just got to move on and get ready for playoffs.\"\nJa'Kai Brooks led Easton with 19 points.\nJaden Harris had 12 points for Cambridge-SD and Anthony Hughes added 10.\n\"We got four goals and we just accomplished one of them,\" said Tucker of winning the North Bayside.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 76 KENT ISLAND 73\nCENTREVILLE — Vince Gilberto scored 19 points and Collin Woolford went over the 1,000-point mark for his career as the Lions outlasted their county rival in overtime.\nAmari Baynard had 15 points for Queen Anne's (16-4, 14-2) and Jadereon Thompson had 13.\nSSPP 56, JEMICY 41\nOWINGS MILLS — Brayden Smith had 18 points and Aaron Ewing netted 10 as Saints Peter and Paul improved to 14-8 overall, 7-3 in the Maryland Interscholastic Athletic Association C Conference.\nCOL. RICHARDSON 93 NORTH CAROLINE 75\nAMERICAN CORNER — Dreden Lake scored 23 points and Jazir Dickerson added 20 to pace the Colonels.\nMakiah Batson had 15 points and Manny Wallace 12 for Colonel Richardson.\nNorth Caroline's Nasai Bell led all scorers with 24 points. Jarvis Thomas had 17 and Tyson Weber and Quinell Gross each had 12.\nGIRLS BASKETBALL\nQUEEN ANNE'S 49 KENT ISLAND 33\nCENTREVILLE — Kariana Hickson scored 18 points as the Lions clinched the North Bayside title.\nFreshmen Laila Coles had 13 points for Queen Anne's (19-1, 16-0) and Bea Holthaus had 11.\nNORTH CAROLINE 58 COL. RICHARDSON 28\nRIDGELY — Ashleigh Lawry scored 18 points and Selaya Garrison finished with 16 to power the Bulldogs (13-6). Malayah Phillips finished with 12. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
