  {
    "title": "HOME RULE",
    "date": "2024-02-20",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "HOME RULE\n\n February 20, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Home Rule \n \n 1088\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n BERLIN — Now that a fifth consecutive state dual-meet championship has been tucked away, Stephen Decatur High head wrestling coach Josh August said his team can focus on individual competition at the regional and state levels, though the approach is slightly different.\"There are certain things that you would do,\" August said, comparing dual-meet to individual competition. \"If you're losing, you don't give up the bonus, or you don't get pinned in a dual meet because that score matters. But out here, like if you're down, sometimes you got to let it fly. You might not get the takedown but you went for it. The mentality is a little different, but overall just go out, wrestle tough, get to the positions you prefer. Most of the time things work out pretty good for you.\"\nThings again worked out pretty good for the Seahawks Monday, as they placed nine in the finals and left with seven individual titles on the way to winning their fifth straight Bayside Conference championship in their home gym.\nDecatur totaled 269½ points to easily outdistance runner-up Parkside by 86½ points. Kent Island was third with 179½ points, followed by North Caroline (fourth, 166), Colonel Richardson (fifth, 146½) and Queen Anne's County (sixth, 123).\n\"They are just a really tight team,\" August said. \"The momentum from state duals definitely kind of bleeds over into this.\"\nDecatur reached the finals in the first five weight classes, with Juan Hinojosa (33-3) leading off with a fall over Kent Island freshman Gavin Lindner (27-10) in 1 minute, 47 seconds in the 106-pound title match. Elijah Collick (35-2) followed with a 16-4 major decision over Queen Anne's freshman Alan McDonald (30-7) in the 113 final. Liam Hugues (14-5) made it three straight titles for the Seahawks with a 7-1 decision over Wicomico's Tavenon Outlaw (29-7) at 120.\nDecatur's Reid Caimi (32-2) advanced to the 126-pound final but forfeited to Queen Anne's senior Tremaine Jackson (32-3) due to injury.\nThe Seahawks' win streak ended at 132, where Kent Island's Greg Couch (27-7) scored takedowns in each period for a 7-0 victory over Decatur's Aaron August (29-5).\n\"He's been wrestling 38 for most of this year,\" Kent Island head coach Tommy Sitar said of Couch. \"He came down and had a great day.\"\nAmarian Manuel (34-8) gave Decatur its fourth individual crown with a 9-2 decision over Parkside's Xavier Moorman (19-3) at 150. Teammate Gavin Solito (39-1) built a 7-1 lead before pinning Kent Island's Brandon Adler (22-12) in the 157-pound final. Senior Parker Intrieri (28-2) completed the Seahawks' second three-match win streak of the finals, earning a 14-1 major decision over Colonel Richardson sophomore Derrick Mitchell (32-7) at 165.\nTwo bouts later, Decatur senior Kole Kohut (32-6) pieced together a 12-4 major decision over Kent Island's Nick Morey (32-5) to win the 190-pound weight class.\nThe only other teams with multiple winners were Kent Island and Parkside.\nThe Bucs got their second crown from senior Giuseppe Mellinger (33-5), who after surrendering an escape to Parkside's Canaan Mapp (22-7), strung together a second-period takedown, with an escape and another takedown in the third period en route to a 7-2 decision at 175.\n\"Giuseppe, another awesome tournament,\" said Sitar, who had six in the finals, including Cody Sandefer (29-9), the runner-up at 144. \"We were kind of hoping we'd get seven in there today, but I'm pretty happy with the effort. Those guys that didn't make it for the most part wrestled back pretty well. I'm proud of our team score.\"\nParkside got pinfalls from Shaheed Armstrong (33-6) and Ali'i Turner (24-5) to win the 215- and 285-pound titles, respectively, and nudge past Kent Island for second place in the team standings.\nKent County sophomore Scottie Ford (34-1) was the fourth wrestler from a North Bayside school to win a title, as he crafted a 6-0 decision over Parkside's Rocco Tully (33-8) to win the 138-pound weight class.\nNorth Caroline's fourth-place finish was bolstered by eight top-four finishes, including four winners in the consolation finals — Darly Ortega (106), Edward Darling (157), Tommy Morris (215) and Daketa Kinsey (285).\nSophomore Chatydrick Pickrom (23-13) gave Colonel Richardson a second finalist in the boys' competition as he reached the 285-pound final, where he lost to Turner.\n\"I would say it's definitely up there,\" Colonel Richardson head coach Bryan Hall said of the Colonel's fifth-place finish possibly being its best in conference tournament history for his program. \"They did very well. We only had one guy that didn't place in the top seven.\"\nThe Colonels also finished with four champions in the girls division. Dale Swann (16-7) pinned Decatur's Melanie Ruiz (5-8) in 1:16 to win the 105-pound crown. Freshman Olivia McGregor (15-1) pinned Queen Anne's Lilly Hammond (14-5) in 3:26 to win the 145-pound division. Teammate Hannah Kefauver (9-7) almost duplicated that effort, pinning Decatur's Amari Purnell (9-4) in 3:25 of the 155-pound final.\n\"They wrestled well,\" Hall said of his girls. \"We kind of expected Olivia McGregor to win. She only lost one match on the season. The finals match she wrestled very tough. She was down early and was able to come back and pin the girl.\n\"Hannah, she really showed up,\" Hall continued. \"She showed a lot of grit, wrestled hard, beat two tough girls. And Dale of course, I think she beat everyone in the weight class, so she beat them all again.\"\nRyleigh Porter (7-3) was Colonel's fourth champ, but the only other wrestler in the 235-pound weight class dropped out of the tournament. Colonel's Luna Infante-Reyes (110), Landine Dorleans (125) and Danika Jeanty (170) each placed second.\nLilliana Mellinger (8-1) gave Kent Island its lone girls champion when she earned a 10-5 victory over Queen Anne's Ava Price (15-6) in the 120-pound final.\nQueen Anne's finished with three conference champions, including Ally Conley (17-5) — the 2022 2A-1A state champion — who pinned Parkside's Logan Newton (7-4) in 30 seconds to win the 100-pound title.\nThe Lions' other two finals wins came against teammates. Keira Corcoran (4-3) earned a 3-2 decision over Linda Vail (7-9) to win the 135-pound weight class, and Julia Reburn (12-6) pinned Emma Coppage (2-8) in the 140-pound final.\n\"Overall I would say it was a great day for girls wrestling in the Bayside, finally getting the tournament going,\" said Queen Anne's boys head coach David Stricker, who has played a key role in the growth of girls wrestling in the state. \"Team-wise we did pretty well. I think we kind of underachieved a little bit. We had a rough semifinals and finals. It was sort of a reality check for some of our girls going into regionals Friday.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 20,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
