  {
    "title": "BACK ON TOP",
    "date": "2024-02-21",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "BACK ON TOP\n\n February 21, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1013\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n SALISBURY —Many in the crowd that jammed into the Wicomico Youth & Civic Center for Tuesday night's Bayside Conference boys basketball championship between Stephen Decatur and Cambridge-South Dorchester High figured the game was a mere formality in the Seahawks' coronation as kings of the hardwood.Uh, the Vikings were not part of that crowd.\n\"They had Decatur beating us by 25,\" Cambridge-SD junior forward Koby Ennals said of Bayside forecasters.\n\"We were 25-point underdogs,\" Vikings head coach Shawn Tucker said. \"But that's why you play.\"\nCrashing the boards like every rebound carried a prize, Cambridge-SD played with a fever-like intensity from baseline to baseline en route a 70-57 victory that gave the Vikings their first outright conference title since 1996.\n\"We just didn't match their intensity,\" Decatur head coach B.J. Johnson said of the Vikings. \"Congratulations to Coach Tucker and Cambridge. They just wanted it more than we did in my honest opinion.\"\nThough Cambridge-SD shared the 2003 conference crown with Pocomoke, it had lost in its last seven trips to the Bayside final.\nMany figured that string would be extended as the Class 3A Seahawks came in riding an 11-game win streak.\n\"Nobody in this gym but a couple of people believed that we were going to win this game. And they were probably all from up North,\" Tucker said after the Vikings improved to 20-1.\nCambridge-SD's fan base may have had its faith shaken early as Decatur (19-2) opened an 8-0 lead less than four minutes into the game, and carried a 16-10 lead into the second quarter.\nLed by guard Anthony Hughes, Cambridge-SD started hitting the boards harder and attacking the offensive rim more in the second quarter.\n\"Anthony Hughes played his butt off,\" Tucker said. \"For a 6-(foot)-1 guard, he rebounded like he was 6-foot-7. This kid just gave us everything. And that's what we've been preaching.\"\nBaskets by Ennals (game-high 21 points) and senior guard LaShaun Moody (18 points), and two free throws from Sterling Perkins (12) pulled the Vikings even at 16-all less than two minutes into the second quarter.\nDavin Chandler (15 points) put the Seahawks back in front. But Hughes (nine points) scored on a double-pump shot to tie the game, then watched Moody make a three-point play for the Vikings' first lead at 21-18.\nA three-point play from Brycen Coleman (team-high 18 points) drew Decatur even at 21-21. The mercurial Moody put Cambridge-SD back in front at 23-21. But Chandler and Brogan Eastlack hit back-to-back 3-pointers for the Seahawks. Chandler then scored on a drive that stoked Decatur's fans and moved the South Bayside champs to a 29-23 lead with 1:24 left in the first half.\nThe Vikings seemed unfazed. Perkins hit a 3-pointer, was fouled, and hit the free throw to complete a four-point play with 46.5 seconds left. Cambridge-SD then got what Tucker considered a game-changing 3-pointer from Thomas Wilson with 19 seconds left, giving the Vikings a 30-29 halftime lead.\n\"The 3-pointer that Thomas Wilson hit in the first half. We went up 30-29 and I saw Decatur's body language, like 'Another guy we've got to account for?'\" Tucker said. \"That's when I knew.\"\nHughes also had a feeling.\n\"We held them to 29 points at the half,\" Hughes said. \"Coach told us if we keep them at 29 like that we're going to win the game. And that's what happened.\"\nTrybe Wise's free throw seven seconds into the second half forged the game's fourth and final tie at 30-all. Twenty-five seconds later, Darren Belizare scored inside, giving Cambridge-SD a lead it never relinquished the rest of the game. Ennals followed with a layup. Hughes scored inside for a 36-30 Cambridge-SD lead.\nDecatur called time and got a layup from Coleman out of the break. But Perkins splashed a 3-pointer and Hughes muscled inside for a score and a 41-32 lead that prompted another Decatur timeout.\n\"We came in with a great mindset tonight, thinking that we were going to win,\" Hughes said. \"We knew we had to crash (the boards) and get the rebounds tonight because they were taller. We had to box out, play together, play relentless.\"\nCambridge-SD continued to do all three.\nA 7-2 run got Decatur within four at 43-39 with under two minutes to go in the third. But the Vikings answered the last basket of that run, and the Seahawks' other two field goals of the period, to take a 49-43 lead into the fourth.\n\"Coming into this game everybody said their size was going to be the problem, but in all actuality it wasn't,\" said Moody, who buried a 3-pointer 1:24 into the final period for a 52-43 Cambridge-SD lead. \"We were equally matched up. We boxed out hard. Anthony Hughes got us great rebounds in the stretch. I think that's where we won this game with the rebounds. It didn't come from offense. It came from defense and getting our rebounds.\nThe Vikings continued to use that combination to their advantage over the final six minutes, forcing turnovers, pounding the glass on both ends, and often limiting the Seahawks to just one shot a possession.\n\"That's our calling card,\" Tucker said of his defense. \"We just turn people over. Sometimes our offense can get a little stagnant, but we turn people over. That's our offense.\"\nDecatur closed within 55-50 on a Chandler bucket with 3:47 left. Ennals' 3-pointer and a basket by Belizare pushed Cambridge-SD's lead back to 10 at 60-50 with just over three minutes left. Back-to-back baskets by Careen Bolden (10 points) again narrowed the Viking lead to six. But Ennals scored off a Perkins miss and Moody hit two free throws to make it 64-54.\nWise hit a jumper to get the Seahawks within 64-57 with 1:13 left, but the Vikings cemented the win, hitting six of eight free-throw attempts over the final 1:10.\n\"We've been preaching the last couple of days, and it's been in our scouting report: match their physicality,\" Tucker said. \"And that's what we did tonight. We matched their physicality. Those guys, they're resilient. We had a couple of guys go down that play a lot of minutes, but they still found a way to win this game. I just love those guys' resiliency.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 21,
