  {
    "title": "Late surge lifts Wi-Hi past Lions",
    "date": "2024-02-21",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Late surge lifts Wi-Hi past Lions\n\n February 21, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 652\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n SALISBURY — It had been 12 days since its undefeated season was stopped cold via a 40-point shellacking from unbeaten Wicomico.Now, just 11 seconds into the fourth quarter of a seesaw Bayside Conference championship, Queen Anne's County High's girls basketball team was holding a one-point lead over that same Wi-Hi bunch Tuesday night. But the Lions' hopes for a second conference crown in three years virtually evaporated over the next four minutes, as the Indians went on a 13-0 run that proved fuel enough for a 58-52 victory at the Wicomico Youth & Civic Center.\n\"It was a great game,\" Queen Anne's head coach Mike Kern said after Wicomico had earned its first conference championship since 1985.\nRouted 81-41 by Wi-Hi on Feb. 8, Queen Anne's (19-2) took a quick 5-0 lead Tuesday, prompting Indians head coach Willie Downing to burn a timeout with only 28 seconds elapsed. The Lions never trailed in the first half, and carried a 25-23 lead into halftime.\nThe game became a back-and-forth contest in the third period, with five lead changes and two ties. A pair of Taylor Jordan free throws pulled Queen Anne's even at 36-36 with 1:08 remaining in the third. But Wicomico sophomore guard Se'Lah Foreman (game-high 26 points) hit a 3-pointer just 12 seconds later for a 39-36 Indians lead.\nA transition layup from freshman guard Kariana Hickson (team-high 20 points) with 12 seconds left in the third quarter, and freshman Laila Coles' drive 11 seconds into the fourth nudged Queen Anne's to a 40-39 lead.\nBut Foreman and Wi-Hi (21-0) took over from there.\nIn one of the few defensive lapses of the game, Foreman was left alone and canned a 3-pointer with 7:37 left in the game for a 42-40 Wicomico lead. A Lions turnover led to Carlisa Johnson scoring inside. Foreman converted a steal into a layup. Gabrielle Corbin (10 points) then scored on a put-back, giving the Indians a 48-40 lead, drawing a Queen Anne's timeout.\nWi-Hi wasn't quite through.\nAnother Lions turnover led to an inside bucket from Sariah Sanon. Foreman then got behind the Queen Anne's defense for an easy breakaway layup and a 52-40 lead with a little over four minutes to play.\n\"What really hurt us is the fourth quarter, leaving Foreman open for that wide-open three, and then she got a couple breakaways,\" Kern said. \"We weren't marked up well and had some mental lapses there, and they got some easy baskets out of it. That turned a one-point (Queen Anne's) lead into a (12)-point (Wi-Hi) lead and it was hard to fight back.\"\nJordan's basket with 3:44 left ended a four-plus minute Lions' scoring drought, but the Indians answered with baskets from Jecolia Traore (eight points) and Johnson (eight points) for a 56-42 cushion with under three minutes remaining.\n\"The big thing we talked about at halftime was stop hesitating, because it was like they were overthinking what we were doing,\" Downing said. \"Once they did that part and just started relaxing, the defense picked it up. Once they got active on defense, they didn't have time to think about anything else.\"\nQueen Anne's consistently drove to the basket, but often had shots rejected by Sanon or Corbin, who together accounted for the bulk of Wi-Hi's 15 blocked shots.\n\"We were hoping to get them in foul trouble and we ended up in foul trouble,\" said Kern, who watched Coles draw her fourth foul early in the third quarter and RJ Ensor foul out midway through the fourth. \"That hurt us a little bit.\"\nThe Lions were also hurt at the free-throw line, where they converted 14 of 25 attempts.\n\"Foul shooting we struggled,\" Kern said. \"If we had shot better it would have been a different score.\"\nQueen Anne's did manage to outscore the Indians, 10-2, over the final 2:48, with freshman Bea Holthaus hitting a free throwing to put the finishing touches on a 15-point, 10-rebound performance.\n\"I was proud we did fight back,\" Kern said. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
