  {
    "title": "Bucs' Tucker Claxton looks to 3rd straight title",
    "date": "2024-02-24",
    "author": "LUKE BONANNI Special to the Bay Times Record Observer",
    "content": "Bucs' Tucker Claxton looks to 3rd straight title\n\n February 24, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: LUKE BONANNI Special to the Bay Times Record Observer | Section: News \n \n 445\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n STEVENSVILLE — Senior Tucker Claxton has committed to Mount Saint Mary's for the fall. Claxton was a key piece to the 2023 State Championship for Kent Island High School's varsity men's lacrosse team and also contributed to the 2022 team that took home the state prize. This spring Claxton looks to lead the Bucs to a third-straight title.Athletics at KIHS have been very competitive and successful over the last few years. After two amazing years for the Bucs with the 2022 and 2023 graduating classes, Claxton's commitment to the Class of 2024 is unparalleled.\nLacrosse might be Claxton's best sport, but he's also been on varsity football since sophomore year. In 2022, the Bucs were eliminated in the state quarterfinals and looked to bounce back in 2023. That's exactly what they did. Led by Kasey Heath and Claxton, alongside many other key players the Bucs had on their stacked roster, they were able to make the state championship before falling just short to Milford Mill Academy. Claxton stated, \"There are a few memories that stick out to me. One was being a part of the first team in Kent Island football history to make the state championship. Another was running out of the tunnel to see a couple thousand people supporting you week in and week out. Breaking the receiving record for Kent Island school history was very cool as well.\" Claxton broke this record in his final high school game in a quarterfinal loss to Huntingtown. Claxton has also been a member of the varsity basketball team for the last two years.\nWhen asked about playing three sports during his high school career, Claxton said, \"I haven't had more than really a week off since Covid. I'm always doing something as soon as one season ends I was right on to another. Even the summer was filled with football camp, workouts, and club lacrosse. But this has taught me great discipline and mental toughness which I think is a hard lesson to find.\"\nLast year, Claxton's role on the Buccaneer lacrosse team increased dramatically. His father, Andy Claxton, holds second place as the all-time leader in face off wins at the University of Maryland, and last season served as the offensive coordinator for Kent Island. The state championship in 2023 meant a lot to Tucker Claxton. \"Scoring the game-winning goal in the state semifinals, and winning the state championship with my friends and teammates I've known since kindergarten meant a lot to me. From playing together the whole time, and being able to hug my dad after to celebrate, that was always the dream,\" he said.\nLuke Bonanni is a student reporter from Kent Island High School. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
