  {
    "title": "PLACING THEMSELVES",
    "date": "2024-02-24",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "PLACING THEMSELVES\n\n February 24, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 773\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Last Monday's Bayside Conference championships was not one of the more memorable performances by Queen Anne's County High's young girls wrestling program.\"We didn't wrestle our best,\" Lions head coach John Waters said.\nBut Waters used Monday as a learning tool heading into Friday's Class 2A-1A East Regional championships.\n\"I think with a lot of young wrestlers, not knowing what Baysides mean, Monday was probably the best thing for us because it showed that the girls needed to be focused and ready for every match. A few of them at Baysides won their first match and they weren't ready for the semifinals.\"\nThe Lions certainly appeared ready Friday, placing in 11 of the 14 weight classes for a group that now advances to the Class 2A-1A state championships, beginning Thursday at the Show Place Arena in Upper Marlboro.\nThe top three finishers in each weight class in the four regional girls tournaments advance, where the boys tournaments advance the top four at each weight.\n\"Overall I'm proud of the girls,\" said Waters, who had 10 in the championship finals and left with four individual champs. \"They really wrestled well. They've worked extremely hard to get where they are. Most of the girls on the team are first-year wrestlers. And if they're not first, they're second. So that says a lot about where they've come. Most of the varsity lineup are freshmen and sophomores.\"\nAmong that group, freshman Finley Patchett (125 pounds) and sophomore Bree Conard (115) each managed to win titles after being turned to their backs, as did junior Ally Conley (100).\n\"That just says a lot about their focus at practice this week,\" Waters said.\nConley (17-5) scored an early takedown against Parkside's Logan Newton (7-5), got turned, but recovered to earn a pin in 1 minute, 20 seconds to win the 100-pound crown. Conard (14-7) battled off her back and built a 10-4 lead before pinning Parkside's Brylee Davis (15-7) in 4:29 of the 115-pound final.\nThen there was Patchett (13-4), who led 7-1 after one period against Stephen Decatur's Rachel Plata (9-2) in the 125-pound final. Patchett was leading 9-2 when Plata escaped, scored a takedown and picked up three near-fall points to close within 9-8 heading into the third. But Patchett, starting off bottom, hit a reversal 17 seconds into the third and added a late takedown for a 13-9 decision.\nJunior Keira Corcoran (4-3) gave Queen Anne's a fourth champion when she pinned Decatur's Aya Mejber (2-11) in 3:45 to win the 135-pound championship. Corcoran got a takedown one minute into the match, but Mejber escaped to make it 2-1 entering the second period. Corcoran worked an escape 32 seconds into the second and got a takedown before earning her fall.\n\"We talked at practice about just being ready, staying focused,\" Waters said. \"And doing what they're best at; not trying to do something they saw on Youtube or something. Just do what you're good at.\"\nQueen Anne's got runner-up finishes from Ava Price (15-6) at 120, Delaney Gray (15-7) at 130, Julia Reburn (11-7) at 140, Lilly Hammond (14-5) at 145, Morgan Morris (5-6) at 170, and Kaylynn Bryant (17-4) at 190. Maggie Rooney (3-7) placed third at 235.\n\"There were a few matches, especially in the finals, we just came out a little flat,\" Waters said. \"It's hard to come back unless you're ready. But the ones that lost in the finals, they'll have a couple good days of practice, they'll bounce back. They'll be ready. They'll refocus. That's what we're looking for.\"\nColonel Richardson will have the East's second largest contingent at states, as it qualified eight, led by junior Ryleigh Porter (9-3), who pinned Cambridge-South Dorchester's Sienna Mitchell (9-7) to win the 235-pound weight class.\nColonel's Dale Swann (17-8) lost by fall in the 105-pound final, but four teammates earned pins in the consolation finals.\nLuna Infante-Reyes (11-13) pinned Kent County's Rhilynne Ashley-Pinder (4-11) in 4:44 of the 110-pound final. Frankie Aguiar (5-8) earned a 59-second fall over Decatur's Jessica Garcia (1-10) for third place at 120. Landine Dorleans (14-12) defeated James M. Bennett's Sinai Horsey (12-23) via a 2:35 pin at 125. And Olivia McGregor (17-2) wrestled back to earn a 3:46 fall over Queen Anne's Adelina Dickens (8-16) at 1:55.\nColonel's Annabelle Morales (2-11) and Danika Jeanty (4-9) won their consolation finals at 130 and 170, respectively with a bye.\nKent Island went two for two in the championship finals.\nSophomore Lilliana Mellinger (10-1) got a pair of takedowns before pinning Queen Anne's Price in 1:25 to win the 120-pound title. And senior Kendra Coffey (6-2) bundled two first-period takedowns with a second-period escape en route to a 5-2 decision over Queen Anne's Hammond in the 145-pound final. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 2,
    "day": 24,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
      "explanation": "The article reports on high school girls wrestling competitions, detailing matches, athletes, and coaches, which is clearly a sports news story."
    },
