  {
    "title": "TIME TRIALS",
    "date": "2024-02-27",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "TIME TRIALS\n\n February 27, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1299\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Giuseppe Mellinger needed a late block.Tremaine Jackson needed an extra minute.\nAnd Scottie Ford. Well, he needed more time than either one of them.\nNo matter the time frame, Jackson, Ford and Mellinger each finished with the same result Saturday night at Queen Anne's County High, winning their respective weight classes at the Class 2A-1A East Regional wrestling championships.\n\"He's been one of our elite wrestlers for three years now,\" Queen Anne's head coach David Stricker said of Jackson, who with his 5-3 overtime victory against Rising Sun's Xzavier Brown in the 126-pound final earned his 99th career win. \"Tremaine, he's just so quick. And his power has really come along this year. When he gets in on a leg it's really hard to fend off.\"\nSaturday's region final was the third time Jackson and Brown faced off this season. Brown pieced together an 8-3 decision at the Westminster Duals in late December, then earned a 3-1 overtime win when the two met in the 2A-1A East Regional Duals earlier this month. Their third encounter would also take longer than six minutes.\n\"They've been wrestling each other since youth, so they've known each other really well,\" Stricker said. \"I'm not surprised it went that far.\"\nA senior, Jackson (35-3) scored a first-period takedown, but Brown escaped with 44 seconds left in the period to make it 2-1 after one. Brown (35-8) rode Jackson through a scoreless second period, and hit a reversal 11 seconds into the third period for a 3-2 lead. Jackson then got an escape off a restart with 15 seconds left in the third to forge a 3-3 tie, sending the match into a 1-minute overtime.\n\"It's obviously been a bit harder because they've been training him, trying to study my style, his coaches,\" Jackson said. \"They obviously know me very well, the good moves I do. So yeah, it definitely was a lot more difficult than the other times wrestling him.\"\nBrown got in a single-leg shot in the overtime, but Jackson blocked, spun and earned the takedown for the 5-3 sudden victory.\n\"My last match at the high school, and what was going through my head was, I can't lose,\" said Jackson, whose second regional title came five days after winning his third Bayside Conference crown. \"I feel great. I put in the work. I think I've got a good shot of getting in the state finals.\"\nFord also had a familiar opponent in his 138-pound final — Parkside junior Rocco Tully — who the Kent County sophomore decisioned 6-0 in the Bayside championship five days earlier.\n\"I knew it was going to be tougher,\" said Ford, who coupled two takedowns with a reversal for Monday's 6-0 victory. \"Every time you wrestle somebody it feels like it gets tougher and tougher. They always learn from it. I was nervous. I would have much rather faced somebody else I hadn't seen before.\"\nAfter a scoreless first period, Ford (37-1) hit a switch off bottom for a reversal with 35 seconds left in the second period for a 2-0 lead. Tully (35-9) scored a takedown with 44 seconds to go in the third and rode Ford the rest of the period, forcing overtime.\nNeither wrestler scored in the 1-minute overtime. Starting on top, Ford rode Tully through the first 30-second sudden-victory period. Less than two seconds into the second 30-second period, Ford reversed Tully and rode out the remainder of the period on top for the 4-2 win.\n\"I knew I had a good switch on him because on Monday I had a nice switch,\" Ford said. \"He knows how to throw legs, so he's really tough on top, but I knew I could get out. I just beat him off the whistle.\"\nSomething that did not surprise Kent County head coach Chris Yiannakis.\n\"He's been on that switch,\" Yiannakis said of Ford. \"That's one of his number one escapes from bottom. Most people can't stop it. It's so fast. Rocco did stop it in the first couple of periods though. He just wore him down by the final (period) and he couldn't stop it.\"\nThe 175-pound final was yet another Bayside rematch, as Mellinger (36-5) clashed with Parkside's Canaan Mapp (24-8) for the third time this season.\n\"The first time we wrestled he actually was more aggressive,\" said Mellinger, who defeated Mapp via a 12-4 major decision during the dual-meet season. \"He would actually tie up with me and stuff. So that allowed me to work my offense. The second time, at Baysides (a 7-2 Mellinger win), he would run around, he kept moving, but I got a few takedowns there.\n\"This time, because I think his coaches were telling him how to make some good adjustments, he wasn't tying up,\" Mellinger continued. \"He was keeping me at a distance. He would try to work in some shots, I would try to work in mine. But we both got a good shot defense. And then when we're so far apart, it's hard to score points.\"\nMellinger got a double-leg takedown 54 seconds into the match, but Mapp escaped 11 seconds later, making it 2-1 heading into the second. Mapp escaped five seconds into he second period to tie the match.\nIt remained that way until 18 seconds into the third, when Mellinger, starting on bottom, escaped to take a 3-2 lead. Mellinger blocked a shot midway through the period before a neutral restart with 41 seconds left. Mellinger got in a shot, but Mapp countered with a shot with less than 20 seconds left. The Kent Island senior, who was named the Bayside Conference's defensive player of the year in football for 2023, could not be taken down though, hanging on for the 3-2 win.\n\"I started getting a little nervous at the end when I took a shot and then he re-shot and got in on my leg with like 15 seconds left,\" Mellinger said. \"And I was able to defend that because if he had gotten that and I didn't get up in time, that would have been the match.\"\nIt was Mellinger's first regional title.\n\"It's nice to win, said Mellinger, whose younger sister Lilliana won the 120-pound title at Friday night's girls 2A-1A East Regional. \"My sister and I, we're both Bayside champions. Now, we're both regional champions. Now, we're both competing for a state title. That's cool. It's a lot of fun.\"\nSaid Kent Island head coach Tommy Sitar of Mellinger, \"He had another great tournament. He worked really well in his matches leading up to the final, and fortunately got the win in the final. It's hard to beat a wrestler three times in a season. It's a good feat.\"\nKent Island seniors Greg Couch (132) and Cody Sandefer (144) each took runner-up honors, and seniors Brandon Adler (157) and Nick Morey (190) each won their consolation final to place third.\nThe top four in each weight class advance to this year's state tournament, which begins Thursday at the Show Place Arena in Upper Marlboro and concludes Saturday.\nStephen Decatur and Rising Sun each advanced nine to states, while North Caroline finished with the most wrestlers from a North Bayside school, qualifying six and two alternates.\n\"We had a rough Monday at Baysides,\" North Caroline head coach Robin Eaton said. \"I preached to them all week 'That wasn't the week to shine. 'Work harder and fix a few things and we could shine at regionals.' And that's what they did. They did everything we worked on; wrestled very smart, wrestled tough. Didn't make mistakes.\"\nNorth Caroline freshman Caleb Melvin (120), junior Corey Bunce (165) and senior Dakota Kinsey (285) each wrestled-back to win their respective consolation finals, while sophomores Darly Ortega (106), Connor Frank (113), and Jacob Carpenter (126) placed fourth.\nKent County senior Brady Sutton decisioned Harford Vo Tech's Nikolai Chervak in the 144-pound consolation final, and Cambridge-South Dorchester sophomore Mark Jones placed fourth at 285. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
