  {
    "title": "ENDING ON A GREAT NOTE",
    "date": "2024-02-29",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "ENDING ON A GREAT NOTE\n\n February 29, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1261\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Gary Gould was admittedly a bit baffled as he kept watch on Beth Tfiloh School's boys basketball team heading toward the postseason.\"They lost a couple games the last week of the (regular) season,\" said Saints Peter and Paul High's head coach, whose team had lost three straight to the Warriors. \"And then some of the games they won, they only won by like four or five points to teams that we were beating by double-digits. I was confused why other teams could play them so well and we couldn't. And then I thought about it and said, it had to be me. I was doing too much. I was trying to be too good in the x's and o's part, instead of just letting us play.\"\nGould let his team play Saturday night, made a halftime adjustment, then watched the Sabres pull away in the second half for a 68-58 victory over Beth Tfiloh in the Maryland Interscholastic Athletic Association C Conference championship game at UMBC's Chesapeake Employers Insurance Arena.\n\"It feels amazing,\" said senior forward K'den Spears, named player of the game after Sts. Peter & Paul won its first MIAA C title since 2016. \"The only reason why we keep losing games is when we selfishly play. We really played as a group and really helped each other and built each other up.\"\nThough a mid-season injury limited junior star Garett Hemingway's minutes, Sts. Peter & Paul had built a nice hot streak heading into the playoffs, having won nine of its last 10, including its last eight straight.\nBeth Tfiloh defeated Sts. Peter & Paul in last year's conference championship, won a two-point nail-biter in Easton on Jan. 11, then rolled to a 72-45 victory at home Jan. 31, dealing the Sabres their most lopsided loss of the season, as well as the only blemish during their hot streak.\nBut Warriors head coach Ari Braun didn't necessarily put a lot of stock in his team's Jan. 31 romp.\n\"The first game this year was down to the wire at Saints Peter and Paul. Either team could have won it,\" Braun said of the Jan. 11 clash in which Hemingway scored 33 points. \"The second time, I think that was (Hemingway's) first game back from his ankle and he was not the same player. They weren't all right yet, and we made shots and defended. Obviously we played an awesome game, and it looks worse than maybe it should have.\"\nThen came Saturday night's title bout.\n\"I think the difference was we were more focused,\" Gould said. \"We realized the game at Beth was something we knew wasn't going to happen again because we didn't shoot the ball well. Plus that second game, I did too much. I was trying to coach too much strategy wise and take away things and add stuff. I think I had my kids playing too slow.\n\"The kids knew that last year we didn't know what to expect, so we played with a little nerves,\" Gould said of the Sabres' 65-58 loss in the title game. \"But this year we knew going in what to do. So the kids were just focused about getting it done, went out and just played. And we knew it was just our time.\"\nHemingway (game-high 19 points) canned a pair of 3-pointers early as the Sabres (16-8) carried a 27-24 lead into the locker room after a back-and-forth first half. And while he was guarded against over-coaching again, Gould searched for an edge during the first half.\n\"I was trying to figure out on the sideline what was happening; what were they doing that was working, and what we could take away to give us the advantage,\" Gould said.\nGould's key takeaway was switching from the man-to-man and 2-3 zone defenses the Sabres used in the first half to a 1-2-2 zone. The move proved critical, especially when it came to defending Warrior senior guard Michael Cohen, who scored a game-high 35-points in last year's title game.\n\"Their skip passing wasn't available to them,\" Gould said. \"And then we kept number four, Michael Cohen, in front of us so he wasn't able to penetrate and do all the stuff he did to us last year. He was the guy who hurt us.\"\nConsistently penetrating inside and drawing contact, Cohen went 19 of 28 from the line in last year's final, which included a 12-of-16 effort in the fourth quarter. In Saturday's rematch he finished with 17 points.\n\"Defense was big for us,\" Hemingway said. \"We came into the game saying, if we can slow them down, hold Cohen. Slow him down, get in front of him, take charges. As long as we get the defense down the offense will just come.\"\nBraun agreed Gould's defensive tweak was big.\n\"They did an unbelievable job with their defense, Braun said. \"They started off in the man, which I didn't think it went great for them, but then they quickly pivoted to the zone. And they were very disciplined in staying back and clogging the paint and not really giving Michael as many opportunities to drive. Last year in the championship game, they were pressing and allowing us to get out in the open court, and that led to a lot of his free throws and baskets. But this year they did a great job of staying at home defensively.\"\nThat move produced steals and kick-started the Sabres' transition game. Senior point guard Brayden Smith scored eight of his 13 points in the third quarter, and Robbie Cunningham netted seven of his 11, as Sts. Peter & Paul outscored the Warriors 19-9 for a 46-33 lead heading into the fourth quarter.\nBraun, who graduated a number of seniors from last season's title team, acknowledged two other significant factors. Elan Vogelstein, Beth Tfiloh's top rebounder and assist man, suffered a thigh injury in the second Sts. Peter & Paul game, and has been sidelined the remainder of the season.\n\"I don't know if that changes the outcome of the game, but obviously that would have helped us,\" Braun said.\nAnd then there was Sts. Peter & Paul's job on the boards, especially the effort delivered by Spears.\n\"I think what really hurt us even more than the zone was their offensive rebounding,\" Braun said. \"When we can get a stop and end it with a rebound we can then run. That's way different than them getting rebound after rebound and pounding us and then scoring. And then we're just playing against a settled defense. So I thought they just did an awesome job in terms of game plan and execution, just playing with a lot of effort on the court.\n\"And I think number zero, he was a beast on the glass and our guys really struggled with that,\" Braun said of Spears.\nSpears was a force on the boards in the Sabres' semifinal victory over Cristo Rey. Against the Warriors, he was again tough on the glass, yanking down eight rebounds, and in the score column, finishing with 13 points.\n\"That's the best I've ever seen K'den play,\" Hemingway said.\nGould echoed that sentiment.\n\"He was more consistent and forceful in what he was doing,\" Gould said of Spears\nSpears was also more confident.\n\"I just felt because this is the second time for us being there, and there was no point in us being nervous because it was the same team we played last year,\" Spears said.\nBeth Tfiloh (25-8) outscored the Sabres, 25-22, in the fourth, but it couldn't rally enough.\n\"As a team I felt more together,\" Spears said. \"This was our last game and I just wanted to end it off on a really great note.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
