  {
    "title": "QA's better half enough to top Rams",
    "date": "2024-03-02",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "QA's better half enough to top Rams\n\n March 2, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 846\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Freshmen Bea Holthaus, Laila Coles and Kariana Hickson have been huge in the Queen Anne's County High girls basketball team's return to the top of the North Bayside this season.Friday night, juniors Lucy Taylor and RJ Ensor showed how big they could be.\nTaylor and Ensor combined for nine consecutive points during a 9-2 run to open the second half that staggered third-seeded Parkside while launching the Lions to a 47-31 victory in a Class 2A East Region II semifinal.\nSecond-seeded Queen Anne's (20-2) travels to Salisbury to face top-seed Wicomico (22-0) on Wednesday at 5 p.m. for the region title. The Indians are the only team to defeat the Lions this season, rolling to an 81-41 victory Feb. 8, and a 58-52 win in the Bayside Conference championship 12 days later.\n\"I've been trying to find my shot all year it feels like,\" said Taylor, who led Queen Anne's with 15 points. \"But tonight, I don't know. I saw the freshmen, their first year being here (in the playoffs). And being one of the captains I felt like I needed to kind of do something. Kind of step up and keep the encouragement up. It was a really good team win tonight.\"\nQueen Anne's looked like a team that was playing its first game in 10 days, as Jeliah Gardner scored six of her game-high 16 points in the first 4:28 to help the Rams (18-4) to a 10-2 lead.\n\"Slow start,\" Lions head coach Mike Kern said. \"The first six points were off transition. Then their next couple baskets were backside so we switched to a 2-3.\"\nColes (11 points) sandwiched a 3-pointer and a layup around two Holthaus free throws to get Queen Anne's within 12-9 by the end of the first period.\nGardner's three-point play extended the Rams' lead to 15-9 with 2 minutes elapsed in the second quarter. But Ensor (eight points, six rebounds, four assists) scored on a drive. Coles snagged a rebound and went coast to coast for a layup before Jordan Taylor scored off an Ensor dish, tying the game at 15-15 with 2:50 remaining in the second quarter.\nGardner scored consecutive baskets to help move Parkside back to a 20-15 lead before Ensor hit a free throw with 5 seconds left to make it a four-point Lions' deficit at the half.\nBut Taylor and Ensor gave Queen Anne's a quick offensive jolt to start the third quarter. Taylor converted an Ensor pass for an inside bucket. Ensor splashed a 3-pointer. Taylor scored from in close for a 23-20 Lions lead.\nZion Oge scored the Rams' first basket of the second half with 4 minutes left in the third quarter. Taylor answered, taking a Coles feed and scoring inside. TaLayah Clark (nine points) scored, drawing Parkside within 25-24.\nBut the Rams wouldn't score again until 12 seconds remained in the third quarter, as the Lions dominated the defensive glass, allowing Parkside just one shot time after time.\n\"Total collapse,\" Parkside head coach Warren White said after the Rams were held to just six points in the third quarter and five in the fourth. \"We've been telling these kids all year, 'You have to play through this mental stuff.\n\"And free throws killed us,\" said White, whose team was 3 of 15 from the foul line. \"The whole game I don't think we made more than three free throws. That would have kept us in the game.\"\nBut Queen Anne's work on the defensive boards helped fuel a 16-6 third quarter and a 15-5 fourth period.\n\"Defensive rebounding,\" Kern said. \"And that was what our mentality was: one shot and done. So we did better rebounding. We like to press but we kind of just sat back in a 2-3 zone and made it tough. And they shot a lot from the outside. We just got to get the rebound. I don't know how good they were from the three-point line but they weren't very good tonight.\"\nQueen Anne's used a 7-0 run in the closing 2:21 of the third quarter to begin pulling away. Jordan hit a short jumper, Ensor converted a steal into a layup, and Coles sank a pair of free throws before a Taylor free throw capped a 15-4 third-quarter run, giving the Lions a 32-26 lead.\nThe Lions continued to widen their lead in the fourth, starting with Julia Rendulic, who came off the bench to drain a 3-pointer to start another 9-0 Queen Anne's run.\n\"Defensively we did well,\" Kern said. \"I think in the second half when RJ hit that three got us going, got us playing with some momentum. Julia comes in and hits a three. Those are the kind of things that are big plays.\n\"Laila had a couple of good steals, and took it to the basket and got fouled,\" Kern continued. \"And that combination was good. \"K-5 (Hickson) was the leading scorer the last two big games we played and she had a little slow night, and so did Bea. But it was good to see Lucy and RJ step up, and Taylor Jordan came off the bench and got a couple big baskets.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
