  {
    "title": "STATE CHAMPS",
    "date": "2024-03-06",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "STATE CHAMPS\n\n March 6, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1457\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n David Stricker was trying to digest the past three days.Or maybe it was the past three years.\nSome 16 hours earlier, Queen Anne's County High's girls wrestling team and coaching staff piled onto a podium at the Show Place Arena in Upper Marlboro in celebration of winning the first-ever team title at the Maryland Public Secondary Schools Athletic Association's girls state wrestling tournament.\nSeated at the end of a long table inside a school conference room early Sunday afternoon, Stricker was still wrestling to find the right words as he looked at the state championship trophy perched in front of him.\n\"As I told the girls, I had about three sentences of a speech prepared in my brain and I only got to about three, and then I started rambling,\" Stricker said with a smile. \"It's just humbling. Even this morning, flipping through pictures and stuff, I still tear up just thinking about it.\"\nIt's only been three years since Stricker, Queen Anne's boys head wrestling coach, started the school's girls program.\nThat first team had only three girls but ended the season with then-freshman Ally Conley winning the 100-pound state title.\nThe program grew to 14 last season, and 22 this season, which started with a December bus ride to the Maverick Duals at Manchester Valley, during which Stricker and girls head coach John Waters joked about possibly winning a state title.\nThe Lions went 11-0 in dual-meet competition, which included defeating Winters Mill in the first dual meet in MPSSAA history where both teams filled all 14 weight classes.\nThey had three individual champions at the Bayside Conference tournament. Four days later, they crowned four champions at the Class 2A-1A East Region championships.\nThen came the newly formatted three-day state tournament, which ended Saturday night with Conley earning a second-period pin in the 100-pound final for her second state title in three years.\n\"Yes, we ended up with that,\" Waters said Sunday, nodding toward the state championship trophy. \"We earned that. But we wanted the girls and the entire team, coaches included, to enjoy the moment. We told them Thursday when we get there, 'Take pictures. Walk around the building. No matter what, us coaches will be there next year hopefully. But we're not going to bring the same team back. This team was special.'\"\nQueen Anne's had a wrestler in 12 of the 14 weight classes at states. Of those 12, only one, Morgan Morris, didn't win a match.\nBut Morris was symbolic of the hard work ethic Waters, Stricker and assistant Jimmy Swindell forever preached at practice.\nMorris lost in a wrestle-off at 155 pounds with teammate Adelina Dickens prior to the conference championships. The sophomore could easily have opted to begin focusing on the upcoming softball season. Instead, Waters and Stricker asked if Morris would consider bumping up to 170 for regionals.\n\"We don't have anybody there,\" Waters said to Morris of the weight class, \"and there's a good chance you might make states.\"\nThough there were only three wrestlers in the 170 bracket at regionals, Morris chose to give it a shot, won a match, and placed second to qualify for states.\n\"She did one heckuva job,\" Waters said of Morris, who wrestled opponents that not only had 15 to 20 pounds on her, but had more experience. \"She came across a couple of tough matches. The girls were just heavier.\"\nQueen Anne's other 11 wrestlers won at least once.\nSenior Julia Reburn, one of the originals on the inaugural 2021-22 team, earned a win at 140, as did sophomores Lilly Hammond (145), Dickens (155) and Maggie Rooney (220).\nJuniors Ava Price (120) and Keira Corcoran (135) each went 2-2. Senior Delaney Gray (130), another 2022 original, went 3-2 and reached the consolation quarterfinals, as did sophomore Bree Conard, who went 2-2 at 115.\nAnd while Conley went 4-0 en route to the title that capped a 21-5 season, no Lions wrestler won more over the tournament's three days than Kaylynn Bryant.\nBryant won her pigtail match at 190, lost 5-4 to two-time defending state champion Jabea Ewane of Winters Mill, then never lost again.\nThe junior pinned her first two consolation-bracket opponents, then gutted out a 5-4 victory in an ultimate tiebreaker. She earned another one-point decision in the quarterfinals, then pinned her next two opponents, notching a 4:33 fall in the consolation final.\n\"I knew she could get on the podium. But she just kept going,\" said Stricker of Bryant, who went 7-1 to finish a 24-5 season. \"Actually her pin in the consolation quarterfinals is what kind of put us over the top team score wise.\"\nThen there was Finley Patchett at 125.\n\"Finley and Kaylynn, they surpassed what they were supposed to do,\" Waters said. \"Kaylynn we kind of figured she'd come back to get in that third- and fourth-(place) match.\n\"But Finley was a wild card because she's only wrestled for two months,\" Waters continued. \"And she was probably in the hardest weight class from top to bottom. And she's one of the few freshmen in that weight class. A lot of the other girls were 11th graders with four or five years of wrestling experience.\"\nPatchett (17-6) wrestled nothing but juniors the whole tournament. She won by fall in the first round before getting pinned in her second match. After winning her first two consolation bouts by decision, she lost by fall in the consolation semifinals, but bounced back with a pin to place fifth.\n\"Finley as a first-year wrestler just did amazing things,\" Stricker said. \"At the beginning of the season she was in sort of a logjam with Bree and Ava. We were trying to figure out what weight class to get her to go to. As the season went on we kind of got everybody situated, and once she got more consistent mat time, I mean she just really kind of started to take off.\"\n\"She did extra practice at home,\" Stricker added. \"Then what she did at the state tournament. … That's the biggest state tournament that there's ever been. There were 24 girls per weight class, which was even more than the boys (16 per weight class). The depth and the ability in the tournament, and then for her to go out and take fifth, and only lose to people who finished above her on the podium\" — Hammond junior Clara Freeman (third, 21-13) and Montgomery Blair junior Joni Chuka (fourth, 24-7) — \"was really impressive.\"\nAs has Queen Anne's rapid rise to the top.\n\"There's a joke in my house,\" Stricker said. \"My wife says, 'My son can swim, but my daughters can wrestle.' So when I started this, my whole thought, at least at our school, was to take away the stigma of girls wrestling. It's not like this novelty thing, that one girl wrestler on the team.\"\nOther schools around the state began building strong girls programs, most notably Manchester Valley, Francis Scott Key, Winters Mill, La Plata and Eastern Tech. Waters began tracking those \"so-called big dogs\" in tournaments and noticed a trend.\n\"I remember all the big-dog schools, they're just talking about these certain schools,\" Waters said. \"I remember going to Dave and saying, 'There's a lot of good girls out there wrestling right now that these schools. ... they're not giving them any credit. These girls are going to come in and there's not going to be one team that dominates and wins like six state girls titles this year. It's going to be a lot of random schools with three or four good girls.'\"\nWaters was proven correct at states, with no school having more than one champion in the 14 weight classes.\nQueen Anne's girls head coach also thought the way his team wrestled, it could challenge for a state title in the near future.\n\"We had four first-year wrestlers go to states,\" Waters said. \"And we have some girls with only two years experience. So I'm thinking by Ally's senior year they should be able to push for the team title.\"\nThat time frame got pushed up last weekend, as the Lions amassed 119 points on the way to the title, outdistancing runner-up Oakland Mills (86), third-place Francis Scott Key (82) and fourth-place Stephen Decatur and Eastern Tech (80).\nFifteen of Queen Anne's combined 21 losses at the tournament came against wrestlers who placed in the top six.\n\"It's like they are on the forefront of the state of Maryland in the fastest growing sport in the nation,\" Stricker said. \"They're doing more than I ever could have possibly imagined.\n\"I figured it would take us 10, 15 years to get to this point,\" Stricker said with another head nod toward the state trophy. \"And I mean we led it from Thursday to Saturday the whole time. We were always in first.\"\nNotes: Rondell Bryant, Frank Conley, Kyron Hutchins, Sean Blackwell, Kei Rickard and Cody Barbiche also helped coach this season. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 3,
    "day": 6,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
      "explanation": "The article reports on a high school girls wrestling team winning a state championship, which is clearly a sports event."
    },
    "llm_classification_meta": {
      "model": "gpt-oss:120b",
      "llm_failed": false
    },
