  },
  {
    "title": "Late push seals Vikings' title shot",
    "date": "2024-03-12",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Late push seals Vikings' title shot\n\n March 12, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 691\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n SILVER SPRING — Anthony Hughes and Koby Ennals know basketball can be a game of runs.The two juniors were part of the run that mattered most Tuesday night.\nA one-time 17-point lead reduced to three, Hughes and Ennals helped fuel a decisive 8-0 fourth-quarter burst that returned momentum back to Cambridge-South Dorchester High's boys basketball team en route to the Vikings earning a 60-53 victory over Fort Hill in a Class 1A state semifinal at Montgomery Blair High School.\nTop-seeded Cambridge-SD (24-1) will bid for its first state title since 1996 Friday, when it faces No. 2 seed Edmondson-Westside (22-2) for the Class 1A championship at the University of Maryland's Xfinity Center. Edmondson defeated third-seeded Smithsburg, 79-59, in Tuesday's other 1A semifinal.\n\"We just got a believe attitude,\" Cambridge-SD head coach Shawn Tucker said. \"We told them, 'If we just slow it down we'll be fine. We just got to be patient.'\"\nThe Vikings went on an 11-1 run to open the game, and packaged a 13-2 scoring spree during the second quarter, when they outscored the Sentinels, 23-8, to carry a 37-20 lead into halftime.\n\"We turned it over way too much in the first half,\" Fort Hill head coach Thad Burner said. \"I thought that their energy was a lot better than ours, particularly in the second quarter when they extended that lead.\"\nSenior point guard LaShaun Moody (team-high 18 points) had a three-point play and a drive that helped Cambridge-SD build its early 11-1 lead. But Fort Hill (19-4) got 3-pointers from DeShaun Brown (team-high 18 points) and Jabril Daniels (10 points) in the final 3:16 of the first quarter to pull within 14-12 heading into the second quarter.\nThe Vikings led 16-14 when they pieced together a 9-0 run midway through the second quarter. Ennals (13 points), who scored his 1,000th-career point in the first quarter, converted a Moody dish for a bucket off the glass. Semaj Pinder (nine points) hit a turnaround jumper then scored on a drive. Ennals scored on a put-back before Thomas Wilson canned a 3-pointer for a 27-14 Vikings lead.\nBrown hit another trey to end a three-plus minute Fort Hill scoring drought. But Moody hit a 3-pointer, Ennals made two free throws, and Darren Belizare scored on a follow before Moody splashed another trey at the buzzer for a 37-20 Vikings lead.\n\"We preach getting off to a fast start,\" Tucker said. \"We knew Fort Hill was a second-half team and it was going to be a battle. So to get out on a team like that early is huge.\"\nThe Sentinels went on an 11-2 run to start the third quarter, getting within 39-31 with 4:36 to go in the period. Hughes stopped that momentum briefly when he converted a steal into a layup, putting the Vikings back up by 10. A pair of Sterling Perkins free throws moved Cambridge-SD to a 44-33 lead heading into the fourth quarter.\nFort Hill came out bombing in the fourth. Landon Sensabaugh hit back-to-back three-balls and Brown turned a steal into a layup, cutting Cambridge-SD's lead to 45-41 with 6:30 left. Tucker called time and watched Hughes score another big bucket inside to make it a six-point lead.\nJabril Daniels answered with another 3, shaving Fort Hill's deficit to three at 47-44 with 5:38 remaining.\nBut Cambridge-SD showed it too could respond.\n\"We just had to come back and be ourself,\" Hughes said. \"It's win or go home.\"\nHughes scored on a put-back. Ennals converted a pair of free throws. Moody scored on a follow before Ennals snatched a rebound and raced coast-to-coast for a bucket that pushed Cambridge-SD's lead to 55-44 with under 4 minutes to play.\n\"We made some pretty good runs in the third and the fourth, but (number) two (Ennals) and five (Moody), they were pretty good,\" Burner said.\nThe Sentinels wouldn't get any closer than nine the rest of the way, as Moody scored on a drive and made three of four free throws in the final 1:24 to cement the victory and Cambridge-SD's first state final appearance since 1996.\n\"They're a team that's not going to give up,\" Ennals said of Fort Hill. \"But we're a team that's not going to give up either.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
