  {
    "title": "GOLDEN DELIVERY",
    "date": "2024-03-14",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "GOLDEN DELIVERY\n\n March 14, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 962\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Easton High head swim coach Tim Kroeger calls the 200-yard freestyle a hard race to swim.\"Everybody seems to have a love-hate relationship with that event because it's too short to be distance, and it's too long to be a sprint,\" Kroeger said. \"It's one of those things you have to train for, you have to get a feel for. In a sense a lot of kids don't like swimming it because it's just hard. It's hard to find those kids who are really good at it.\"\nIn the 16 years the Maryland Public Secondary Schools Athletic Association has held the state swimming championships, the Bayside Conference has had only four champions — all girls — win the 200-yard free, including three from Easton.\nThe Warriors added a fourth Feb. 24.\nMaddie Weems showed why she was a heavy favorite in the event, as she outdistanced her closest competitor by over 4 seconds en route to winning the girls' 200 freestyle title during the Class 3A/2A/1A state championships at the University of Maryland's Eppley Recreation Center.\n\"Maddie always delivers the big swims when we she needs it,\" Kroeger said of his sophomore star who was the only Bayside swimmer to win a state title this year. \"She's been very consistent.\n\"She's kind of branched out,\" Kroeger added. \"Last year she was more of a freestyle sprinter for us, and this year she wanted to explore some of the other events. I mean she's good at all of the events, which is a good and bad thing for any swimmer really.\"\nWeems was second in the 100 freestyle and third in the 50 free at last year's state meet.\nShe changed things up heading into this year's postseason, winning the 100 butterfly and 100 breaststroke at the conference championships in early February.\nBut when it came time for regionals and states, Weems opted for events she thought she could place in.\n\"I thought about doing the 100 fly and 100 breast, but then I really wanted to do freestyle,\" Weems said. \"And I looked at where I would maybe place the best. I was like, I feel the best in the 200 free.\"\nBut.\n\"I've never liked the 200,\" Weems said smiling. \"But I had been practicing it a lot, and I just felt the most comfortable in it. So I was like, I'll just try and give it my best.\"\nWeems practiced a lot. She also swam a lot.\nBecause of poor weather conditions, the 3A/2A/1A East Regional championships were moved from Feb. 17 to Feb. 20 — four days before states. A member of the Talbot County YMCA team, Weems was also competing in the Maryland Club State Championships at St. Mary's College, which were sandwiched around the MPSSAA state meet.\nWeems was admittedly tired and sore after swimming five events — including preliminaries — the Friday before the MPSSAA state meet. She didn't do well in the morning prelims of the 200 free, but rebounded that night, clocking a personal-record time of 1:54.17.\nAnd though that effort potentially stoked Weems' confidence heading into Saturday's MPSSAA meet, it may have also added a bit more pressure to the favorite's shoulders.\n\"Everybody was telling me I was going to win,\" said Weems, who was seeded No. 1 by 6 seconds. \"But I was like, 'I don't know.' I was nervous, but I felt confident. And even if it ended up being close I knew that I would be able to push through.\"\nShe did just that, reaching the first turn with a lead she never relinquished on the way to a first-place 1:54.53, giving Easton its third straight title in the 200 free. Kroeger's daughter, Molly, won the championship in 2022 (1:53.76) and last year, when she set a 3A/2A/1A record of 1:51.94. Weems' cousin, Tori Weems, won the event in 2017 (1:55.96).\nThe only other Bayside swimmer to win the 200 was Queen Anne's County's Kelsey Lessard (2:03.6) in 2010.\nWeems was back in the pool for the 100 fly, and held the lead before getting passed over the final 25 yards, taking fourth in 1:00.58.\n\"My body just kind of gave out,\" Weems said.\nWeems teamed with Katheryne Kelsey, Claire Morris and Isabella Westerfield to place sixth (1:59.42) 200 medley relay and fourth in the 400 freestyle relay (3:50.12), helping defending state champion Easton place sixth in the final girls standings — the highest finish by any Bayside team.\nA senior, Westerfield posted fifth-place finishes in the 50 free (25.24) and 500 free (5:35.89).\nKent Island's girls finished seventh, led by freshmen Clare O'Keefe, who was second in the 50 free (24.82) and Kara Seymour, fourth in the 100 breaststroke (1:11.18), eighth in the 200 individual medley (2:24.88). Seymour and O'Keefe joined Marissa White and Maddie Parks to take fifth in the 200 free relay (1:45.29) and seventh in the 200 medley relay (1:59.86).\nQueen Anne's sophomore Madalyn Clagett was fourth in the 100 free (55.50) and sixth in the 200 free (2:03.29). Kent County sophomore Mary Jane Joiner was ninth in the 200 free (2:06.74).\nKent County junior Henry Martinez delivered the top efforts of any Bayside swimmer on the boys side, finishing fourth in both the 50 free (22.32) and 100 free (49.71).\nEaston again had the best finish of any Bayside boys team, placing 11th. Senior Brady Tipton was seventh in the 200 free (1:52.08), ninth in the 500 free (5:08.76), and teamed with Bryce Slaughter, Aiden Lavezzo and Alex Harding to take eighth in the 400 free relay (3:35.88) and ninth in the 200 free relay (1:37.31).\nKent Island's Jack Da Rosa, Reef Adams, Johnathan Mangold and Giovanni Aguirre were 10th in the 200 free relay (1:37.32). Mangold later joined Dylan McCormick, Jacob Reeder and Keefer Pullen to finish 10th in the 400 free relay.\nQueen Anne's William Meyer, Quinlan Justice, Giles Stanton and Jacob Gardner were ninth in the 400 free relay (3:39.22). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 3,
    "day": 14,
