  {
    "title": "Sabres' first game may be toughest of season",
    "date": "2024-03-16",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Sabres' first game may be toughest of season\n\n March 16, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 632\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Saints Peter and Paul High's girls lacrosse team may not play a tougher opponent this season.\"I knew this was going to be a great matchup,\" Sabres head coach Alison Beyer said of Friday's season-opening clash with St. Mary's. \"It was just an exciting game to watch. This is how lacrosse should be, and that's what I told them at halftime. I said, 'Just play lacrosse. Play like you know how to play.' And that's what they did.\"\nSts. Peter & Paul shaved a five-goal deficit to one midway through the third quarter, but couldn't get any closer, as the Saints scored seven of the game's last eight goals on the way to a 16-9 victory on a cloudy afternoon.\n\"This will be our toughest game of the season,\" Beyer said. \"I got to see how I know they can play. I saw some real good things.\"\nAmong those good things was sophomore goalie Kylie Kroniser, who made five big saves to keep the Sabres within striking distance until late.\n\"Kylie (Kroniser) played a heckuva game,\" Beyer said. \"She was on fire. I'm loving Kylie this year. It could have been way worse than it was. She made some real difficult saves, at real key moments too. That was crucial.\"\nSophomore Caroline Ewing (three goals) scored twice and classmate Avery McCall (three goals) once to give Sts. Peter & Paul and early 3-2 lead. But Sydney DeMaio (two goals), Kendall Kemezis and Anna Ervin (two goals) each scored in a 2-minute, 21-second span to give St. Mary's a 5-3 lead after one quarter,\nErvin scored her second goal 39 seconds into the second quarter, but Sabres senior Morgan Quade countered. Circling from behind the net, Quade took a stick check to the head before slipping a shot past St. Mary's goalie Addy Drain to get Sts. Peter & Paul back within two at 6-4.\nBut St. Mary's steadily passed the ball around the perimeter before working a weave in front of Kroniser that produced goals from Chloe Wiese (hat trick), Ellie Spurge and Jayci Frederick for a 9-4 advantage heading into the break.\n\"St. Mary's knew how to work us,\" said Beyer, who watched nine different Saints score, including six with multiple goals. \"And they're just good. They knew exactly what angle to get through our defense, and they were killing us on the slide.\"\nSts. Peter & Paul rallied though in the third quarter.\nEwing bounced a free-position opportunity into the net 1:15 into the third. Freshman Emery Zimmerman tallied on another free-position chance with just under 9 minutes left in the period. McCall capitalized on another free-position shot with an overhand shot, drawing the Sabres within 9-7. Quade then took possession behind the left post, was knocked off her feet, but on the way down slid a shot into the net to make it 9-8 with 5:31 to go in the third.\n\"I love that I have so many contributors,\" Beyer said. \"We have such a young team this year. And again, like every year, I can put my girls on offense or defense. And I can move them around and switch them back and forth. And no matter, I have contributors at every angle.\"\nSt. Mary's also appears to have a slew of contributors heading into the season, and showed that quality the remainder of the game. Marin Hodor (two goals) and Frederick (two goals) scored 10 seconds apart to give the Saints an 11-8 lead entering the fourth quarter.\nMcCall scored 38 seconds into the fourth to get the Sabres within two. But St. Mary's got two goals from Wiese and one apiece from Hodor, Grace Mitchell (two) and Maggie Marohl over the final 10:32 to pull away.\n\"It just happened our very first game was against St. Mary's and this was the best game of the season.\" Beyer said. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
