  {
    "title": "CLOSING STATEMENTS",
    "date": "2024-03-21",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "CLOSING STATEMENTS\n\n March 21, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1379\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n UPPER MARLBORO — Giuseppe Mellinger didn't storm past his coaches, chuck his headgear in a fury, or try to leave an impression on the nearest concrete wall with his fist.The opportunity to direct blame was certainly there, but the Kent Island High senior refused to point a finger.\n\"I'm not going to make excuses for myself,\" Mellinger said in the wake of a 3-2 ultimate-tiebreaker loss in the 175-pound final during the Class 2A/1A state wrestling championship tournament at the Show Place Arena on March 2. \"You try not to let refs dictate your match. Wrestling you know, you've got to be accountable. It's only you out there wrestling somebody else.\"\nOf the 16 wrestlers from North Bayside schools that qualified for states, Mellinger was the only one to reach the finals in the boys competition.\n\"Coming from Kent Island, we rarely have people that make it that far,\" said Mellinger, who became just the third Buccaneer to reach the finals during Tommy Sitar's eight seasons as head coach — joining Matt Miller (2020) and Justin Thomas (2016). \"So it was surreal to be there in that moment.\n\"Senior year. You've wrestled three years of high school,\" Mellinger added. \"Wrestled since you were like five or six years old, and then you make it to the state finals. Like wow. This is it. But I felt pretty confident. I felt relaxed. That was my last match ever so I'm going to go out there and let it fly. Like it's just one more match.\"\nOn the way to that last match, Mellinger had earned a first-round 15-13 win over Hammond's Jaden Timithy in the first sudden-victory period, took a forfeit in the quarterfinals, then defeated Middletown senior Kashton Dalhouse (47-4) via a 5-4 decision in the semifinals.\nThat set up his title shot with South Carroll sophomore Anthony Rodrigues (47-3). With 45 seconds remaining in the first period, the lead official held up two fingers, signaling a two-point takedown for Mellinger. But that initial call was waved off when the official ruled Mellinger did not to have enough control.\n\"I thought I did too,\" said Mellinger when asked if he thought he had the takedown. \"I didn't hear anybody argue against it. And the South Carroll guys, their body language was like, 'Yeah, wow. We got saved on that one.'\"\nThough he did not protest or go to the scorer's table for an explanation, Sitar also thought Mellinger had a takedown.\n\"I would have liked to see that call stand at two,\" Sitar said. \"It wasn't something where they came to the table an overturned it or anything like that, or even that the second ref came in and overturned it.\"\nIt remained scoreless through the remainder of the first. Starting on bottom, Rodrigues scored an escape just two seconds into the second period for a 1-0 lead. Sixteen seconds into the third, Mellinger escaped off bottom, to make it 1-1. Mellinger blocked a shot 14 seconds later and after two restarts, the score remained 1-1 heading into overtime.\nMellinger got two deep shots in during the 1-minute overtime, but Rodrigues wouldn't be taken down as the duel moved into the two 30-second ride-out periods. Rodrigues again escaped with only two seconds elapsed for a 2-1 lead. Mellinger answered with an escape with six seconds left in the second ride-out, forging a 2-2 deadlock heading into the ultimate tiebreaker.\nBecause he had scored the match's first point, Rodrigues got to choose top or bottom to start the final period. Rodrigues took bottom and six seconds into the final stage pulled his left leg from Mellinger's grasp for a 3-2 victory.\n\"That (takedown) would have won the championship,\" said Sitar (39-6), who was bidding to become Kent Island's first state champion since 2015 when Stevan Smith won his second straight state title. \"Whoever had a takedown was going to win because shots were so hard.\n\"I was hoping for a state title,\" Mellinger added. \"But at the end of the day I was the aggressive wrestler. As the match went on, offense got harder and harder, especially when we got sweaty. Nothing you can down now.\"\nKent Island senior Greg Couch (132 pounds) had his hopes for a state championship end in Friday's semifinal round, when he lost a 12-6 decision to defending — and eventual — state champion JoJo Gigliotti of South Carroll.\n\"I think he gave him his toughest match,\" Sitar said of Couch's battle with Gigliotti. \"I think he knew he had a match with Couch. I think had Couch been on the other side of the bracket I might have had a couple of finalists. Greg wrestled his butt off. He was peaking at the right time.\"\nCouch looked headed for the consolation bracket, when he fell behind 4-0 in the first period of his opening match to Southern Garrett's Hayden Harvey.\n\"He got taken down twice and then it was like the switch was flipped,\" Sitar said of Couch. \"He was ready to go. He was comfortable and he just let the rest of the weight class have it from there.\"\nStarting with Harvey, who would not score another point while Couch was rallying for a 13-4 major decision. Couch earned a 7-1 decision over Huntingtown senior Jett Cramer (24-5) in the quarterfinals but then lost 12-6 to Gigliotti (51-1), who pinned his other three opponents in the tournament en route to his second state title.\n\"When I got into the consi round I realized, 'This is all or nothing,'\" Couch said. \"'This is my senior year. This is all I got left.'\"\nAnd it took some doing.\nCouch and C. Milton Wright's Mitchell Nguyen (36-8) were tied 2-2 through regulation and overtime. Couch then rode Nguyen out through the first ride-out stage before working an escape in the second ride-out for a 3-2 win.\n\"That was definitely one of my hardest matches of the year, it not the hardest,\" Couch said of his bout with Nguyen.\nIn the consolation final, Couch crafted a 7-2 victory over Northern Garrett's Mason Glotfelty (39-5) for his 100th career victory and a third-place finish.\n\"I had a lot going through my mind before that last match,\" said Couch, who finished 33-9. \"I had the support of my family. My dad and my uncle were on the mat coaching for me in my corner. It definitely provided me with everything I needed for that last match.\n\"It was definitely a hard path back to try to get third,\" Couch said. \"They say second place usually looks the most upset and third is smiling a lot more, and I can definitely agree with that. Definitely makes a difference ending it on a win rather than a loss.\"\nQueen Anne's County senior Tremaine Jackson earned his 100th career win when he earned a 54-second fall in his first-round match on Thursday, but watched his state-title aspirations end Friday with a 4-2 loss to Northern Garrett's Devon Opel (38-7) in the 126-pound quarterfinals.\n\"I had the whole night (Friday) to think about what happened,\" Jackson said of his loss. \"I mean it hurt, but I knew I just had to bounce back, just start doing what I do best.\"\nJackson's road to the consolation final started Saturday morning with a 15-0 technical fall over Liberty's Alex Pearl in 3:15. He advanced to the consolation semifinals with a forfeit, then put together a 13-0 major decision over Eastern Vo Tech's Chase Ullman.\"\n\"The tech fall, the (Pearl) kid walked off the mat and he was like, 'Man, that kid is fricking good,' when he shook my hand,\" Queen Anne's head coach David Stricker said. \"We were like, 'Yeah, we know.'\n\"He could just stop putting the pressure on himself and just go be Tremaine, and pretty much show off his full talents; his full arsenal,\" Stricker said of Jackson after his quarterfinal loss. \"When he's there and he can just let it fly, there's not a whole lot of people that can stop him.\"\nThat included Rising Sun's Xzavier Brown, who faced off for a fourth time this season against Jackson in the consolation final and lost 5-2.\n\"It was close but I feel like I calculated that match,\" said Jackson, whose second consecutive third-place finish at states capped a 40-4 season. \"I wrestled him a bunch of times. And that was my last high school match, so I was coming out on top regardless.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 3,
    "day": 21,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
      "explanation": "The article details high school wrestling matches, state championship results, and athlete quotes, which are clearly sports coverage."
    },
