  {
    "title": "Easton follows great loss with victory",
    "date": "2024-04-06",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Easton follows great loss with victory\n\n April 6, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1245\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Easton High assistant baseball coach Matt Blue died Friday night after a 17-month battle with cancer.Less than 24 hours later, the Warriors were back on the field, having decided to go forward with Saturday's home game against Southern-Anne Arundel.\nAnd Blue's son Dustin wanted the ball.\n\"He wanted to pitch,\" Easton head coach Albert Pritchett said of his sophomore right-hander. \"We talked before the game. He wanted to pitch.\"\nDustin Blue pitched three-plus innings of two-hit ball before running into a jam in the fourth inning, but by then the Warriors had built a lead that was never in jeopardy en route to an emotional 11-2 victory.\n\"He wanted to play last night,\" Pritchett said of Dustin Blue. \"As soon as we knew (of Matt's passing), the kids, the players and families wanted to play. We felt that was the best way to honor Matt. Matt would have wanted us to play. I think we showed it was the right decision.\"\nMatt Blue was a three-sport athlete at Easton High before graduating in 2002. He went on to play goalkeeper for Salisbury University's soccer team before returning to teach in Talbot County. The former boys' head soccer coach at Easton High, he was diagnosed with cancer in December, 2022. Matt continued teaching at Easton Middle School, and remained an assistant on Easton's varsity baseball team until he passed Friday. Matt Blue was 40 years old.\n\"I thought the kids responded,\" Pritchett said. \"They loved Matt Blue. They loved the coach. The community has a tremendous loss. It's going to be hard to replace him. I don't think we will.\n\"So I thought the kids, in the face of a lot of emotion, a lot of feelings, and a lot of loss, they did a good job,\" Pritchett continued. \"They were here together playing the sport they love, supporting Dustin, the family. I'm very proud of them.\"\nMatt Blue gave a speech prior to last year's St. Michaels at Easton game on April 13. Before Saturday's game, the public-address announcer reminded players, coaches and the large home crowd settled behind the backstop of one of Blue's main themes of recognizing the moment.\nHis son did just that on a wind-chilled afternoon of gray clouds and big patches of blue sky.\n\"It's incredible,\" Southern head coach Kyle Lynch said of Dustin. \"The effort. Obviously the emotion. You don't know what's going through his head, but he threw tremendously. He was great. We have a lot respect for them and coach Pritchett. But what Dustin did was truly pretty special. Been around baseball for a long time and haven't seen anything like that.\"\nDustin's first pitch of the game was a strike to Thomas Martin. He got Martin on a grounder to short, then hit JC Wilson with a pitch. Easton catcher Colin Moody threw out Wilson trying to steal second then watched Dustin get a called strike three on Gavin Crandall to end the inning.\nEaston's starting pitcher issued a one-out walk to Owen Meyers in the second, induced Andrew Cook into a fielder's choice, then zipped a pickoff throw to first that led to Cook getting caught in an inning-ending rundown.\nSouthern's Brendan Hinkle was hit by a pitch to lead off the third. But Nick Youmans grounded into a fielder's choice, struck out Dylan Clarke looking before Martin hit a deep fly that Warrior left fielder Carter Mooney made a nice play on.\nWilson lined Dustin's first offering of the fourth sharply through the middle for Southern's first hit. Wilson was erased on a fielder's choice. O'Brien was then hit by a pitch before Meyers dumped a singled into shallow left, loading the bases. Cook hit into a fielder's choice to plate the Bulldogs' first run before Pritchett came out to relieve Dustin with Garrett Fink.\n\"I thought he threw great,\" Pritchett said of Dustin (2-0), who remained in the game at shortstop. \"I think (he threw) 60-something percent strikes. He commanded all three of his pitches. Again under a tremendous amount of pressure and emotion. Just showed a lot of character; lot of grit. It's a tremendous effort again in the face of tremendous loss and emotion. He's a tough kid. I'm proud of him.\"\nEaston (3-1) scored all the runs it needed during a six-run second inning — all with two outs — against Bulldogs starter Crandall (1-1). Carter Mooney began the scoring, lining a stand-up RBI double down the left-field line. After Cullen Murphy walked, Dylan Cassell roped another run-producing double to left. Murphy scored on a wild pitch. Josh Dressler (2 for 4) singled to score Cassell for a 4-0 lead. Dressler eventually scored on a wild pitch before Dustin capped the scoring with an infield, RBI single.\nThe Warriors tacked on another five runs with two outs in the third for an 11-0 bulge. Murphy singled to center off reliever Rhett Dorval and was lifted for courtesy runner Mikey O'Connor. Cassell was hit by a pitch. Dressler singled into the hole at short, plating O'Connor. CJ Dugan (2 for 4) delivered the inning's big blow with a two-run single. Dustin (2 for 4) followed with an infield single.\nFink, who escaped a bases-loaded jam in the fourth, yielded a run in the fifth, but didn't give up a hit in two innings. He was relieved with two outs in the bottom of the sixth by O'Connor, who struck out three of the five batters he faced to close the win.\n\"I think it's a healthy distraction,\" Pritchett said of playing the game. \"I think the kids needed it. They wanted it. And they proved it was a good decision for all of them. I thought their energy was high. They were positive despite the emotion.\"\nAfter the game, Pritchett handed the game-ball to Dustin.\n\"Dustin looked fabulous,\" Pritchett said.\nCOLONEL 10, NORTH DORCHESTER 5\nAMERICAN CORNER — Senior Daniel Hesson hurled a complete game Saturday morning, striking out nine, as the Colonels remained undefeated.\nDerrick Mitchell hit a two-run homer for Colonel Richardson (5-0) and finished with three RBIs. Jordan Regulski and Johnny Beaven each had two hits.\nSOFTBALL\nNORTH DORCHESTER 7, COLONEL 2\nAMERICAN CORNER — Emilee Cohee surrendered four hits, struck out 12 and issued one walk Saturday as the defending Class 1A state champions stayed unbeaten.\nCohee, Mackenzie Lewis, Maggie Hubbard and Maddie Nagel (two RBIs) each had two hits for the Eagles.\nMakayla Newcomb suffered her first loss of the season as North Dorchester totaled 11 hits. Newcomb struck out six and walked two. Aubrey Taylor led Colonel with two hits, including a double.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 8, CHESAPEAKE 5\nCENTREVILLE — Abbie Denes blasted a three-run homer in the first inning and Autumn Huber was 3 for 4 with a four-inning homer Saturday to lead the Lions.\nCaroline Taylor and Reece Brooks each had multiple-hit games for Queen Anne's. Hannah Martin earned the win, allowing just one run and four hits over six innings.\nBOYS LACROSSE\nEASTON 9, STEPHEN DECATUR 8\nBERLIN — Drew Schmidt scored the game-winner in overtime Friday night as the Warriors edged the Seahawks in a rematch of last year's Bayside Conference championship.\nSchmidt finished with a hat trick, Hudson Royer had four goals and an assist, and Tyler Rardin added two goals for Easton (1-1). Warrior goalie Jack Kilbourne made 11 saves.\nGIRLS LACROSSE\nNORTH CAROLINE 14, J.M. BENNETT 5\nSALISBURY — Claire Blue scored four goals and Riley Walstrum had a hat trick to go with three assists Friday to pace the Bulldogs.\nReece Bisesi and Ashlynn Bisesi (assist) had two goals apiece for North Caroline. Calleigh Tribbett, Ashley Kercheval and Maddi Nicholson had one goal each. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
