  },
  {
    "title": "Sabres stay hot, top Seahawks",
    "date": "2024-04-09",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Sabres stay hot, top Seahawks\n\n April 9, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 853\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Hank Wolters was comparing his team's defensive performance Tuesday afternoon to Monday's loss to Cape Henlopen of Delaware.\"Defensively I think today we just played better,\" said Saints Peter and Paul High's sophomore goalie. \"We were more focused on slides. One-on-one defense was incredible. Midfield defense was fantastic. A lot better than Cape.\"\nWolters hadn't quite finished his final sentence when J.J. Rardin, knowing his goalie had left out one glaring detail, stepped in almost as fast as one the five goals he had scored on the day.\n\"Goalie also stood on his head,\" said Rardin, bringing a smile to he and Wolters' face. \"Great day in the goal.\"\nHard to argue that considering Wolters and his defensive mates held Stephen Decatur 10 goals under its scoring average Tuesday during a 9-4 victory that gave Saints Peter & Paul its fourth win in five games.\n\"Defense was on key and Hank was on fire,\" Sabres head coach Todd Wolters said of his son's 10-save performance.\nSts. Peter and Paul's offense wasn't too shabby either, as it built a 3-1 halftime lead.\n\"We were just moving,\" Rardin said. \"We seemed to play a lot faster.\"\nThe Sabres were also playing a lot crisper.\n\"We were catching and throwing,\" Coach Wolters said. \"And we were clearing the ball. We still lost the ground-ball battle, especially on faceoffs. Again, it wasn't my faceoff man. We made it a 50-50 (ball) every single time. I don't have people that want to go in and truly come in and pick up ground balls.\"\nSts. Peter and Paul (4-3) does have people who can fill the net, and on this day, Rardin led that charge, starting 2:24 into the game. A little over 3 minutes later Noah Zimmerman found an open Andrew Steinhardt, who beat Seahawk goalie Declan Horan (six saves) one on one.\nHank Wolters and Horan each made big saves throughout the rest of the first half, but Rardin again found the back of the net with 4:16 left in the second quarter, breaking in from the right side of the goal and slipping one past Horan for a 3-0 lead.\nThe Seahawks (3-2), who came in averaging 14 goals a game — not including their 1-0 forfeit against Gunston — finally got on the board with 2:24 remaining before half on Ashton March's goal.\n\"He made some good saves,\" Decatur head coach Hoffy Hoffman said of Wolters and company who held the Seahawks to their lowest goal total of the season. \"And not to take anything away from their defense, but I think it was more what our offense wasn't doing today than necessarily what their defense was doing. Hats off to them for keeping us to four. But if we're not executing what we've been practicing all week, if we're not executing the plays, if we're not getting in the right formations from the get-go we can't be successful.\n\"I think that was apparent,\" Hoffman continued. \"They did step up on some big occasions and the goalie made some great saves. It was what it was today. It just didn't fall in our favor.\"\nLiam Durnbaugh began working on his hat trick with 3:44 left in the third quarter, when he intercepted a Decatur clearing attempt and deposited a shot past Horan. March answered with just 14 seconds left in the quarter to get the Seahawks within 4-2.\nRardin and Durnbaugh then just started unloading darts into the corners as they steadily expanded the Sabres lead. Rardin scored 52 seconds into the fourth then moved Sts. Peter & Paul to a 6-2 lead with just under 9 minutes to play.\nA Sabres defenseman inadvertently knocked the ball past Wolters while trying to scoop the ball from the crease with 7:34 to go, trimming the Seahawk deficit to 6-3. But Durnbaugh ripped a left-handed laser into the upper left corner a little over 2 minutes later. Rardin followed, weaving his way through 35 yards of traffic before parking his fourth goal into the net. Durnbaugh then sent yet another rocket into the upper left corner to make it 9-3 with 50 seconds left.\nGraham Geiser scored Decatur's final goal with 43 seconds left.\n\"Very happy,\" Todd Wolters said. \"It's the first four quarters we've put together in our first seven games. I thought we played four (quarters).\"\nBASEBALLST. MICHAELS 15 KENT COUNTY 4WORTON — Will Sherwood went 3 for 4 with two RBIs and Connor Wheeler jacked a two-run homer and finished with three RBIs as the Saints stayed undefeated.\nBrock Grow earned the win, allowing one hit and striking out five over two scoreless innings. Jacob Bealfeld (three RBIs) and Pearson Greenwood each went 2 for 3.\nSOFTBALLST. MICHAELS 12 KENT COUNTY 8WORTON — Angel Bock was 3 for 4 with a triple and three runs scored and Madi White went 2 for 4 with a pair of RBIs to lead the Saints on Tuesday.\nMyla Ramey earned the win, giving up seven runs (five earned), six walks to go with six strikeouts over six innings. Bock pitched one inning of relief, allowing one hit, one run and one walk. She struck out one.\nHaven Haddaway was 2 for 3 with two RBIs for St. Michaels. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 9,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
