  {
    "title": "Saints top Eagles, stay unbeaten",
    "date": "2024-04-10",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Saints top Eagles, stay unbeaten\n\n April 10, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 879\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n ST. MICHAELS — St. Michaels High head baseball coach Brian Femi has always been known for the small things.And the small things again helped produce a victory Wednesday afternoon.\nBut the Saints also got a big pitching performance from Will Sherwood and a huge throw from center fielder Connor Wheeler that cut down a run at plate while helping St. Michaels remain unbeaten with a 4-1 victory over North Dorchester.\n\"Anytime you don't walk anybody you're going to be in a ballgame,\" Femi said. \"Their pitcher (Wes Windsor) held us in check and we made a couple errors. And because Will pitched so well, it just kept us in a good position to win the game.\"\nA junior right-hander, Sherwood (2-0) was touched for seven hits, but did not walk a batter and struck out 12 over six innings, mixing fastballs and curves.\n\"Coach was calling a lot of curveballs,\" Sherwood said of assistant Zach Wallace. \"I was getting 'em over and they were swinging and missing at the ones that were low that he called. And locating the fastball was really nice today.\"\nNorth Dorchester (4-2 overall, 0-1 North Bayside) took a 1-0 lead in the second inning. Nate Swann reached on an error to lead off the frame. Sherwood got a called strike three on Vernon Price, but Manny Reyes singled Swann to third, and Chance Blake singled down the left-field line to plate Swann. Sherwood ended the rally there, striking out the next two batters.\n\"He's picked up a little more velocity, but I've noticed he's got a lot more poise to him now,\" Femi said of Sherwood. \"And I think it comes with experience. And he doesn't get rattled.\"\nSherwood could have gotten rattled in the fourth, when the Eagles' first two hitters got on. Instead, he struck out the next two batters before getting an inning-ending groundout.\nThe Saints (6-0, 1-0) took a 2-1 lead in the home fourth, but North Dorchester pieced together a two-out rally in the fifth, when Nate Fraccaroli reached on an error and Windsor dumped a single into center field. Swann followed by lacing a single up the middle that Wheeler got to quickly before unloading a strike to catcher Henry Mullikin, who tagged out Fraccaroli trying to score from second.\n\"I thought North Dorchester actually had the momentum in the early parts of the game until that throw,\" Femi said. \"I think it just changed the whole complexion of it; took the wind out of their sails. And I think it gave us a little bit of an offensive boost once that happened.\n\"That ball was a little bit in the gap,\" Femi continued. \"It wasn't like it was right to him. So he had to cut it off and then just made a throw right on the money. And that's a hard play for a catcher to make. People don't know. You got a guy barreling down on you. He still has to slide, but that's as bang bang as you can get.\"\nVernon Price hit a leadoff single in the sixth but Sherwood retired the next three batters to end his day. Anthony Massaro came on to pitch the seventh, allowing one hit.\n\"Pitchers did a great job today,\" North Dorchester head coach Dave Morrissette said. \"Both sides. Will did a great job. Kept us off balance with that curveball a little bit, early especially. We hit some balls hard, but we got to score some guys once we get 'em on too.\"\nSt. Michaels struggled for offense, totaling six hits against Windsor, but scratched two runs across in the fourth inning.\nJacob Bealefeld worked a leadoff walk. Mullikin singled to right field. Pearson Greenwood's groundout to second moved Bealefeld to third. Pinch-hitter Eli Wallace then dropped a bunt to score Bealefield with the tying run.\nWindsor hit Ethan Royer with a pitch. Royer stole second, broke for third and got caught in a rundown, allowing Mullikin to score from third for a 2-1 St. Michaels lead.\nThe Saints tacked on a run in the fifth. Hunter Gottleib reached on a bunt single and stole second. Wheeler hit a hard comebacker that Windsor made a nice play on before throwing to first for the out. Gottleib moved to third on the play and looked like he might get stranded, as Windsor retired Sherwood, then walked Bealefield. But Bealefeld broke for second, drawing a throw from Windsor. Gottleib raced home for a 3-1 lead.\n\"We bunt the ball and just try to put some balls in play,\" Femi said. \"We had a lot of at-bats where we were striking out and just didn't put balls in play. He (Windsor) competes. I can't say enough about him and his catcher (TJ Leonard). We just had to do what we had to do not so much because of us, but because of them. I thought they played a really good game.\"\nSt. Michaels added a run in the sixth. Greenwood singled off the glove of the leaping Fraccaroli at second. Bobby Fink moved Greenwood to second with a sacrifice bunt. Two batters later, Gottleib popped up a bunt that Windsor made a lunging attempt to catch, but couldn't come up with the ball, allowing Greenwood to score from third.\nNotes: Wheeler hit a two-out triple in the third but was stranded when Windsor got an inning-ending groundout. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
