  {
    "title": "Colonel strikes early, hangs on late",
    "date": "2024-04-11",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Colonel strikes early, hangs on late\n\n April 11, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1096\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Perhaps one of the biggest reasons behind Daniel Hesson's success on the mound the past three seasons has been his practical approach.\"I felt like I had decent stuff,\" Colonel Richardson High's senior left-hander said. \"Missed a couple locations. But you know, that's baseball. Sometimes you miss a spot, sometimes you put one where you want to put it. And other stuff happens. There's not much you can do about it.\"\nHesson tried to do as much as he could Thursday afternoon, limiting Easton to four runs over six-plus innings before the Warriors pieced together a late rally that fell short in an 11-8 loss to the Colonels.\n\"We got a saying: 'Winners find a way,'\" Colonel first-year head coach Garrett Nepert said after his team's fourth straight win. \"We found a way. It wasn't pretty, but winners find a way. So that's what we're kind of going with to make ourselves feel better today.\"\nThe Colonels (8-1 overall, 2-0 North Bayside) were feeling pretty good about themselves after sending 13 batters to the plate in a nine-run first inning that featured eight walks, Jordan Regulski's three-run double, and Hesson's RBI two-bagger.\nHesson (2-1) yielded three runs and six hits over 6 1/3 innings, striking out five and walking two. And though he surrendered Dustin Blue's leadoff homer to left-center field in the second inning, and Mikey O'Connor's two-out, two-run double in the fourth, he avoided the big inning for the most part.\n\"I was hitting locations early,\" Hesson said. \"Missed a couple spots early. But really just movement on the changeup and everything like that was working well today.\"\nO'Connor (0-1), Easton's starting pitcher, didn't get out of the first inning and was relieved by Pax Echternach. The Warriors' first reliever faced two men, including Regulski (2 for 3) who hit his bases-clearing double. Echternach was relieved by Cullen Murphy, who quieted the Colonels until the fourth, when Gavin Whitby (2 for 2) hit an RBI single and courtesy runner EJ Jones scored on a pop-out to first.\nDown 11-3, Easton (4-4, 0-1), playing its fifth game in six days, had a potential rally going in the fifth, when Finn McGuckin and Dylan Cassell (2 for 3, RBI) led off with back-to-back singles. That rally was squelched though, when Josh Dressler hit a rope to short that Regulski lunged forward to catch. Colonel's shortstop, who has verbally committed to Wake Forest, got to his feet, stepped on second and threw to first to complete a triple play.\nBut Easton wasn't quite finished.\n\"We felt very comfortable today and that kind of came back and bit us,\" Hesson said. \"They made it close in the very end. We got too comfortable. We have stuff to work on as a team. Me as well. This isn't our final form. We just got to get better as a team.\"\nHesson was relieved after allowing a one-out single to Carter Mooney in the home seventh. Reliever Jack Walls walked the first three batters he faced before Kayden Webb stung a two-run single to center, drawing Easton within 11-6. CJ Dugan's sacrifice fly trimmed Colonel's lead to 11-7. Blue walked before Colin Mooney singled to score Webb to make it 11-8. But Walls extinguished the rally there, getting a called third strike on O'Connor to end the game.\n\"At the end we didn't play our best baseball,\" Nepert said. \"We have to continue to coach no matter what the lead is. Early on we kind of stuck to a good approach. Kind of went away from that later on in the game. But when you get a nine-run lead you get a little bit of a cushion which is a great thing.\"\nThough his team's pitching was thin, Easton head coach Albert Pritchett liked how his team battled despite the early deficit.\n\"We probably had a few too many walks,\" said Pritchett, whose team issued 13. \"But like the team we've always had in Easton, we never gave up. We had a hole. Cullen Murphy and Al Rhodes came in and held down things, and our offense chipped away. We got Hesson out of the game and got to the next pitcher and scored a few runs to make it competitive. Had a couple extra errors I would have liked to have got ridden of. But we're hitting the ball. I don't think this team thinks it's ever over.\"\nST. MICHAELS 16 KENT ISLAND 8\nSTEVENSVILLE — Jacob Bealefeld (2 for 2) homered, Connor Wheeler (2 for 4) doubled, and Will Sherwood (2 for 4) stole three bases as the Saints remained undefeated.\nAnthony Massaro earned the win, allowing four runs (two earned) and eight hits over four innings. Brock Grow pitched three innings for the save.\nGrow also went 2 for 2 with a pair of walks, and Bobby Fink has an two-run double for St. Michaels (7-0).\nSOFTBALL\nST. MICHAELS 8 KENT ISLAND 7\nSTEVENSVILLE — Angel Bock picked up the win, allowing seven runs on 10 hits over seven innings Thursday to lead the Saints.\nBock, who struck out seven and walked just one, was 2 for 4 at the plate. Katie Jenkins also went 2 for 4 and Myla Ramey, Mercy Haddaway and Brianna Crow each had hits. Madi White drove in a pair of runs.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 7 NORTH CAROLINE 6\nCENTREVILLE — Autumn Huber drove in a pair of runs and Caroline Taylor went 3 for 4 Thursday as the Lions rallied from a five-run deficit to edge the Bulldogs.\nEmily Hirschbock earned the win, allowing one hit and one run over 4 2/3 innings.\nQueen Anne's scored five runs on four hits in the bottom of the third inning, with Mikayla Livingston and Emilie Smith hitting RBI singles.\nBOYS LACROSSE\nEASTON 16 CAMBRIDGE-SD 5\nEASTON — Drew Schmidt and Tyler Rardin each had three goals and two assists Wednesday to lead the Warriors.\nCampbell Taliaferro and Jackson Wieland had two goals and an assist apiece for Easton (2-1) and Charlie Kaminskas had a pair of goals. Hudson Royer and Sabin Long finished with one goal and one assist each, and Logan Friend and Luke Stanco netted one goal apiece. Easton's Angel Fernandez won five faceoffs and goalie Jack Kilbourne made three saves.\nGIRLS LACROSSE\nSSPP 24 SALISBURY SCHOOL 6\nEASTON — Senior Morgan Quade totaled five goals and seven assists, and freshman Emery Zimmerman finished with four goals and three assists Wednesday as the Sabres improved to 5-1.\nCaroline Ewing had four goals and one assist for Saints Peter and Paul, and Eryn Callahan (four assists), Kendall Kroniser (two assists), Avery McCall (assist) and Eden Zimmerman each had two goals. Emma Hainey, Samantha Murphy and Julia Spero added one goal apiece for the Sabres. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
