  {
    "title": "Colonels upend unbeaten Queen Anne's",
    "date": "2024-04-16",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Colonels upend unbeaten Queen Anne's\n\n April 16, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 953\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Makayla Newcomb has pitched well this season.But Colonel Richardson High's softball team either couldn't deliver the big hit or make the big play defensively when it was needed.\nTuesday, the Colonels got both.\nAubrey Taylor ripped a two-run triple down the right-field line and Newcomb crafted a complete-game five-hitter, as the Colonels defeated Queen Anne's County, 4-1, dealing the Lions their first loss of the season.\n\"She's had a good year for us,\" Colonel Richardson head coach Brett Ireland said of Newcomb (7-4), who finished with 10 strikeouts and no walks. \"All year we just couldn't make enough plays or one thing would go wrong and it sort of snowballed. But we got the clutch hit today. We've struggled with the runners in scoring position.\"\nColonel (7-4 overall, 2-2 North Bayside) took a 1-0 lead in the first inning, as Kylie Rhinehardt, Clarissa Peugh, Eileen Johnson and Tyla Dickerson each singled off Hannah Martin (6-1), with Dickerson's hit to center plating Rhinehardt.\nMartin though prevented the big inning, retiring the next three batters to end the inning, then setting down the side in order in the second.\nMartin issued her only walk of the game to Peugh to lead off the third. Johnson (2 for 3) singled to left. Peugh was forced at third when Dickerson bounced into a fielder's choice. Taylor (2 for 3) then laced a 1-0 offering down the right-field line, scoring Johnson and Dickerson. Taylor raced home on an overthrow to third.\n\"Mostly I was just looking for a hit to the right side because I knew it would score both the runs on first and second,\" Taylor said.\nThat's exactly what her coach was looking for as well.\n\"Aubrey stepped up today and gave us a big hit,\" Ireland said. \"Runners on first and second, she hit a triple down the line. I was hoping someone would hit it down that far just because I could see the right fielder was in right-center a lot. That (right-field) line was open and once it got there both runs were able to score. And Aubrey made good hustle to third. And then there was an overthrow and (she) was able to get in there and score. That really gave us nice comfortable lead. I was wishing we could tack on more but we just couldn't do it.\"\nMartin yielded only one more hit the rest of the way, retiring 13 of the final 15 batters she faced.\n\"I think Hannah pitched extremely well,\" Queen Anne's head coach Aaron Stewart said, whose pitcher allowed seven hits and struck out seven. \"Four runs. Our average is eight, nine runs. We score eight, nine runs — we had that one bad inning — we win the game.\"\nNewcomb held the Lions (9-1, 2-1) to their lowest run total of the season and retired the first nine batters she faced before Queen Anne's scored its lone run in the fourth.\n\"I had a little bit of pressure, but I just go on top of the hitters,\" Newcomb said. \"Once I got three-up, three-down on the whole lineup the first time (I was good). My curveball was good. And just the fastball, spotting it.\"\nNewcomb recorded five of her 10 strikeouts through the first three innings.\nThat streak ended in the fourth, when Cam Jacobi drove a leadoff double to the fence in left field. Caroline Taylor then hit a line drive that Peugh made a diving stop on at second base. Autumn Huber followed with another double to the left-field fence, scoring Jacobi to make it 4-1.\nBut Newcomb got Abbie Denes and Martin to fly out to end the inning.\n\"Makayla threw a great game today,\" Ireland said. \"She hit her spots well. We know Queen Anne's has some good sticks and I thought she was able to throw her off-speed pitch for strikes and got a couple of big outs with that. I thought the second and third time through the lineup, the changeup was good enough to get us some outs. \"\nQueen Anne's threatened again in the fifth.\nEmily Rendulic stroked a single just over the outstretched glove of Taylor at short. Newcomb got a called third strike on Morgan Gottleib. Emilie Smith then doubled into the left-field corner, moving Rendulic to third. But Newcomb stifled the rally there, notching a strikeout and before inducing an inning-ending pop-up.\n\"We couldn't hit the ball today,\" Stewart said. \"That's what it pretty much came down to. We've hit the ball extremely, extremely well all year long and we just couldn't get the hit today. Looked like she (Newcomb) was throwing the good stuff and we couldn't hit the ball. We normally put up eight, nine runs every game and one run isn't going to win you games. But we move on.\"\nNORTH DORCHESTER 4, EASTON 1EASTON — Emilee Cohee hurled a two-hitter, striking out 14 and walking none, as the Eagles took over sole possession of first place in the North Bayside.\nMackenzie Lewis and freshmen Kinsley Peters and Journey Pearson each had two hits for the Eagles (9-0, 3-0), who ended Easton's three-game win streak.\nBASEBALLST. MICHAELS 10, CAMBRIDGE-SD 0CAMBRIDGE — Connor Wheeler went 3 for 3 with a double and a stolen base, and Jacob Bealefeld tossed a three-hitter, striking out six and walking one, as the Saints blanked the Vikings in five innings.\nBealefeld went 2 for 3 with a double and two runs scored, and Henry Mullikin (2 for 3) doubled and drove in two runs for St. Michaels (9-0, 4-0).\nCOLONEL 5, QUEEN ANNE'S 3CENTREVILLE — Daniel Hesson pitched a complete game, striking out six and yielding no earned runs as Colonel (10-1, 3-0) handed the Lions (7-2, 2-1) their first North Bayside loss.\nJack Walls and Hesson each had two hits for Colonel Richardson. Gavin Whitby added a hit. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 16,
