  {
    "title": "Easton continues new approach, tops Saints",
    "date": "2024-04-16",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Easton continues new approach, tops Saints\n\n April 16, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1079\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n ST. MICHAELS — The rough start to the season was one thing.Not making contact though may have troubled Ryan Brice even more. So Easton High's first-year head softball coach tried a different approach.\n\"I told them, 'We're not going to have any more double-digit strikeout games,'\" Brice said. \"I told them before each game we're going to line up in the on-deck circle and time the pitcher. I'm not sure, but I think today we only had one strikeout. So I'm going to keep on going with that approach.\"\nThat approach certainly worked Monday, as the Warriors churned out 17 hits, including four doubles, on the way to a 14-3 six-inning victory over St. Michaels that extended their win streak to three.\n\"It's very hard to beat a team like St. Michaels like this,\" Brice said of a Saints team that entered the game having won three of four, its lone loss coming to defending Class 1A state champion North Dorchester. \"They are very well coached. They're very aggressive. They're a good team from top to bottom. So for us to come here and do what we did is huge.\"\nEaston (4-3 overall, 3-0 North Bayside) pushed four runs across in the first inning. Mayah Wilkins (4 for 5) and Lyla Roe (3 for 5) reached on back-to-back singles. Olivia Orrell and Sammy Townsend followed with consecutive run-producing groundouts.\nSt. Michaels starting pitcher Angel Bock (2-3) looked like she might escape further damage when she worked the count to 1-2 on Laillah Janes. But Janes (2 for 5) singled to right and Gemma Vogel reached on an error before Brynlee Buchkoski (2 for 4) doubled down the left-field line to plate Janes and Vogel for a 4-0 lead.\nMyla Ramey relieved Bock to start the second, but Easton stretched its lead to 6-0, scoring two runs on a throwing error.\n\"Just an off-day all around,\" St. Michaels head coach David Fisher said after his team slipped to 4-4, 1-2). \"I really felt like we were focused and we were ready. We just didn't click. Couple mental errors. It wasn't even the Easton-St. Michaels hype. It was just lack of focus. And then all of a sudden pitching change and we got down early and just didn't pull ourself out.\"\nEaston expanded its lead in the fourth, cranking out five runs on seven hits. Roe reached on an error and scored on Orrell's double down the left-field line to make it 7-0. After Townsend singled, Janes and Vogel delivered consecutive RBI singles for a 9-0 cushion. Isabelle Ludwig added an RBI single before Roe hit a ball deep in the hole at short for an RBI infield hit and an 11-0 lead.\nRoe (2-1) had a shutout through four innings, but issued back-to-back walks to Bri Grow and Ramey to open the bottom of the fifth. Roe was relieved by Townsend, who got Katie Jenkins to bounce into a fielder's choice. But Madi White (2 for 3) singled to center, scoring Ramey. Bock followed with a single to right that plated Jenkins before White scored on Lola Browning's sacrifice fly to left, drawing the Saints within 11-3. Townsend stopped the threat there, inducing Brooke Reilly into an inning-ending groundout.\n\"She pitched a great game,\" Brice said of Roe, who allowed two runs, three hits, three walks and struck out three over four innings. \"I call her a dog for a reason. She's a tough one. Lyla Roe did an amazing job today.\"\nThe Warriors regained their 11-run edge in the sixth. Leigha Bosley lined a leadoff double to left. After a Wilkins single, Roe singled to right, scoring Bosley. Wilkins scored on a wild pitch before Townsend's groundout to short plated Roe for a 14-3 lead.\nMercy Haddaway reached on an infield hit to open the Saints' sixth, but Townsend recorded a strikeout before getting a pair of groundouts to second to end the game.\nCOLONEL 6 CAMBRIDGE-SD 1AMERICAN CORNER — Makayla Newcomb pitched a one-hitter, striking out 11 and walking just one, as the Colonels ended a four-game skid.\nNewcomb also had a two-run single for Colonel Richardson (6-4). Montana Davis hit a two-run triple, Sadie Prettyman belted an RBI triple, and Aubrey Taylor cracked a double.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 16 KENT COUNTY 0WORTON — Emily Hirschbock had three hits, including a two-run triple and a two-run double as the Lions remained unbeaten.\nHirschbock also earned the win, pitching five innings of three-hit shutout ball. She struck out seven and walked two.\nCamaryn Jacobi had three hits for Queen Anne's, and Caroline Taylor and Emily Rendulic had multiple hit games.\nBASEBALLST. MICHAELS 3 EASTON 2ST. MICHAELS — Senior left-hander Jacob Seek allowed no earned runs and five hits over 6 2/3 innings to help the Saints remain unbeaten on Monday.\nSeek struck out 12 and walked one. Will Sherwood earned the save.\nAnthony Massaro was 2 for 3 with a two-run double, and Jacob Bealefeld was 1 for 2 with an RBI and a run scored.\nNORTH DORCHESTER 8 KENT ISLAND 5SHILOH — Nate Swann was 2 for 3 with two RBIs and a run scored, and Wes Windsor reached base three times which included an RBI double to fuel the Eagles.\nVernon Price had an RBI double and TJ Leonard and Blake Bramble each scored twice. Boyle also got the save.\nCOLONEL 10 CAMBRIDGE-SD 0AMERICAN CORNER — Kenton Robbins yielded one hit over 3 2/3 innings, striking out four, as the Colonels blanked the Vikings.\nJack Walls had two hits for Colonel Richardson, and Jordan Regulski, Caleb Rouser, Manny Wallace, Derrick Mitchell and Preston Rickwood each had one hit.\nGIRLS LACROSSENORTH CAROLINE 20 EASTON 11RIDGELY — Riley Walstrum (two assists), Reece Bisesi and Claire Blue each had four goals Monday to lead the Bulldogs.\nAshley Kercheval netted a hat trick for North Caroline (3-1) and Ashlynn Bisesi had two goals and two assists. Gracie Faulkner, Calleigh Tribbett and Maddie McNinch had one goal each, and Maddie Nicholson had an assist.\nSSPP 21, PALLOTTI 4EASTON — Senior Morgan Quade dished nine assists and scored four goals, and sophomore Eden Zimmerman had four goals and two assists to pace the Sabres.\nFreshman Emery Zimmerman added a hat trick an two assists for Saints Peter an Paul, and Caroline Ewing and Kendall Kroniser had two goals and two assists apiece. Emma Hainey scored twice, and Eryn Callahan (assist), Avery McCall (assist), Kate Sweeney and Julia Spero had one goal each.\nGUNSTON 25 INDIAN RIVER 21CENTREVILLE — Sophomore Layla Kent had 12 goals and freshman goalie Sarah Porter made 12 saves to lead the Herons to their third straight win Monday. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
