  {
    "title": "Mardela blanks ND in clash of unbeatens",
    "date": "2024-04-18",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Mardela blanks ND in clash of unbeatens\n\n April 18, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 691\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n SHILOH — This was act one in a potential three-part series.If acts two and three do materialize, North Dorchester High head softball coach Carol Hubbard hopes they go a lot better than this one.\nMardela cranked out 14 hits and sophomore pitcher Hayden Adkins hurled a complete-game one-hitter for her sixth shutout of the season Thursday, as the Warriors rolled to a 10-0 victory over the Eagles in a battle of unbeatens.\n\"Definitely an off-day,\" Hubbard said after the Eagles committed an uncharacteristic six errors. \"We knew going in we're both tough teams. We both have strong pitching. We both typically put the bat on the ball. Tonight they outplayed us. No other way to say it. We had errors in the field. We didn't put the bat on the ball nearly as well as we're capable of. It just wasn't our day.\"\nMardela (12-0) took a 2-0 lead in the first inning against Eagles ace Emilee Cohee (5-1). Lexie Guy led off by blooping a single into center field. Julia Wisk advanced her with a sacrifice bunt. Camryn Dorr reached on an error that pushed Guy to third. Ava Twilley hit a comebacker to the mound that Cohee fielded. Knowing she didn't have time to get Guy at the plate, Cohee wisely threw for first for the second out. Karsyn Shockley then smacked a 2-2 pitch to the right-field fence for a double, plating Dorr. Cohee got Elana Donalds to ground out to second, ending the inning.\nCohee retired the first two batters of the second inning. Samantha Witte then hit a towering pop-up that landed between second baseman Emily Boyle and right fielder Journey Pearson. Witte raced to second on the play, stole third and scored on Guy's single to left field for a 3-0 lead.\n\"Eight of our nine in the lineup are batting .390 or above,\" said Mardela head coach Kory Shiles, whose team defeated North Dorchester in last year's Bayside Conference championship — the last team to defeat the defending Class 1A state champion Eagles. \"We have five\" — Twilley, Wisk, Dorr, Donalds, Abby Campbell — \"that are batting over .500. That to go with our phenomenal pitching ...\"\nPhenomenal indeed.\nComing into Thursday's game the Warriors had yielded just two runs in regular-season play — that to Colonel Richardson. Adkins (7-0) struck out eight and walked two. Then there's Twilley who has only given up one hit all season.\n\"Mardela played a great game,\" Hubbard said. \"They made the plays when we put the ball in play. And Hayden pitched a great game.\"\nNorth Dorchester got its only hit in the third, when Mackenzie Lewis beat out a spinning infield single up the third-base line. Cohee followed with a walk, but Adkins squelched the rally there, getting Kinsley Peters to ground out to short.\nThe closest the Eagles came to a second hit came in the fourth when Maggie Hubbard scorched a hard grounder went off first baseman Shockley that Shiles called an error. Adkins retired 11 of the final 12 batters she faced, surrendering a leadofff walk to Maddie Nagel in the seventh. Nagel, who was hit on the ankle with ball four, was lifted for a courtesy runner, who was thrown out trying to steal second.\nMardela extended its lead to 4-0 in the fourth. Guy and Wisk hit back-to-back two-out singles before Dorr singled to right, scoring Guy.\nThe Warriors put together another two-out rally in the fifth, but Cohee got a called third strike on Witte to end the inning.\n\"I thought Emilee pitched well,\" Hubbard said, whose starter gave up 11 hits, the last, an RBI double by Dorr that pushed Mardela's lead to 5-0 in the sixth and ended Cohee's day on the mound. \"She gave up one walk. Not a bad performance. Can't ask a whole lot more out of your pitcher than that.\"\nTwilley greeted reliever Jewels Vroman with an RBI single. The Warriors tacked on two more runs — both scoring on errors.\n\"I got a feeling we'll see them one more time, if not two more times,\" Shiles said of a potential rematch with the Eagles in the Bayside Conference championship and the Class 1A East Region I final. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 18,
