  },
  {
    "title": "Lions closer to finish line",
    "date": "2024-04-23",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Lions closer to finish line\n\n April 23, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 590\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — One word has been on the collective minds of Queen Anne's County High's softball team all season. Finish.\"Our motto has been finish all year long,\" Lions head coach Aaron Stewart said. \"We didn't finish last year. Coming into the season this year, one of my coaches just came up to me and said, 'You know the word of the year has to be finish.' And we're finishing right now.\"\nQueen Anne's took a big step in putting itself in a position to finish atop the North Bayside Tuesday afternoon, using a nine-hit attack to back the pitching of Emily Hirschbock in a 6-1 victory over defending division champion North Dorchester.\nThe Lions (12-1 overall) moved into first place in the North at 4-1 and now hold the tiebreaker over the Eagles (11-2, 4-1) after beating them in their only head-to-head meeting of the season. Colonel Richardson (10-4, 4-2), which defeated Kent County by forfeit Tuesday, North Dorchester and Easton (6-4, 4-1) remain in contention but need help to overtake the Lions.\n\"The bats definitely came back today,\" said Stewart, who was puzzled after his team was limited to just five hits in last Tuesday's 4-1 loss to Colonel Richardson. \"We hit the ball well. Opposite field, which is even better. I like to see opposite-field hitting. We practice that over and over and over again, to take that outside pitch and drive it to right field. And we executed it today.\"\nThe Lions did just that in the third inning, breaking a scoreless tie with three consecutive two-out, opposite-field hits for a 3-0 lead.\nMorgan Gottleib worked a one-out walk against North Dorchester ace Emilee Cohee (5-2) and was advanced to second on Emily Hirschbock's sacrifice bunt. Cam Jacobi lined a single into right field to score Gottleib. Caroline Taylor (3 for 4) drilled a 1-0 offering into the right field corner for a double. Autumn Huber then drove another Cohee delivery the other way for a two-run triple.\nNorth Dorchester scratched a run across in the top of the fourth. Maggie Hubbard (2 for 3) hit a leadoff single and scored four batters later, when freshman Journey Pearson lined a shot to center that Jacobi just missed making a spectacular leaping catch on for the third out of the inning.\nThat would be the lone run allowed by Hirschbock (7-0) who finished with a complete-game five-hitter, striking out four and walking four.\n\"I was just kind of thinking to attack every pitch,\" said Hirschbock, who retired nine of the last 11 batters she faced. \"It's been a big thing for me to attack specifically the first batter. I feel like I did that pretty well tonight. I'm proud of myself for that.\n\"It's always nice pitching with the lead,\" Hirschbock said with a big smile. \"My hitters hit and it was awesome.\"\nQueen Anne's extended its lead in the fifth. Jacobi reached on a one-out error. Taylor dropped a bunt that Cohee fielded, but had to hold onto with no one covering first. Huber walked to load the bases. Abbie Denes singled to left field to plate Jacobi for a 4-1 lead. Cohee notched her seventh and final strikeout before Emily Rendulic singled to score Taylor and Huber for a 6-1 cushion.\n\"Tough day,\" North Dorchester head coach Carol Hubbard said. \"They out-hit us. We had a few unfortunate things on the base paths. Not making excuses. They made the plays they had to make.\"\nMaddie Nagel relieved Cohee, got an inning-ending strikeout, then held the Lions scoreless in the sixth despite yielding singles to Jacobi and Taylor. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 23,
