  {
    "title": "RIGHT IN THE MIX",
    "date": "2024-04-25",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "RIGHT IN THE MIX\n\n April 25, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 925\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — The North Bayside softball championship.Defending division and Class 1A state champion North Dorchester was certainly cast among the favorites at the beginning of this season. Queen Anne's County and Colonel Richardson also figured to be right at the top.\nEaston?\nAfter a 1-3 start, some may have brushed the Warriors to the side, thinking this was a season of rebuilding, or at least readjusting under first-year head coach Ryan Brice. Well, Brice's bunch showed again Thursday afternoon they have adjusted just fine, notching their seventh win in eight games via a 10-0 five-inning shutout of Cambridge-South Dorchester.\nEaston (8-4 overall) improved to 5-1 in the North, tied with Queen Anne's (13-1, 5-1) and North Dorchester (12-2, 5-1) — though the Eagles hold the tiebreaker over the Warriors, having won the only meeting between the two teams. Queen Anne's plays Easton on Tuesday.\n\"We're on a roll,\" Brice said. \"Hopefully we can keep this going until next week because we have three straight (Queen Anne's, James M. Bennett, North Caroline), Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday.\"\nBrice is also hoping Sammy Townsend can keep it going. With Lyle Roe not pitching this week due to an injury, Townsend (7-2) started all three games in the circle for the Warriors. The sophomore punctuated her week by throwing Easton's first shutout of the season, a three-hit gem that including 11 strikeouts — eight swinging — and no walks.\n\"Sammy did an amazing job, not only today, but the past three games that we had,\" Brice said. \"Three-and-oh. She pitched amazing today with a shutout. I can't say enough about her. She's one of the best pitchers I've ever had the pleasure of coaching.\"\nTownsend gave up a game-opening single to Enazajah Young, who raced to second when the ball bounced and hit center fielder Leigha Bosley in the throat. Townsend promptly struck out the next three, leaving Young, who stole third, stranded at third.\nCamryn Russum singled sharply to left field with one out in the second, but was thrown out stealing by Warrior catcher Laillah Janes. Townsend fanned Evelyn Windsor to end the inning.\n\"I felt like my spins were really tight today, and the past couple of games,\" Townsend said. \"I felt like this week I've pitched the best that I've pitched all season.\"\nTownsend set the Vikings (6-7, 1-5) down in order in the third before Laila Insley reached with a bunt single down the third-base line to open the fourth. Townsend got Emma Robinson to pop out to short, watched Lainey Jones move Insley to second with a sacrifice bunt before striking out M'Kya Molock to end the frame.\nThe right-hander fanned the first two batters she faced in the fifth before Keyth Rodriguez smacked a comebacker off Townsend's glove. But Townsend finished the mercy-shortened game with her fourth inning-ending strikeout of the game.\n\"I knew my defense had me,\" Townsend said. \"I feel like we have a set infield and outfield now, because I know in the beginning of the year we were all wishy-washy with who we were going to put where.\"\nThe Warriors took a 1-0 lead in the bottom of the first, when Mayah Wilkins (2 for 3) reached on an infield hit, dashed to third on a two-base throwing error on the play, and scored on a wild pitch.\nBack-to-back, bases-loaded walks with two outs in the second extended that lead to 3-0.\nEaston widened its advantage to 5-0 in the third. Townsend (2 for 3, two RBIs) roped a leadoff double into the left-field corner and scored on an error. Two batters later, Brynlee Buchkoski laced a triple to right that plated Janes. Buchkoski tried to score on a passed ball, but Viking catcher Lainey Jones threw to pitcher Robinson, who tagged out Buchkoski for the inning's final out.\nThe Warriors' lead ballooned to 10-0 in the fourth. After Bosley reached on an error, Wilkins doubled to left. Three batters later, Townsend lined a two-out, two-run single to right for a 7-0 lead. Janes and Gemma Vogel (2 for 3) followed with consecutive RBI singles. Robinson was relieved by Windsor, who gave up a bases-loaded walk to Isabelle Ludwig before getting two fly-ball outs to end the game.\nNORTH DORCHESTER 9 NORTH CAROLINE 0\nSHILOH — Senior Emilee Cohee hurled a no-hitter, striking out 10, as the Eagles improved to 12-2, 5-1. Freshmen Kinsley Peters and Journey Pearson each had two hits for North Dorchester.\nBASEBALL\nST. MICHAELS 4 KENT COUNTY 2\nST. MICHAELS — Brock Grow tossed three innings of scoreless relief to earn the win, striking out seven of the nine batters he faced, as the Saints bounced back from Wednesday's 6-5 loss to Colonel Richardson.\nEli Wallace started for the Saints (14-1, 7-0), allowing two hits and one earned run. Jacob Bealefeld went 2 for 2 with a double and two RBIs, and Pearson Greenwood and Wallace each had a run-producing hit.\nCOLONEL 6 ST. MICHAELS 5\nST. MICHAELS — Jordan Regulski struck out seven over five innings and Jack Walls fanned six in two innings Wednesday, as Colonel Richardson handed the Saints their first loss of the season.\nManny Wallace and Regulski each had two hits for the Colonels. Walls, Brayden Dadds, Gavin Whitby and John Beaven had one each.\nBOYS LACROSSE\nEASTON 17, WICOMICO 2\nEASTON — Jackson Weiland and Blake Royer each scored four goals Wednesday to lead the Warriors.\nLogan Friend had a hat trick for Easton, Sam Noble scored twice, and Hudson Royer, Kyle Dyott, Landon Chaney and Grayson Buritsch had one apiece. Angel Fernandez won 12 faceoffs, and the defense of Jackson McKay, Nathan Lapp, Darren Foxwell, Colin Keenan and Charlie Beaufait limited the Indians to two goals. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 4,
    "day": 25,
