  {
    "title": "Easton extends streak, but Eagles win title",
    "date": "2024-05-02",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Easton extends streak, but Eagles win title\n\n May 2, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1119\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Prior to the beginning of this season, Easton High first-year head softball coach Ryan Brice made a calendar.\"I just had us going to the Bayside championship game and states, 'cause I told them I believed in them that much, that they could do anything that they put their mind to,\" Brice said.\nThe Warriors continued their push toward turning Brice's calendar into reality Thursday, grinding out a 4-3 victory over North Caroline for their 10th win in 11 games. But Easton's title bid ended when North Dorchester earned a 5-1 victory over Colonel Richardson yesterday to clinch its second consecutive North Bayside championship.\nThe defending Class 1A state-champion Eagles (15-2 overall, 7-1 North Bayside) will meet unbeaten Mardela in a rematch of last year's conference championship on Tuesday at 5 p.m. at Salisbury University.\n\"We're doing what we need to do,\" Brice said after Easton improved to 11-4, 7-1 North Bayside, its lone division loss coming to North Dorchester on April 16.\nEaston did just that on this sunny, wind-swept afternoon against a North Caroline team (8-8, 4-4) that threatened to end the Warriors' six-game win streak.\n\"They battled,\" North Caroline head coach Lee Hutchison said. \"They played a good game. Both sides. We had a couple calls go against us there at the end that could have gone both ways. We get those calls and it's a tie game.\"\nTied 2-2, Isabelle Ludwig singled up the middle with one out in the Easton fourth. Ludwig advanced to second when Bulldog starting pitcher Katelyn Tribbitt walked Leigha Bosley. Mayah Wilkins (3 for 4) then stroked a single up the middle, scoring Ludwig from second for a 3-2 Warrior lead. Tribbitt ended the rally there, getting Lyla Roe to bounce into a fielder's choice before striking out Olivia Orrell.\nEaston nudged its lead to 4-2 in the fifth. With one out, Tribbitt issued back-to-back walks to Laillah Janes and Gemma Vogel. Tribbitt was relieved by Anna Hutchison, who hit Brynlee Buchkoski with a pitch to load the bases before walking Ludwig, scoring courtesy runner Kristian Cherry. That would be the Warriors' final run, as Hutchison retired the next two batters, with third baseman Maddie Porter making a nice grab on Wilkins' low line drive to end the inning.\n\"They kept us in the game,\" Lee Hutchison said of his two pitchers. \"They've been doing it all year. We just got to clean the defense up and get the key hits.\"\nNorth Caroline threatened to tie the game in the top of the sixth.\nHatteras Ghrist led off with an infield hit, ripping a hard shot that Warrior third baseman Orrell made a nice backhanded stop on, but couldn't get the throw to first in time. Easton catcher Janes threw out Ghrist stealing before Hadley Eigenbrode dumped a single into shallow right field. Brooklyn Spencer came in as a courtesy runner for Eigenbrode.\nEaston starting pitcher Sammy Townsend (10-2) got Jordyn Hunt to pop out to Wilkins at short for the second out. Alyssa Garlick followed by drilling an 0-1 pitch down the left-field line for a double, pushing Spencer to third. Porter smacked a comebacker off Townsend's glove that scored Spencer, making it a 4-3 game. Townsend then got Natellie Newman to bounce out to third for the final out, though North Caroline's coaching staff protested, arguing Newman beat Orrell's throw from third.\nGracie Calloway reached on a two-out error in the top of the seventh, but Townsend got Ghrist to pop out to second to end the game.\n\"It's been a long two weeks with the stretches of games that we've had,\" said Brice, whose team has gone 6-0 in that span. \"I can't say enough about Sammy Townsend. She's an amazing pitcher. She's an amazing player. For her to be called on (in Wednesday's 8-6 come-from-behind win over James M. Bennett) in the first inning without warming up and finishing the game. She's matured these last two weeks and it shows.\"\nTownsend gave up eight hits, struck out six and walked two.\nNorth Caroline took a 2-0 lead in the third. Porter reached on a bad-hop single. Newman got on via an error. After walking Anna Hutchison, Tribbitt singled into center field, scoring Porter and Newman.\nEaston answered in the bottom of the inning. Wilkins reached on a bunt single, stole second and third and scored when Roe jerked a double to left. Orrell singled. Townsend then sent a sacrifice fly to right that scored Roe with the tying run.\n\"We battled through our errors and everything, and we showed up at the plate,\" Townsend said. \"I've definitely pitched better, but I've definitely pitched worse.\"\nNORTH DORCHESTER 5, COL. RICHARDSON 1\nSHILOH — Emilee Cohee pitched a three-hitter, striking out 12 and walking two as the Eagles clinched the North Bayside crown.\nMackenzie Lewis, Maggie Hubbard and Kinsley Peters each had two hits for North Dorchester.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 19, CAMBRIDGE-SD 3\nCAMBRIDGE — Abby Gunther went 4 for 4 and Morgan Gottleib was 3 for 4 with a homer to lead the Lions (16-2, 6-2).\nAbbie Denes and Autumn Huber each drove in three runs for Queen Anne's, and Caroline Taylor, Camaryn Jacobi and Hannah Martin had multi-hit games.\nMartin earned the win, allowing four hits over five innings, walking one, and striking out 10.\nBASEBALL\nNORTH DORCHESTER 6, WASHINGTON 5\nPRINCESS ANNE — Nate Fraccaroli singled twice and doubled, driving in two runs, and Wes Windsor had three hits, an RBI and a run scored to lead the Eagles on Wednesday.\nStarting pitcher John Boyle allowed four runs over four innings, striking out five for North Dorchester. TJ Leonard pitched three innings of scoreless relief, yielding one hit and fanning three, to earn the win.\nBoyle had a hit, was walked twice and scored two runs. Vernon Price had two hits and stole four bases.\nQUEEN ANNE'S 6, PARKSIDE 5\nCENTREVILLE —Noah Palmer pitch 6 1/3 innings before Dalton Brown came on to earn the save Wednesday as the Lions topped the defending Class 2A state champions.\nPalmer also went 2 for 3 with two RBIs for Queen Anne's. Bobby McCarthy (2 for 3)( knocked in a run, and Jacob Clough (two RBIs), Evan Keppler and Dulin Clark had one hit apiece.\nGIRLS LACROSSE\nSSPP 18, ST. MARK'S 5\nEASTON — Senior Morgan Quade scored two goals and set up five others Wednesday to reach 200 goals and 400 assists for her career as the Sabres rolled to their 12th straight victory.\nSophomore Caroline Ewing netted seven goals and one assist for Saints Peter and Paul (12-1) and classmate Eryn Callahan had a hat trick and an assist. Kendall Kroniser and Emery Zimmerman each scored twice, and Samantha Murphy and Kate Sweeney had one goal apiece. Avery McCall finished with three assists and Emma Hainey had an assist. Goalie Kylie Kroniser made six saves and Veronica Pool had two stops. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 5,
    "day": 2,
