  {
    "title": "Friars strike quick, top SSPP",
    "date": "2024-05-18",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Friars strike quick, top SSPP\n\n May 18, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 822\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n ANNAPOLIS — Saints Peter and Paul High's boys lacrosse team swept Archbishop Curley during their two regular-season meetings this spring.The top-seeded Sabres met the Friars for a third time Friday at Navy-Marine Corps Memorial Stadium. Only this time, the two-time defending Maryland Interscholastic Athletic Association B Conference champions showed just how much experience matters.\nThe Friars scored twice in the opening minute, taking control with a five-goal opening flurry, then tightened its grip even further during a dominating second quarter en route to a 18-9 victory over Sts. Peter & Paul and a third straight MIAA B Conference title.\n\"I guess we're too young and they couldn't handle where it was,\" Sabres head coach Todd Wolters said. \"They (Curley) came out running. We came out walking. Again, they (SSPP) haven't been here. That's their third (title) in a row. They got a lot of seniors on that team and I think that showed. And again, they're a really good team.\"\nCurley (12-7) wasted little time showing how good it is, as senior Carson Gianelli (three goals, assist) scored just 28 seconds into the game, then added a second 11 seconds later. Senior captain James Zingo (four goals) made it 3-0 with 7:08 left in the first quarter. Brandon Mejia (three goals) and Matt Ramirez scored in the closing minute for a 5-0 Curley lead.\nSts. Peter and Paul's Grant Messick (three goals) converted a Noah Zimmerman pass into the Sabres' first goal with 16.6 seconds left in the first quarter, but that was the only ball put past Friars goalie Ryan Cosey in the first quartr. Curley answered, Carson Gianelli setting up the first of Dominic Broadwater's three goals with just 5.6 seconds left in the opening period for a 6-1 Friars' lead.\n\"It was huge,\" Curley head coach Chris Ogle said of the opening quarter. \"I think experience played a role in that. Our seniors, our leaders carried us through that first quarter.\"\nThat senior leadership continue to pace the Friars in the second quarter.\nAfter Zingo's goal 8 seconds into the quarter made it 7-1, Messick responded with an unassisted strike for the Sabres 15-5).\nBut Curley's Cooper Granados, Carson Gianelli, Ramirez and Gavin Albright scored the game's next four goals in a little over 2 minutes. Albright and Mejia tacked on two more for a commanding 13-2 lead with 35.1 seconds left in the first half.\n\"Again, they've been here,\" Wolters said of Curley. \"They know what it takes to win. They out-physicaled us. They out-hustled us. We'd turn it over, not with them doing anything.\"\nLiam Durnbaugh (three goals) scored on an extra-man opportunity with 2.9 seconds left in the first half to make it 13-3 at the break.\nZingo made it 14-3 early in the third. But the Sabres scored consecutive goals for the first time when Vincent Dyer set up a Zimmerman goal and Durnbaugh scored unassisted to make it 14-5 with under 6 minutes left in the third.\nCurley called time and came back with a pair of goals, Albright (three goals) bouncing a shot past Sabres goalie Hank Wolters and Mejia scoring while falling down to make it 16-5.\nSts. Peter & Paul, which won 9-6 at Curley on April 23 and 11-9 at home on May 10, continued battling, as JJ Rardin — a focal point for the Friars' defense — and Messick scored just 12 seconds apart, pulling the Sabres within 16-7 heading into the fourth.\n\"My defensive coaches did an outstanding job putting together a game plan that we've kind of had in our backpocket,\" Ogle said. \"We had a feeling we were going to see the Sabres again, and the kids executed to perfection for us. We wanted to take Rardin out of the game. Our boys did an outstanding job with that. I also think we shot the ball better. We've been working on that and it really came to fruition today.\"\nThe teams played even in the fourth quarter, with Zimmerman scoring on an EMO and Durnbaugh scoring with a left-handed shot with a little over 6 minutes left. David Fritz and Zingo added goals for the Friars.\n\"I can't say anything but give them credit,\" Wolters said. \"The second half we beat them (6-5). Not that that matters. Our kids could have given up when you're down by 10. To come back in the second half, and they were still shooting with a minute to go.\n\"The kids didn't give up,\" Coach Wolters continued. \"We're young. We're going to be back next year. Then we'll come back with some experience.\"\nGIRLS LACROSSE\nQUEEN ANNE'S 6, STEPHEN DECATUR 4STEVENSVILLE — Junior RJ Ensor had a hat trick and set up a fourth goal Friday, as the sixth-seeded Lions defeated the No. 2 seed Seahawks in a Class 2A state semifinal at Kent Island High.\nQueen Anne's will play fourth-seeded Glenelg for the state championship on Tuesday at 4 p.m. at Stevenson University's Mustang Stadium.\nBaillie Pinder (assist), Riley Klepper and Kaisa Hickson each scored once for Queen Anne's. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
