  {
    "title": "Lions can't find way past Glenelg",
    "date": "2024-05-22",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Lions can't find way past Glenelg\n\n May 22, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 746\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n OWINGS MILLS — It had faced tough defenses before this season.But usually Queen Anne's County High's girls lacrosse team solved the opposition's defensive scheme, finding enough openings and seams to carve a path to victory.\nThe Lions couldn't find a solution though late Tuesday afternoon, as Glenelg dominated possession and yielded Queen Anne's few offensive opportunities en route to a 13-3 victory in the Class 2A state championship at Stevenson University's Mustang Stadium.\n\"They were able to come out defensively and put on the pressure and hold it,\" Lions head coach Kesley Fitzgerald said of Glenelg.\nThe Gladiators (18-1) were also able to hold the ball. Glenelg consistently won draw controls, reached ground balls before the Lions, and seemed to have someone on the ball every time Queen Anne's (15-3) lost possession or had a pass miss connection.\n\"Lots of speed and explosiveness,\" Fitzgerald said of the Gladiators. \"And when you don't have lots of possessions you have to make every single one count.\n\"In the beginning of the first quarter we were,\" said Fitzgerald, whose team fell behind 2-0 in the opening 61 seconds, then cut that deficit in half with 8:46 left in the first quarter. \"We were working through some kinks on defense, and every time offense had it in the first quarter we were converting. And then it came down to the draw possessions, and just making sure we got the ball out safely. And we didn't do that as well as we have all season.\"\nGlenelg took a lead in never surrendered 34 seconds into the game when Kayleigh White (two goals) swept in the from the left and put a shot past Lions goalie Maddie George. Just 27 seconds later, Kamryn Henson (three goals) gained possession at the top of the box, dodged two defenders and stuck a shot into the right side of the net for a 2-0 lead.\nQueen Anne's, which entered the game on a 10-game winning streak in which it averaged 13 goals a game, didn't appear rattled, as it pulled within 2-1, when RJ Ensor set up Riley Klepper's goal. The Gladiators regained their two-goal cushion a little over a minute later, when Regan Kelleher (two goals, two assists) found Reilly Kelleher alone in front of the net for a goal. Maggie Metz (three goals) scored in transition, extending Glenelg's lead to 4-1 with 3:36 left in the opening quarter.\nThe Lions answered, as Baillie Pinder dished to Ensor to make it 4-2 with 52.4 seconds remaining in the first quarter.\nBut Queen Anne's wouldn't score again until Madison Brown's goal with 3:40 left on a running clock in the fourth period made it 13-3.\n\"Offensively, they were all moving,\" Fitzgerald said of Glenelg. \"Great off-ball movement. That's a strong team.\"\nA team that scored nine consecutive goals after Ensor's strike to build a 13-2 lead with 9:41 left in the contest.\nGlenelg had six different goal scorers before Henson netted her second off an assist from Emmy Dello Russo with 58.8 seconds left in the first half, pushing the Gladiators' lead to 8-2 at halftime. Chloe Key, Dello Russo and Regan Kelleher also scored in the first half.\nFitzgerald rotated George in goal with Emma Dulin throughout the game, both making big saves to keep the score respectable.\n\"Both those girls did an amazing job,\" Fitzgerald said.\nGlenelg tacked on three third-quarter goals, with Henson scoring once and Metz twice. Regan Kelleher and White scored one apiece in the fourth before Brown ended Queen Anne's scoring drought.\n\"I do know we have the makings of being right there with them,\" said Fitzgerald, whose team was bidding for its second state title in four years. \"Wasn't our day today. Super proud of the team. They have nothing to hang their heads about. We got here. We deserved to be here. And we'll be back.\n\"Every year we start out and try to figure out what the roles are going to be,\" Fitzgerald continued. \"This year we made those roles very apparent, very early; earlier than I ever have before. And that's what worked for us this year. Everybody fell into their role. I'm fortunate every year at Queen Anne's as their coach to just have amazing student-athletes.\"\nBASEBALLBRUNSWICK 26, COL. RICHARDSON 6FREDERICK — Derrick Mitchell had a pair of hits for Colonel Richardson, which had its hopes of a third straight Class 1A state final appearance end in Tuesday's semifinal round at McCurdy Field.\nBrayden Dadds, Caleb Rouser and Gavin Whitby each had one hit for the Colonels, who finished the season 20-3. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
