  {
    "title": "CHAMPS",
    "date": "2024-05-23",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "CHAMPS\n\n May 23, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Champs \n \n 1036\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n LANDOVER — Clint Falduto knew last summer, predicting Cambridge-South Dorchester High's boys outdoor track and field team would win a state title.The Vikings' co-head coach knew for certain Thursday, when his 4x100 relay took runner-up honors, clinching the program's first-ever state title outdoors with one event remaining in the Class 1A state championships.\nBut co-head coach Lois Narr didn't know, and didn't want to know until an official verdict was announced.\n\"I didn't look,\" said Narr, who at any time could have asked Falduto, or checked the AthleticLive app on her phone to see how her team was doing. \"I didn't look the whole time. I didn't want to know. All I wanted to know is every kid got a chance to do their best. And he (Falduto) kept trying to tell me and I'm like, 'Don't tell me until we know. I don't want to hear it. I don't want to be anxious. I don't want to know.' And when he told me I was just crying.\"\nA meet that started with light rain ended with Cambridge-SD hoisting the Class 1A state championship trophy under a beaming sun in the infield of Marvin F. Wilson Stadium at the Prince George's Sports and Learning Complex.\n\"This is the best thing ever, man,\" said Cambridge-SD's Tori Willis Jr., who won the boys 300-meter intermediate hurdles in a 1A meet-record time of 38.68 seconds. \"We've been doing this since two years ago when we first started. It was just a few of us, we added on, and got better and better each year. It feels like Avengers' Endgame. We finally got Thanos. We finally got Catoctin.\"\nCambridge-SD totaled 80½ points to defeat runner-up Catoctin (72) and third-place Western Tech (58).\nThe Vikings' victory was the first for a Bayside Conference school since James M. Bennett's girls won in 2004, and the first for a Bayside boys' team since Wicomico in 1988.\n\"They're the best,\" said Narr, fighting back tears. \"Before we got off the bus I said to them, 'This is the finest team we've ever had.' We've never taken second. We've never taken first. It was a team effort.\"\nLeading that effort was Willis, who not only won his second straight 300 hurdles title, but placed fourth in the 100 meters (10.98), and led a 3-4-5 Viking finish in the 110 high hurdles with a time of 14.69. Antonio Blueford (14.76) was fourth and Jayden Smith fifth in 14.86. Willis capped his day by anchoring the 4x100 relay (43.27) of Jakuez Snead, Kay'ion Marine and Teshar Drummond, breaking into a huge grin after crossing the finish line, knowing the Vikings had clinched the title.\n\"My main goal was just to get out very fast and just try to keep as long as possible to get the win,\" Willis said of the 300 hurdles, which, despite setting a new 1A state meet record, was not a personal-record.\nWhile Falduto admitted he was hoping for a better finish in the 110 hurdles, he also noted the Vikings gained huge points from some unexpected sources, most notably Snead finishing fourth in the shot put (46-6), and Majestic Jones (6 feet) and William Jackson (5-10), finishing fifth and sixth, respectively in the high jump. Jackson also added four points with his fifth-place finish (12-6) in the pole vault.\n\"Just figuring out their best position of where we think they can succeed the most,\" Falduto said. \"And we definitely put that team together this year. This year has just truly been incredible. Watching Tori break the (1A) state record, and just showing up in places we were not expected. Our boys just knew it meant every point. Even though we didn't place everywhere we wanted to, we still finished the races and got the points that were needed.\"\nIn addition to his fifth-place finish in the 110 hurdles, Smith delivered two more big efforts, taking third in the triple jump (43-¾) and fourth in the long jump (21-¾). Then there was Tekai Drummond, who settled into a rhythm in the 800 before putting on a big surge down the stretch to place second in 1:58.16.\n\"That was a big swing race,\" Narr said, noting Catoctin had three runners seeded in the top eight, but only had one finish in the top 11.\nOn Day 1 of the meet Wednesday, Cambridge-SD senior Kassidy Young won the girls 1A discus with a heave of 124-4 on her first attempt, beating her previous best by an impressive 12 feet.\n\"I wasn't expecting to PR by as much as I did,\" Young said who couldn't explain her first throw. \"I don't even know. I just let it fly. It was a real shock. I walked up to my coach (Narr), and I couldn't even explain.\"\nNorth Dorchester senior Vernon Price delivered what some may have considered a surprise, when he won the boys 1A high jump title Wednesday in his first year competing outdoors, clearing the bar at 6-6.\n\"I knew I could go for gold,\" Price said. \"I wanted to go for No. 1 in the state of Maryland with 6-8. And I should have cleared but form, because it is my first year, I really haven't had a lot of time to practice.\nA dual-sport athlete who also played baseball this season, Price was second in the triple jump (44-1¾) and third in the long jump (21-2).\nQueen Anne's County junior Ben Marks dueled Century's Ryan Hartranft and Southern's Eric Penkala in the 2A boys 1,600 meters, finishing second with in a personal-record 4:14.03. Hartranft won in 4:13.00, while Penkala placed third in 4:15.24.\nMarks came back to place second in the 800, clocking a 1:54.85 to Harford Tech's Alexander Wockenfuss, who won in 1:54.28.\nEaston's Sumayah Wilkins took runner-up honors in the 2A girls high jump, clearing 5-4. Patuxent freshman Aiyana Smalley won at 5-6.\nColonel Richardson's Aydan Downie was second in the 1A boys long jump (22-4½), and Queen Anne's freshman JaDereon Thompson finished third in the 2A boys long jump (22-0).\nKent Island senior Gabrielle Bernhard was second in the 2A girls 300 hurdles (45.52), third in the pole vault (11-0) and fifth in the 100 hurdles (15.44). Cambridge-SD junior Le'Asia Todd was third in the 1A girls long jump (16-4¾), fifth in the triple jump (33-7¾), and sixth in high jump (4-8). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
