  {
    "title": "DURRIE'S DEDICATION",
    "date": "2024-07-27",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "DURRIE'S DEDICATION\n\n July 27, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 831\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — The late Durrie Hayes would have enjoyed the afternoon of May 28, 2024, his light mustache unable to conceal that Cheshire cat-like smile.A large collection of friends, former players and assistants gathered in a loose, elongated semicircle inside the right-field foul line at Easton High's softball field on that spring day fit for a doubleheader.\nSoon bats would be swung, pitches hummed, ground and fly balls collected, and runs scored as part of the school's inaugural alumni softball game.\nNot everyone had come to play, but all had come to catch the pregame that featured speeches thick on compliments, and the unveiling of the newly erected scoreboard behind the center-field fence in Hayes' honor. And those who knew him well can pretty much imagine Hayes' reaction once his eyes settled on his name emblazoned across the top of the scoreboard in big, bold orange letters outlined in white on a black background.\n\"I can just picture him smiling that little smile he had,\" said Casey (Dulin) Baynard, a star on Hayes' early teams and now a Warrior assistant. \"I think he would have been very proud.\"\nDr. Ed Baker, the longtime umpiring legend who said, \"Hayes was by far the best softball coach that I every umpired a game for,\" agreed.\n\"Durrie was a very humble person,\" Baker said of Hayes, who died April 2, 2020 at age 70 after a brief battle with cancer. \"Naturally he would be very, very pleased and honored to see such a scoreboard. But he was never looking for his name to be in flashing lights.\"\nJay Cappa, an assistant during Hayes' 13 years at Easton, perhaps pegged the former head coach's reaction best.\n\"He would have said, 'Who'd a thunk it?'\" Cappa said. \"I do believe he would have said, 'Who'd a thunk it?'\"\nActually, it was Byrd Dog Wheeler who first thunk Hayes be memorialized, initiating the drive for a scoreboard honoring the school's Hall of Fame coach four years ago.\n\"It's incredible what that guy did,\" said Wheeler, another longtime friend and one of the ceremony's speakers. \"And that one run he had there; what the last eight years? Jeez Louise.\"\nHayes coached the Warriors from 1995-2007, compiling an impressive 232-46 record that included seven Bayside Conference championships, six regional titles and three state championships. Wedged in that 13-year stretch was an eight-year run (2000-07), where the Warriors went 162-18 that included a 23-0 season in 2004 — an unbeaten streak that was extended to 30 games the following year — three state championships and three state runner-up finishes.\n\"He put in all that time going to Little League girls softball games and travel team softball games, and talking to the parents and getting them involved, and getting them to buy into the program at such a young age,\" Wheeler said. \"By the time they got up to ninth grade they were on track and knowing what to expect and ready to play. No one else did anything like that.\n\"One thing he couldn't say is that, 'There's others out there that deserve that a lot more than I do.' Not a chance he could say that,\" Wheeler added.\nWell, now hold on there big dog.\nThere was in fact somebody who would have penciled himself into the leadoff spot to protest that very point — Hayes.\n\"It was always about the girls,\" Wheeler conceded. \"It wasn't about Durrie Hayes.\"\nOthers could easily see Hayes lobbying for his players' names to be adorned atop the scoreboard, instead of his.\n\"I think deep down he would have been very honored,\" longtime friend Debbie Urry said. \"But I think he would have been embarrassed because it should have gone to the girls. He was always about the girls. He never wanted that kind of attention. I think he would have been very humbled and surprised. And he would have given credit to all the girls he ever coached.\"\nJay Chance, who coached with Hayes at Easton and Chesapeake College, watched the skipper consistently direct praise his players' way.\n\"He never really made it all about him,\" said Chance, who presented Hayes during the induction ceremony for Easton High's inaugural Athletic Hall of Fame class in September 2019. \"It was more important to make a difference in the girls' lives than it was to win a game. That's what I liked so much about him. Although on game day you would never know that [laughing]. On game day it was about winning.\"\nOh, and the fun Hayes would have had filling out the lineups for both teams in the alumni game.\n\"He definitely would have loved the alumni game that we had after that,\" Baynard said.\nAs the ceremony wrapped up, Cappa and Chance reminisced and joked about what Hayes' take would have been on this big to-do.\n\"Jay said, 'Watch it. He's libel to send down a lightning bolt,'\" Cappa said laughing. \"I told Jay, 'He's not going to do it 'cause they're going to play a softball game. He ain't going to ruin the softball game.' He said, 'Yeah, I guess you're right.'\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
