  {
    "title": "Claxton, Blue notch top honors",
    "date": "2024-07-30",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Claxton, Blue notch top honors\n\n July 30, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1264\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n He's not a sprinter.And yet Tucker Claxton's speed is one of his better and perhaps most underrated qualities, especially when considering his shooting and passing skills.\n\"His speed is very deceptive,\" Kent Island High boys head lacrosse coach Bobby Woolley said of Claxton. \"So when you think you're running with him, he's got another gear that he just quickly shifts into. And it doesn't matter if the teams put a (long) pole on him, or a shortie. It's just so difficult to defend him one on one. So that just allows a lot of guys to kind of free up, because in most cases he's getting a slide really early so it kind of allows things to get in motion for us.\"\nClaxton seemingly put things in motion whenever he was on the field this season, pacing the Buccaneers to their ninth Bayside Conference championship in 12 years — excluding the COVID-canceled title games of 2020 and '21 — while leading the North Bayside in goals and assists en route to being named the North Bayside Offensive Player of the Year, as selected by division coaches.\n\"No one could stop him,\" Easton head coach Dennis Keenan said of Claxton, who netted 47 goals and 41 assists — both North Bayside highs — and 42 ground balls. \"Watching film, I mean everybody knew what he was bringing to the table. But nobody could stop him.\"\nThe two-time defending Class 2A state champions, Claxton and the Buccaneers went 12-5 this season, earned their 11th division crown in 12 seasons — again excluding '20 and '21 — and weren't stopped until the 2A state semifinals, where the bid for a third consecutive state title was foiled by eventual state champion Glenelg.\nAnd while he was the North's scoring leader, Claxton also proved to be a team leader, stepping into the void created by the graduation of Kasey Heath, Kent Island's outstanding goalie on the back-to-back state teams.\n\"I knew Tucker had the potential to be that guy,\" Woolley said of his senior midfielder who is headed to Mount St. Mary's. \"Being around long enough you watch guys mature year over year, and I knew that he would come into his senior year just a different kid than he was in his junior year, where he didn't really have to have a voice. The fact that in his senior year he would, I knew he would respond well. He was certainly the guy that most guys turned to for the leadership that was needed.\"\nQueen Anne's County turned and often got big plays when it needed them from senior goalie Dino Blue, named the North Bayside's Defensive Player of the Year.\n\"Phenomenal,\" Lions head coach Brad Niesz said of Blue. \"This year he just settled in as the absolute backbone of our team. Was an absolute wall. Gave us transition opportunities that led to goals, led to opportunities, led to winning games.\"\nBlue faced 172 shots this season, stopping 110 for a goals-against average of 6.8 per game and a save percentage of 64% for a Lions team that finished 7-6.\n\"He's just good,\" Woolley said of Blue, who does not plan on playing in the collegiate ranks. \"He's spunky. He's twitchy. He's got great reflexes. I think he's able to position himself well against most of the common shots that are coming at him. You've got to get a little on top of him to get quality looks. I think he's a really athletic kid who has great stopping potential.\"\nWoolley thought the Bucs had the potential for a third straight title run this past spring. Kent Island followed a familiar formula, playing stiff competition in the first half of the season in preparation for the playoffs. But that plan didn't produce the same results as in years past, as the Bucs struggled early.\n\"Normally we know who's going to emerge as the replacements for the guys we lose and this year just wasn't clearly defined going into the season,\" Woolley said. \"So we had to use the first part of the year to kind of move pieces around and kind of figure out the puzzle that worked for us. The biggest challenge was finally finding the equation that worked for us via personnel. And along the way there was just some unusual hiccups. We were putting guys in positions to potentially fail as a result of us trying to see if they were the guy.\"\nAfter a 1-3 start, the Bucs figured out who was best where, winning 11 of their final 13 games, earning Woolley all-North Coach-of-the-Year honors.\nHere's a look at this year's all-North teams as selected by the coaches:\nFirst TeamAttackDrew Schmidt, Sr., Easton: Helped Warriors to an 11-3 record and was second only to Claxton in total points, leading Easton with 54. Ranked second in goals scored for Easton (34) and shared the team lead for assists (20) with Hudson Royer. Is headed to Lynchburg (Virginia) University.\nOwen Wynot, Sr., Kent Island: The Bucs' second-leading goal scorer with 32, Wynot also had six assists. Plans to continue playing at St. Mary's College.\nTyler Rardin, Sr., Easton: Led the Warriors with 35 goals and set up 15 more. Plans to walk-on at Salisbury University.\nMicah Quinn, Sr., Kent Island: Another key cog in the Bucs' bid for a third straight title, Quinn totaled 30 goals and 10 assists. Headed to Flagler (Florida) College.\nMidfieldHudson Royer, Sr., Easton: Keenan and the Warriors are probably still thinking of what might have been had Royer not suffered a game-ending injury against Kent Island during a 12-11 loss in the 2A East Region I final. Ended the season with 23 goals and 20 assists. Will join Schmidt at Lynchburg.\nKeegan Green, Jr., Kent Island: Developed into another solid scoring option in a multi-headed offense. Green finished as the Bucs' second leading scorer, netting 28 goals and 16 assists (44 points).\nRyan Lee, Jr., Queen Anne's: Long-stick midfielder often drew the opposition's top scoring threat, and finished the season with 27 ground balls and 12 takeaways.\nDefenseGavan Henry, Jr., Kent Island: The Bucs' top defender, Henry could play a shut down role and also direct as the defense developed through the season. Finished with three goals and three assists, but was a magnet for ground balls, totaling 55. Also caused 17 turnovers.\nColin Scattergood, Sr., Queen Anne's: Niesz called Scattergood a Swiss Army Knife for his ability to play anywhere on the defensive end. Finished the season with 46 ground balls, 14 takeaways, and scored a goal. Intends to play at Washington College.\nKyle Dyott, Sr., Easton: Dyott's communication skills and tenacity made him one the North's top defenders. \"He was relentless on ball carriers,\" Keenan said of the senior defender who is headed to Emory and Henry College in Virginia.\nBilly Schindler, Sr., Queen Anne's: After leaving lacrosse to focus on soccer, Schinder returned his sophomore year, and this season proved to be one of the division's top defenders. The short-stick defensive midfielder who is headed to Washington College to play soccer and lacrosse, finished with 44 ground balls and seven takeaways.\nFace-off SpecialistQuinn McFaden, Sr., Kent Island: McFadden became a catalyst for the Bucs attack, winning 132 of 202 face-offs for a 65.3 percentage. Also collected 33 ground balls.\nSECOND TEAMAttack: Will Speakman, Sr. Kent County; Jamison Kendall, So., Queen Anne's; Jackson Shiley, So., Queen Anne's; Jameson Krukiel, Sr., Queen Anne's; Midfield: Martin Parish, Sr., Kent County; Ethan Clark, Jr., Kent County; Landon Wallace, Sr., Kent County; Ethan Finlay, Jr., Easton; Defense: Frank Kelly, Sr., Queen Anne's; Ben Loller, Sr., Kent County; Ryan Myers, Sr., Kent County; Ty Finlay, Sr., Easton; Goalie: Jack Kilbourne, Sr., Easton; Faceoff: Owen Boeshore, Sr., Queen Anne's. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
