  {
    "title": "BANKING ON EXPERIENCE",
    "date": "2024-08-28",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "BANKING ON EXPERIENCE\n\n August 28, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 737\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n AMERICAN CORNER — James Jackson isn't used to this.Seemingly everywhere Colonel Richardson High's head football coach looks this preseason there's a senior here and a senior there.\n\"I need to talk to (longtime assistant and former head coach) Neil Lambert, but I believe this is the biggest senior class Colonel football's ever had,\" Jackson said. \"Under my supervision it's definitely the most.\"\nAnd for that reason optimism is high for a group that would like to notch seven or eight regular-season victories and possibly match, or even exceed, the postseason run of the 2021 team that made a bunch of school history, which included reaching the Class 1A state quarterfinals for the first time.\n\"They're all talking about making a deep playoff run,\" Jackson said. \"They're just looking forward to getting back to where we were their freshman year.\"\nThe Colonels look to have the talent and certainly the experience to make a push in that direction after going 4-6 a year ago, which included a 4-0 mark against Bayside 1A competition.\nSeniors dominate both sides on the ball, with 6-foot-2, 195-pound quarterback Tarron Hammond directing the Double Wing T attack for a third straight year. Behind him are senior wingbacks Dylan Cecil (5-6, 140) and Eamare Jones (5-8, 155), senior fullback Collin Brewer (5-10, 200) and junior wingback Derrick Mitchell (5-11, 185), who led the Colonels in rushing a year ago. Junior wingback Bradley Thomas (5-9, 205) and senior fullback Chatydrick Pickrom (5-11, 230) could also be rotated into the backfield.\nSenior tight ends Domnique Caven (6-0, 175) and Carter Turbitt (5-11, 180) provide two nice targets for Hammond, as well as additional push for the Colonels' steady-as-it-goes run game.\n\"I think clock control is always in our favor,\" Jackson said. \"So running the football, trying to keep the ball in our possession as long as possible, wearing defenses down, that's what we're known for. If we can continue that, we have more success when we have the ball longer than our opponent.\"\nThough Jackson has yet to settle on a starting right tackle, the remainder of the line is all seniors. Gavin Weiss (5-9, 230) is at center, with Chris Rockwell (5-10, 225) at right guard and Duilio Berduo-Morales (5-8, 235) at left guard. Stevens Austinville (6-3, 200) starts at left tackle.\n\"The seniors have been together for three years,\" Jackson said. \"It's just now making it all come together.\"\nWhat may help pull things together this season was what Jackson thought was missing in 2023 — senior leadership.\n\"We only had two seniors last year,\" Jackson said. \"So this senior class now, none of them knew how to become a leader. I feel like now there's more talking in practice. There's quite a few of them trying to be leaders. So if we can get all the guys to follow behind these guys we should be pretty good.\"\nThe defense also has the potential to be pretty good.\nRockwell and Brewer, who shared the team lead in sacks last year with 10, line up at the ends, with Berduo-Morales at one tackle and Pickrom (8½ sacks), who can be a big-time difference maker, at the other tackle. If that group can tie up opposing linemen, inside linebackers Thomas and Cecil, and outside backers Mitchell, Jones and junior Carlens Jean Baptiste (5-6, 140) should be in position to make tackles.\nCaven and Turbitt are back at the corners, while junior Jayquan Wilkins (5-7, 145) and sophomore JaZir Dickerson (6-0, 170) are the safeties.\n\"Coverages have got to get better, but in a week or two I think we'll be closer to where we need to be,\" Jackson said. \"But we looked a lot better on our tackling in the scrimmage (against Sussex Tech) than we did all last season. Last season there was a lot of miss-tackles early. But in the scrimmage I thought with did a good job of gang tackling. And the first man to the ball was actually either holding on waiting for help, or making the tackle.\nSince its history-making playoff run of 2021, Colonel — after taking a first-round forfeit over Washington in the 2022 playoffs — has been knocked out of the postseason by Bohemia Manor the last two years, including last year's 12-7 first-round loss.\n\"This group of starters haven't been past the first round of the playoffs,\" Jackson said. \"They're looking for a deep playoff run this year. I feel coming in with experience, that hopefully will carry us to a good season.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 8,
    "day": 28,
