  {
    "title": "WELL GROUNDED",
    "date": "2024-09-04",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "WELL GROUNDED\n\n September 4, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 866\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CAMBRIDGE — He's been running offenses for 20 years, including the last four seasons at Cambridge-South Dorchester High.But Rob Burnham, who took over as Vikings head coach in July, knows a thing or two about defense.\n\"For the last 13 years I've just been an offensive guy,\" said Burnham, who during that span was an assistant to former Vikings head coach Toby Peer, first at South Hagerstown, then at Cambridge-SD. \"But I've coached defense previously for a dozen years so it's not new. Just having to oversee everything is a little bit different. But I like doing it.\"\nBurnham has liked what he's seen on both sides of the ball this preseason from a team that returns 18 starters from last year's 9-3 Class 1A East Region championship outfit that reached the state semifinals, where it lost to eventual state champ Fort Hill.\nThe graduation of Class 1A first-team all-state quarterback Blake LaBelle — now at Frostburg State University — may affect the passing game, but Burnham thinks 6-foot-2, 190-pound senior Anthony Hughes can run the offense just fine.\n\"We lose the all-state quarterback and that stings a little bit,\" Burnham said. \"But we can find other ways to make up for the great things he did for us, and do the best that we can with the kids that we have and find a way to make them successful.\n\"We don't have an all-state quarterback this year I don't believe, unless he just cranks it up and starts throwing it a whole lot better,\" Burnham said of Hughes, who is backed up by freshman Nate Twilley (5-11, 200). \"But Anthony's strength is not his arm, it's his legs. He has the ability to throw, but he's a better runner than he is a thrower.\"\nThe Vikings could again have one of the Bayside Conference's better running attacks, led by returning starter Sterling Perkins, a 5-8, 180-pound junior, who along with Hughes helped the Vikings reach the Class 1A state basketball championship game back in March. Senior Will Jackson (5-10, 170) and juniors Ra'Qwan Cornish (5-11, 150) and Jayden Hollowel (5-10, 175) all have the potential to bust the big play every time they touch the ball.\n\"I think our run game is certainly going to drive the bus,\" Burnham said.\nCovering Cambridge-SD's fleet of receivers could drive opposing defensive coordinators crazy. Heading that cast is senior Antonio Blueford (6-4, 190), a four-year starter, and junior Magestic Jones (5-11, 180) a three-year returning starter. They are joined by juniors Tamyron Evans (5-10, 160), TJ Wilson (6-2, 175) and Leonbre Chester (6-1, 180), and senior Kimir Lake (6-0, 160).\n\"We've got some kids that run fast and jump high,\" Burnham said. \"The plan is to try to find the best things that we can execute and get the ball in our playmakers' hands in space and let them do their thing. We try to maximize their ability.\"\nSenior left guard Omarian Travers (6-4, 255) is the lone returning starter on the offensive line. He starts between freshman center Telorian Williams Jr. (6-4, 265) and sophomore left tackle K'Marion Hall (6-2, 240). The right side of the line has sophomore Jeffrey Sellman (5-11, 240) at guard and junior Russell Johnson (6-2, 200) at tackle. Senior KaMarii Stanley (6-2, 190), who's been on varsity all four years, is at tight end.\n\"I think we've done well up front blocking,\" Burnham said. \"We've had a tremendous amount of improvement that we've done over the course of the last year or so in offensive line play. Our group of kids that we have are picking up things pretty quickly, and I think we did well with our run game.\"\nCambridge-SD has done a solid job against the run in preseason, with sophomore JaKuez Sneed (5-9, 210) starting at nose guard flanked by senior tackles Jairyn Lewis (6-2, 195) and Stanley.\nThe tall, rangy Blueford has the potential to be a wrecking machine at outside linebacker, a unit that includes Hughes, Wilson and Chester. Senior Christen Dockins (5-11, 175), who led the team in tackles a year ago, returns at inside linebacker, teaming with senior Donnell Cephas (5-10, 190), Perkins and Hollowell.\n\"Defense will be the backbone of our team,\" Burnham said. \"We're pretty stout against the run and we're fast everywhere. I don't want to sound conceited, but we don't have slow kids on defense. Every position is fast.\"\nThat's especially true in the secondary where Lake will start at corner with either Jones or Cornish, while Jackson and Evans start as safeties.\n\"I've been pretty impressed by our ability to tackle,\" Burnham said. \"We've only missed a handful of open-field tackles in our scrimmages but we've had a couple of mishaps in coverage. But it's to be expected when you go out and scrimmage. You try to teach from that stuff. It's a learning experience, and you get better and you don't make that mistake again. If we get good film and we teach from it the scrimmage is successful.\"\nJunior Collin Bair handles the kicking duties.\n\"I can't wait to get on the field Friday night and see what our kids can do,\" said Burnham of the Vikings' season opener at North Caroline. \"And I think our kids are excited too. That's ultimately the important part.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
