  {
    "title": "A FINE BLEND",
    "date": "2024-09-04",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "A FINE BLEND\n\n September 4, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 846\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Brian Mooney likes the blend Queen Anne's County High's football team has going this preseason.The Lions' second-year head coach likes how his offensive spread game is mixing with the return of former head coach Donny Graef's double-wing approach.\nMooney likes that his offense returns rock-solid 1,000-plus yard rusher John Connolly and sophomore sensation Ja'Dereon Thompson. He likes how his almost new offensive line is coming together under Graef's tutelage.\nMooney also likes his defensive blend, where he and Graef have been molding ends Amari Baynard and Conlan Nagle, where the electric Thompson and two-time all-Bayside Conference corner Carter Dickey are again roaming the secondary, and where Parker Whiteford — after a year at DeMatha — has returned in an alpha role at inside linebacker.\n\"It's a work in progress,\" said Mooney, who went 6-5 a year ago. \"We're transitioning.\"\nPerhaps the biggest transition is on offense, where Graef, the school's winningest head coach — compiling a 176-95 record over 25 years — returns as an assistant. Among Graef's duties will be coaching the offensive line, which was the longtime domain of another Lions' coaching great, Dave Perillo, now at Easton.\n\"We have a brand new line,\" said Mooney, noting senior Nate Mayoral did get a couple spot starts last season. \"He's the only one with varsity starting experience. All those guys are cutting their teeth under coach Graef which is awesome.\nCentering that line will be 5-foot-11, 225-pound junior Drew Sutton. Juniors Brian Maleski (5-10, 185) and Mac Windsor (6-foot, 210) line up at guard, though senior Ethan Wheaton (5-10, 195) figures to be worked in. Junior Elijah Goldsborough (6-4, 280) is at right tackle and Mayoral (6-3, 260) is on the left side.\n\"To learn from a Hall of Famer is an awesome opportunity for those guys,\" Mooney said of his offensive front which includes senior tight ends Christian St. Germain (5-11, 190) and Whiteford (6-1, 215). \"They're really soaking it in. Coach Graef, he has high expectations, and when they don't meet those expectations he holds them to it. It's really cool to watch that blend with the skills we have.\"\nThe Lions' skill group looks strong. Junior Lance Arnold (5-10, 180), who started the last six games a year ago, returns at quarterback, with sophomore Carter Hercher (6-0, 190) at backup.\nA first-time all-conference pick a year ago, Connolly (6-0, 210), a senior, returns to lead a backfield that includes juniors Dorrin Phillips (5-9, 195) and Dickey (5-8, 155), and sophomore Hunter Kelly (5-9, 170).\n\"We have some skill guys that are going to make any offensive line look good,\" Mooney said. \"John Connolly, we expect to lean on him very heavily.\"\nBut as far as play calling goes, Mooney plans to balance his spread philosophy with Graef's old-school style.\n\"We're kind of working on doing some things Coach Graef is more familiar with,\" Mooney said. \"Bringing him in as offensive line coach makes sense to go back to doing a lot of the things we were successful doing before. It's been a good blend.\"\nAdding to that blend is a receiving corps that includes juniors Baynard (6-4, 190) and Brooks Blodgett (6-0, 175), and senior Nagle (6-2, 190). But the biggest threat is Thompson.\n\"Ja'Dereon obviously is a human highlight reel,\" Mooney said.\nThompson is also a threat to blaze into the end zone every time he handles a kick. Last year as a freshman he averaged 41 yards on kickoff returns, taking three in for touchdowns, and 25 yards on punt returns, which included one touchdown.\n\"He's a blessing to have,\" Mooney said of Thompson. \"And the way he practice and carries himself. I mean some of the stuff you see. ... I always tell the college coaches I talk to, if you could see his practice tape you'd offer him right now.\"\nThe Lions look to have more experience on defense, where Thompson returns at deep safety in a secondary that features Dickey and Kelly at strong safety, Blodgett and junior Wyatt Wilhoit (5-8, 155) at corner, and Hercher, who can be inserted at corner or safety.\nWheaton returns at starting tackle and is joined by Sutton, who had spot starts last year, and Goldsborough, who can give either a break when needed. Bookending that trio are ends Baynard and Nagle.\nThe biggest boost to the defense though figures to be the return of Whiteford at inside linebacker, where he plays along side senior Frank Constantino (5-9, 210) and Maleski.\n\"The linebacker core, Parker being an alpha, being that coach on the field, he always has them in the right spots, and he makes up if they mess up,\" Mooney said. \"He's just a downhill dynamo. We're excited about having a kid like that patrolling the middle of our defense.\"\nNagle will kick and punt, though Mooney said Ethan Kepler, a goalie on the soccer team, could also do some place-kicking throughout the season. The Lions also have Michaels Simmons, who goes from being team manager a year ago, to this year's long snapper on punts and kicks.\n\"We expect to have some bumps and bruises along the way,\" Mooney said. \"But it's going to be fun to see.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
