  {
    "title": "PUTTING IT TOGETHER",
    "date": "2024-09-05",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "PUTTING IT TOGETHER\n\n September 5, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 873\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Ja'Mion Franklin has graduated from Duke University.Kendron Wayman is one game deep into his senior season at Wake Forest. And Nasai Bell is on West Virginia University's roster at safety.\nZymear Smith appears to be the next football standout from North Caroline High headed for the Division I ranks, having verbally committed to the University of Maryland.\nAnd while the talented 6-foot, 175-pound senior halfback figures to draw plenty of attention this season from opponents, Smith knows he's just one part of the Bulldogs' offense and its all-senior backfield.\n\"He's a solid team player for us,\" North Caroline second-year head coach Nic McMorris said of Smith, who was the team's third leading rusher a year ago on the way to earning first-team all-Bayside Conference honors. \"He wants everybody to kind of lift their game, himself included. He doesn't think he's above the program or anything like that. He's taking a lot of pride this season, especially in his blocking. He's a solid leader for us.\"\nHe's also part of what again should be one of the Bayside Conference's more solid backfields with the return of fellow seniors Tyson Weber and Justice Hall.\n\"Tyson is a very hard-nosed runner,\" McMorris said of the 5-10, 180-pound halfback. \"And honestly, he has the ability to be our top receiver. It's saying a lot, but he runs a very good route, and he's got 10½-inch hands. His agility is through the roof, and he's a hard enough runner who prides himself in running between the tackles.\"\nThen there's the 6-foot, 205-pound Hall, who can fill the role of battering ram quite nicely.\n\"Justice is a very hard-nosed runner that I'm looking to lean on a lot,\" McMorris said. \"There's not a lot that's flashy, but he kind of prides himself on being a throwback football player. Exactly how the fullback should be.\"\nAdd to that mix Bell's successor at quarterback, senior Corey Bunce (6-foot, 170), who also hopes to show his running abilities when given the opportunity.\n\"We're putting it together,\" said McMorris, who will have junior Owen Walstrum (5-11, 165) backing up Bunce, and senior Tannor Wood (5-9 140) and junior Darrian Somerville (5-7, 140) coming in to give the starters a breather when needed. \"I'm real happy with our run game. And it's not just my starting guys. I know Tannor and Darrian can get it done with the run game and the pass game.\"\nThe pass game includes a four-man rotation at wide receiver — seniors Jackson Conn (5-8, 145), Shelton Nichols (6-0, 160) and Derek Jones (6-1, 160), and junior Glother Gabriel (5-10, 180) — as well as junior tight end Declan Egan (6-2, 205).\nThere is experience on the offensive line, with the return of junior center Grayson Martin (6-1, 250), senior left tackle Owen Piavis (6-1, 210), and senior Cam King (6-4, 240), who moves from tight end to starting right tackle. Joining that group are sophomore left guard Demarcus Forsyth (6-5, 320) and junior right guard David Kusyk (5-10, 195). McMorris said junior Wyatt Eaton (6-2, 210), one of the team's best offensive linemen, figures to work his way into that starting group as he returns from injury.\n\"We still need to clean some things up just in terms of kids' specific jobs,\" McMorris said of his offense. \"I think some kids are worrying about other kids' jobs. It's affecting the play because they're so worried about another block. It's just a matter of everybody jelling together and having faith in each other to get the job done. Once we get that we should have a really solid running game.\"\nThe defense also looks solid.\nMcMorris plans to go with a four-man rotation at the tackles, with Martin and King starting, and sophomore Xavier Comacho (5-10, 255) and freshman Desmeir Duckery (6-0, 210) stepping in when needed. Piavis, a first-team all-conference pick, returns at defensive end along with Egan and junior Caden Deavers (5-10, 205). All-Bayside linebacker Tommy Morris graduated, but the Bulldogs looked to be in good shape, with Gabriel and Somerville starting inside, and senior David Aviles (6-0, 195) and Hall staring on the outside.\nSmith and Weber figure to form one of the conference's more formidable cornerback combinations. Wood starts at safety, with Walstrum also seeing time in the secondary.\n\"I think we're still putting it together,\" McMorris said of his defense. \"We have a lot of guys that are kind of playing in new positions. We're getting there.\"\nMcMorris knows there may be pressure after going 9-2 in his first season as head coach, but that pressure may not necessarily translate into a repeat of, or improving on last season.\n\"It's more knowing that we have a good team and we should do well; more of just living up to the expectations that we've laid out for ourselves,\" said McMorris, who will have Mason Shilling handling the kicking duties.\n\"We often tell them, it's a thing we've said for years, 'You're never as good as you think you are. You're never as bad as you think you are,'\" McMorris added. \"And it comes down to, you don't read into the outside hype of what's going on. Like our film is going to tell us how we played and we need to be our number one critics of ourselves. Anybody else's opinion is rat poison.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
