  {
    "title": "Vikings rush past Knights",
    "date": "2024-09-13",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Vikings rush past Knights\n\n September 13, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 603\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CAMBRIDGE — Rob Burnham is among the football coaching crowd who stresses the importance of the second half's first possession.The last possession of the first half proved pretty important as well for Burnham's Cambridge-South Dorchester High team Friday night.\nSterling Perkins hauled in a 14-yard touchdown pass just before the end of the first half, then scored on a 3-yard run to open the second half, to help give the Vikings separation on the way to a 49-21 victory over Century.\n\"I tell our kids all the time when we get in the locker room that the first possession of the second half is the biggest possession of the game,\" said Cambridge-SD's first-year head coach after his team improved to 2-0. \"And I believe that. You open the game and score, that's OK because there's a lot of ballgame left. But when you open the second half (and score). ... And I thought it was big that we scored right before halftime and then we got the possession again. So that gives us the two-score lead. That's huge. Huge.\"\nAfter a scoreless first quarter, Cambridge-SD senior quarterback Anthony Hughes scored on runs of 20 and 55 yards to help stake the Vikings to a 14-0 lead with 9 minutes, 18 seconds left in the second quarter. Collin Bair drilled both point-after kicks.\nThe Knights (0-2) rallied though. Jonathan Ferguson scored on a 1-yard run up the middle, but the point-after boot sailed wide left, leaving Century trailing 14-6.\nCambridge-SD drove from its 40 to the Knights' 20, where Dylan Vroom intercepted Vikings backup quarterback Nathan Twilley and raced 80 yards up the sideline for a touchdown. Ferguson's two-point conversion run tied the game at 14-all with 3:02 left before halftime.\nThe Vikings came right back, as Hughes lofted a pass toward the end zone that Perkins made a nice over-the-shoulder grab on for a 14-yard touchdown and a 20-14 lead with 7.6 remaining in the first half. Cambridge-SD bobbled the snap from center on the extra-point attempt, and settled for a six-point lead entering the break.\nThe Vikings needed just three plays to expand that lead on the first possession of the second half. Perkins ran for gains of 17 and 27 yards before he took a handoff and made a quick juke into the end zone for a 3-yard touchdown. Hughes hit Antonio Blueford with a two-point conversion pass for a 28-14 lead just 42 seconds into the third.\nOn its next possession, Century tried to convert a fourth-and-four from its own 31. But quarterback Jacob Dexter fired incomplete, turning the ball over to the Vikings. On the next play, William Jackson swept right and blazed into the end zone for a 31-yard touchdown. Bair added the PAT for a 35-14 lead with 9:08 to go in the third.\nCentury pulled within 35-21 with 4:50 left in the third, as Ferguson bulled his way into the end zone from 2 yards out, then booted the point-after.\nThe Knights though could not stop Cambridge-SD's big plays.\nOn its ensuing possession, Hughes broke free for his third touchdown run, this one covering 42 yards for a 41-21 cushion with 3:34 left in the third.\nCambridge-SD again stopped the Knights on fourth down, but penalties from the Vikings back to their own 6-yard line. It didn't matter, as Perkins took a handoff, found a seam up the middle, and bolted 94 yards for a touchdown. Blueford made a diving grab on Hughes' two-point pass for a 49-21 lead.\n\"We're going to get better,\" Burnham said. \"The things we struggled with today we'll make improvements on. We have to be better than we were tonight that's for sure.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 9,
    "day": 13,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
      "explanation": "The article reports on a high school football game, describing plays, scores, and coaches, which directly aligns with the Sports topic."
    },
