  {
    "title": "Mautz' first goal proves to be game-winner for Saints",
    "date": "2024-09-25",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Mautz' first goal proves to be game-winner for Saints\n\n September 25, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 708\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n ST. MICHAELS — Johnny Mautz hadn't played high school soccer before this season.Judging by the celebration he triggered midway through Tuesday night's second half, his St. Michaels High teammates are elated he decided to give the game a go this season.\nEthan Royer crossed a ball that Mautz converted into what proved to be the game-winning goal, as the Saints rallied from a halftime deficit to notch a 3-2 victory over Class 1A rival and previously unbeaten Colonel Richardson.\n\"I was playing baseball for awhile but I just stopped playing last year,\" said Mautz, a standout on the Saints' basketball team. \"I never played soccer 'cause I didn't want to play three sports. But I said I might as well come out because I was only playing one sport (basketball). And then the rest is history.\"\nThere was also a touch of history attached to Mautz' score.\n\"That was his first goal of the year,\" St. Michaels head coach Pat Hambleton said of Mautz. \"He's hit the post six times.\"\nThat strike with 21 minutes, 9 seconds remaining not only capped a three-goal, second-half surge, giving St. Michaels a 3-1 lead, but it also afforded the Saints (3-2 overall, 2-1 North Bayside) the breathing room they needed to weather Colonels' late-game push.\nNot quite 4 minutes after having a goal erased by an offsides call, Colonel (4-1, 2-1) took a 1-0 lead on Aydan Downie's unassisted tally with 9:42 left in the first half.\nSt. Michaels' best chance to pull even came with just under 2 minutes to go in the first half, when Miles Fox put a shot right on Colonel keeper Cohen Carels, who made a midsection stop.\nThe Saints pulled even though just 1:26 into the second half, when Sherwin Mendez parked a direct kick into the back of the net for a 1-all tie.\n\"I think their free kick a minute and a half into the second half definitely gave them some momentum,\" Colonel head coach Dillon Shaheen said. \"And they took that and ran with it.\"\nNot quite 10 minutes later, Fox got deep on the left wing and steered a crossing pass that Connor Wheeler banged into the net for a 2-1 St. Michaels lead.\n\"Their sweeper was playing a little too far back I noticed,\" Hambleton said. \"He was solely intercepting through balls, so I just made a small adjustment, where once he left for that through ball, I had a forward there, and it happened to be Connor. So he left, peeled off to Miles. Miles received and played that ball across, and it was wide open in the front of the net. It was just a good momentum boost that we needed to start the half.\"\nLess than 8 minutes later, Royer slid a pass from the left side through the crease to the right post and a waiting Mautz for a 3-1 lead.\n\"I've been working on staying back post so that I can get whatever comes my way,\" Mautz said. \"And Ethan played me a beautiful ball and I was just right there for the lefty finish.\"\nColonel wasn't quite finished though.\nWith 15:06 remaining, St. Michaels was called for a handball in the box. Colonel's Angel Castro Rios calmly deposited the penalty kick to the left of St. Michaels keeper Lucas Greenwood to make it a one-goal difference.\nWith Colonel continuing to push into the Saints' end, Hambleton dropped his team into defensive mode. St. Michaels foiled rushes and generally got in the way over the final 12 minutes, with Colonel's best chance for the equalizer coming with 6:37 left, when Greenwood smothered a loose ball near the left post in front of a charging Downie.\n\"Energy. They definitely came out a little more ready than we were, and we talked to them about that at halftime,\" Shaheen said. \"I think they wanted the ball more it seemed. We match up pretty well with them, and they know they match up well with us. What it usually comes down to is which ever team plays harder, and they were definitely that team.\"\nNORTH CAROLINE 8 CAMBRIDGE-SD 0\nCAMBRIDGE — Omar Marroquin had a hat trick as the Bulldogs earned their first win of the season.\nNorth Caroline (1-4, 1-2) also got goals from Mason Shilling, Adrian Lopez Rivera, Brayden Boarts, Karson Hollingworth and Jimmy Cohoon. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 9,
    "day": 25,
