  },
  {
    "title": "Lions show grit; Vikings off to fast start",
    "date": "2024-10-01",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Lions show grit; Vikings off to fast start\n\n October 1, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 623\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Queen Anne's County High isn't the fastest or strongest football team in the Bayside Conference.But four games into the season, the Lions are playing with a longtime program staple — grit.\n\"They're gritty,\" head coach Brian Mooney said after Queen Anne's — playing its first home game of the season — evened its record last Friday night with a 20-15 victory over James M. Bennett. \"There's no quit on this team. We're not going to quit at any point. Adversity struck and they smiled back and we ended up pulling it out.\"\nAdversity has been stalking the Lions since the beginning of the season.\nStarting right tackle Elijah Goldsborough went down during a Week 1 loss at Stephen Decatur. Starting quarterback Lance Arnold suffered a season-ending injury on the final drive of a Week 2 setback at Chesapeake-Anne Arundel.\nDespite those two injuries, Queen Anne's notched its first win of the season Sept. 19 with a 24-0 shutout of Wicomico.\nBut the Lions lost two more starting pieces on offense. Starting left tackle and team captain Nathan Mayoral got injured last week in practice. Then Drew Sutton, who starts at center and defensive tackle, got injured during Friday's win over the Clippers.\n\"We're shuffling some guys around for sure,\" Mooney said.\nJunior Gannon Brown, who was getting limited playing time, has taken over for Goldsborough at right tackle. Levi Frank has stepped into Mayoral's spot on the left side. And Ethan Wheaton is at center.\n\"We're getting back to playing Queen Anne's football,\" Mooney said. \"We're going to line up and punch you in the teeth. It's a next-man-up philosophy and hats off to (assistant) coach (Donny) Graef doing his job on the offensive line. We've had four different starting combinations on our line now in the last four weeks. We're just trying to get some consistency there.\"\nThen there is sophomore quarterback Carter Hercher, who on Friday carried nine times for 58 yards that included a 3-yard touchdown run and a QB sneak for the two-point conversion.\n\"He was a DB (defensive back) at the beginning of the season and we flipped him into the quarterback role,\" Mooney said of Hercher.\nThe Lions got a huge performance from running back John Connolly, who carried 27 times for 147 yards, including touchdowns runs of 18 and 8 yards. Hunter Kelly had 56 yards rushing on six touches, and Parker Whiteford logged eight tackles.\nUNBEATEN VIKINGSCambridge-South Dorchester on Friday pushed its record to 4-0 for the first time since 2014 with a 23-0 shutout of Colonel Richardson.\n\"To be fully honest I didn't expect us to shut 'em out,\" Vikings first-year head coach Rob Burnham said. \"I hoped to slow them down. I was very pleased with our defensive performance. I know the weather played a role in that, but our kids responded to the challenge that was laid before them, and I'm very pleased with the result.\"\nThe second half was played in a heavy rain, but Cambridge-SD had already built a big enough lead at that point.\nSterling Perkins racked up 175 yards rushing on 14 carries that included first-quarter 73-yard touchdown romp. The Vikings fattened their lead in the second quarter, as senior quarterback Anthony Hughes hit Antonio Blueford with a 44-yard touchdown pass. Hughes later scooped a fumble and returned it for a touchdown, then ran in the two-point conversion on a bobbled snap from center. Taybrion Watts had a safety for the Vikings' other points.\n\"Our kids are playing really hard,\" Burnham said of his team's 4-0 start. \"They bought into what I asked them to do. They're very coachable. They're doing the little things right for the most part, and they recognize where we have deficiencies and we've made improvements. We've tried to get better each and every time we touch the field.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
