  {
    "title": "Queen Anne's sweeps past Easton",
    "date": "2024-10-02",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Queen Anne's sweeps past Easton\n\n October 2, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 616\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — The view from college to the high school game has been somewhat of a transition for Jamie Leventry.\"The growth of the players at the high school level is fun to see. We can really see it,\" said Leventry, Queen Anne's County High's first-year head volleyball coach, who last year was coaching at Washington College. \"When I was in college, you really had to grind hard on some players to get them to have basically incremental improvements, whereas here, every week we get better. And it's a lot of fun to be back doing that.\"\nTwo nights after an emotional victory over Kent Island, the Lions registered their seventh sweep of the season Wednesday, grinding their way past Easton, 25-23, 25-20, 25-15 to remain unbeaten.\n\"We played so hard on Monday and it really took a lot out of us,\" Leventry said of Queen Anne's 3-1 victory over Kent Island, leaving the Lions (8-0 overall) alone in first place atop the North Bayside Division at 7-0. \"Even at practice (Tuesday) we were a little sluggish. But we pushed through. It wasn't pretty but we pushed through.\"\nQueen Anne's appeared ready to answer their coaches plea for a fast start when it built a 6-2 lead in Game 1. But a kill by Easton's Sofia Stecher-Roberts pulled the Warriors (3-5, 2-5) even at 9-9. Stecher-Roberts tapped down a kill and converted two service points to give Easton a 15-11 lead. Three times Queen Anne's rallied to tie before a block by Elizabeth Darling pushed the Lions in front at 21-20.\nThe Warriors took a 23-22 lead on a Lions' mishit. But a Bianca Baker kill and two errant Easton returns sealed Game 1 for the Lions.\nEaston took an early lead in Game 2 behind Sacoya Williams' four-point service run, which included an ace. But Marylee Kline's six-point run from the service line nudged Queen Anne's to an 8-6 lead. The match would be tied five times before Baker hammered a winner that gave the Lions a lead they never surrendered at 17-16. And though Alli Dill's back-to-back winners clinched the 25-20 victory for Queen Anne's in the second game, Easton first-year head coach Scott Walstrum was pleased with his team's play.\n\"My big concern was that we'd lose focus mid-set and they didn't,\" Walstrum said. \"We've been getting to mid-set and the bottom has been falling out. But tonight the girls played all the way through.\n\"Queen Anne's is a great team, and a lot of teams can be intimidated by that,\" Walstrum added. \"But our girls had nothing to lose and they put everything out there, and I couldn't be more proud. They played all the way to 25.\"\nGame 3 featured only one tie — that coming at 1-1 — as Queen Anne's steadily built a lead before the Warriors sent a return into the net to complete the 10-point, third-set victory for the defending Bayside Conference champions.\n\"I think our volleyball IQ is getting higher, and we're serving and passing well,\" Leventry said of a team that again features heavy hitters Sydney Pinder and Baker. \"Then the others can contribute as well. Libs (Liberty Ott) has had some big games for us. We've got some depth.\"\nThe Lions have the type of poise they showed last year on the way to winning the Bayside and regional titles.\n\"That's kind of our goal this year was really just stay at a high level; not to get too up or down,\" Leventry said. \"Just play at a good level. And we've had some opportunities this year where we were down a little bit but still started playing at a high level and were able to come back and win. It's nice that we're there.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
