  {
    "title": "Clippers rally to spoil Wilkins head coaching debut",
    "date": "2024-10-11",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Clippers rally to spoil Wilkins head coaching debut\n\n October 11, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 491\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n North Caroline High's football team hadn't had a start like this all season.Now, the Bulldogs have to work on finishing.\nSenior wide receiver Zakai Smullen ran for two touchdowns, caught a TD pass, then threw a game-winning touchdown pass to Ja'Mareon Stevens with a little over a minute remaining Thursday night, as James M. Bennett rallied from a 15-point halftime deficit to defeat visiting North Caroline, 27-21, spoiling the head coaching debut of North Caroline's Heath Wilkins.\n\"There was a really good feeling out there for that first half,\" said Wilkins, who was named head coach after North Caroline fired Nic McMorris this past Monday. \"We felt good going into the game. We knew we'd be able to compete.\"\nThe Bulldogs (0-6) weren't only competing, but leading the Clippers, 21-6, at halftime — matching the most points they had scored in an entire game all season when they lost at Easton, 28-21, in Week 3.\nNorth Caroline's Owen Walstrum returned the opening kickoff for a touchdown. Senior quarterback Corey Bunce then threw a pair of touchdown passes to Derek Jones off play-action to help the Bulldogs to a 21-6 halftime lead.\nBut the Clippers rallied behind Smullen.\n\"We have a package of plays where we just try to get him the ball anyway we can,\" Bennett head coach Greg Lasinski said of Smullen. \"Heckuva player. I think he's the most under recruited player in the Bayside (Conference) right now. Whatever we ask him to do he can do it. He's explosive. He's just one of those kids you're lucky to have on your team because he can flip the game in your favor in a hurry.\"\nSmullen did just that in the second half, scoring his second rushing touchdown before hauling in a touchdown pass from Liam Nelson that pulled the Clippers within 21-19.\nSmullen, who was Bennett's starting quarterback the past two years before being moved to wideout at the beginning of this season, then hit Stevens with a 15-yard touchdown pass and ran in the two-point conversion to complete the Clippers' comeback.\n\"I was proud of the effort in the second half,\" said Lasinski, his team now 3-3. \"It was a resilient effort. This is my third year at Bennett, and in previous years maybe we don't come back and win that game. This group, they work hard. They came out focused in the second half and they kept fighting all the way to the end.\"\nDespite the tough loss, Wilkins was optimistic during a Friday morning phone call.\n\"They haven't quit and that's all I can ask at this point,\" Wilkins said. \"We're going to do film (Friday) and this will be my first chance to do film with these guys ,and we'll be able to see where the mistakes were and make the corrections. This is going to be a totally different team in two weeks.\"\nNorth Caroline hosts defending Class 2A state champion Stephen Decatur (6-0) next Thursday before traveling to Kent Island (3-3) on Oct. 25. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
