  {
    "title": "Bratten, Rams race past Warriors",
    "date": "2024-10-11",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Bratten, Rams race past Warriors\n\n October 11, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 662\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Easton High's football team still has a chance to earn the No. 2 seed for the Class 2A East Region playoffs.But head coach Matt Griffith knows his team has to do better than this.\nParkside running back Karontez Bratten returned the opening kickoff 80 yards for a touchdown, then ran for four more TDs Friday night, leading the Rams to a 37-30 upset of the turnover-plagued Warriors on homecoming.\n\"Not good,\" Griffith said of his team's tackling. \"They've got good athletes, good running backs. We knew that coming in. You can only rep it so much in practice. It's hard when you don't have someone that's like number one (Bratten), number two (Tavian Dashiell), number zero (Canaan Mapp). They're very athletic.\n\"But we hurt ourself tonight,\" Griffith continued. \"Four turnovers; three interceptions, one fumble. Missed field goal. So there's seven to eight extra possessions that we're giving a really good athletic football team. And we can't do that.\"\nBratten took the opening kickoff, veered right and bolted down the visiting sideline to give Parkside (3-3) a 6-0 lead with only 18 seconds off the clock. The point-after kick was blocked.\nParkside's Nazere Sturgis intercepted Easton quarterback Mikey O'Connor (13 of 27, 211 yards, two touchdowns, three interceptions) less than 2 minutes into the second quarter. Three plays later, Bratten found room around right end and was off on a 58-yard touchdown romp to make it 12-0 with 8:44 to go in the second quarter. Easton stopped the two-point conversion attempt.\nThe Warriors (4-2) got on the scoreboard with 4:34 left in the half, when O'Connor hit Zach Barnhart with an 18-yard touchdown pass. Dom Stanco's extra-point made it 12-7.\nBratten returned the ensuing kickoff to Easton's 27. Six plays later, Matt Kramer booted a 25-yard field goal for a 15-7 Rams lead.\nStanco missed a 25-yard attempt with 43.9 seconds left in the half. But on the next play, Easton's Jordan Horsey intercepted Parkside quarterback Chris Morandi and returned the ball 30 yards for a touchdown. O'Connor hit Colin Mooney in the left corner of the end zone for the two-point conversion and a 15-15 tie with 15.6 seconds left before the break.\nBut Bratten (23 carries, 248 yards) again stung the Warriors, finding a seam on the right side and blazing 74 yards for a TD and a 21-15 Rams lead at halftime.\n\"He's getting better every week,\" Parkside head coach Brendan Riley said of Bratten. \"He doesn't need much room, man, and he can frickin find it and he rolls.\"\nParkside recovered an Easton fumble on the first play of the second half, setting up Bratten's 1-yard touchdown run. Harmon ran in the two-point conversion for a 29-15 lead with just under 10 minutes remaining in the third quarter.\nThe Warriors narrowed their deficit to 29-21 with 5 seconds left in the third quarter, when O'Connor swept right and slammed into the end zone with a 2-yard touchdown run. Stanco's PAT pulled Easton within 29-22.\nBratten was sprung for his fifth touchdown of the game, a 37-yard jaunt around the right side with 10:13 left in the game. Harmon handled a bad snap from center and hit Austin Townsend with a two-point conversion pass to make it 37-22.\nEaston closed its deficit again, as O'Connor hit Diontaye Copper on a slant down the middle for a 38-yard touchdown play. O'Connor then connected with Carter Mooney on the two-point conversion pass, drawing Easton with 37-30 with 9:19 to go.\nParkside's running game chewed up almost 6 minutes of clock before a bad snap from center got past Morandi. Barnhart recovered for Easton at the Rams 40. But two plays later, Harmon intercepted O'Connor. The Rams picked up three first downs on the ground to bleed the game's final 3:19.\n\"We've got a really solid core of backs and Karontez ran his tail off. James (Harmon) ran his tail off tonight. Offensive line had a great night up front.\"\nNotes: Parkside finished with 492 yards rushing. ... Parkside's James Harmon carried 11 times for 124 yards. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 11,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
      "explanation": "The article reports on a high school football game, detailing player performances and game outcomes, which directly falls under the Sports category."
    },
