  },
  {
    "title": "Thompson flirts with history as Lions roll",
    "date": "2024-10-13",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Thompson flirts with history as Lions roll\n\n October 13, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1051\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Ja'Dereon Thompson knows a little about Queen Anne's County High's football history.\"When I was in eighth grade, KJ Smothers scored five touchdowns against Parkside one homecoming,\" Thompson said of the former Lions rushing standout.\nAfter rushing for three touchdowns and passing for a fourth — all in the first half — Thompson was lobbying to get back on the field during homecoming Saturday afternoon at Lions Stadium. But with three regular-season games and at least one playoff contest still on the docket, Queen Anne's head coach Brian Mooney opted to keep his electric sophomore on the sideline.\n\"We told him 'We just want to get out of here healthy,'\" Mooney said. \"We haven't had a game yet where we've had no injuries. This was the first one that we got out of with no injuries.\"\nThe Lions not only got away injury free Saturday, but notched their second shutout of the season while extending their winning streak to four via a 45-0 victory over Kent County before a large homecoming crowd.\n\"He's special,\" Mooney said of Thompson, who started his first game at quarterback after the Lions lost starter Lance Arnold to injury in Week 2 and backup Carter Hercher to injury last week. \"Four touchdowns in the first half. He wanted more, but I told him, 'Look man, we got to save you for the next game as we keep going.\"\nMooney's reasoning was understandable, considering the Lions (4-2) prior to Saturday had lost two quarterbacks, both starting offensive tackles, their starting center — who also started at defensive tackle — a defensive end and a cornerback all to injury.\nQueen Anne's second-year head coach had most of his starters on the sideline during the second half after the Lions got off to their best start of the season, taking a 38-0 lead into the locker room.\n\"We talked about kind of starting fast, Mooney said. \"It was something we haven't done all year. Most of the time we're like a second-half team. This time we came out in the first half and executed, and that first drive was a real long drive; took about seven or eight minutes off the clock. We pounded it down, got a touchdown out of it.\"\nAfter receiving the opening kickoff, Queen Anne's ran 14 running plays covering 73 yards and eating close to 8 minutes of clock before senior John Connolly (9 carries, game-high 76 yards rushing) scored on a 2-yard run. Thompson ran in the two-point conversion for an 8-0 lead with 3:38 left in the first quarter.\nConnolly gave the ball back to Queen Anne's, when he intercepted Kent quarterback Dwan Harris on the Trojans' first drive. Thompson (9 carries, 70 yards) dashed 16 yards around right end for a touchdown and Levi Frank added the point-after for a 15-0 lead with 13.2 seconds left in the first quarter.\nQueen Anne's next points were set up by punter Ethan Keppler, who booted a 55-yard punt to the Trojans' 1-yard line. Two plays later, Kent's Jysir Valentine was stacked up and dropped for a safety and a 17-0 lead.\nThompson showed why he's nicknamed \"Flash\" on the ensuing kick, darting through traffic before getting knocked out of bounds at the Kent 16. The Trojans (1-5) looked like they might avoid a deeper deficit, but on a fourth-and-nine, Thompson, weaved his way to a 15-yard touchdown before Frank again hammered the PAT through for a 24-0 lead with 4:47 remaining in the first half.\n\"I think we're playing amazing,\" Thompson said. \"With me at quarterback, it's like Lamar Jackson, and Derrick Henry with John Connolly. And then you've got the blockers. They're stepping up to play. It feels great right now. We're rolling right now.\"\nQueen Anne's continued rolling, scoring on its next two possessions. Chance Hegedus wrestled a ball away from a defender for an 18-yard pass reception from Thompson with 3:07 left in the first half. Thompson added his third rushing touchdown with 26.4 seconds to go in the half, racing untouched around left end for a 10-yard score before another Frank PAT gave the Lions a 38-0 halftime cushion.\nParker Whiteford's 7-yard touchdown run and Frank's fifth extra-point kick pushed Queen Anne's to a 45-0 with 1:15 remaining on a running third-quarter clock.\nWhiteford had a greater impact at inside linebacker, though, leading a defense that held Kent County to 49 yards rushing and 13 yards passing. Queen Anne's stopped seven of the Trojans' eight third-down attempts, forced three punts and one quick kick, had two interceptions, recovered a fumble, and foiled Kent's one fourth-down attempt.\n\"Defensively, I think we've been locked in all year long,\" Whiteford said of a unit that since giving up 35 points in a season-opening loss to defending Class 2A state champion Stephen Decatur — the Seahawks' lowest point total of the season so far — has yielded an average of just 7.4 points a game and notched two shutouts. \"We're going out there doing our jobs. Coach Jude (Mandes) puts us in a good spot to keep growing and be good.\"\nWhiteford attended DeMatha last year, where he played early in the season but saw limited duty after the starting linebacker he replaced returned.\n\"I'm real happy to be back,\" said Whiteford, who finished with nine tackles, three for losses against the Trojans. \"This is the most fun I've had ever playing football. I love playing in front of my home crowd. It's amazing.\"\nNotes: Alex McDonald had Queen Anne's second interception, picking off Harris in the third quarter. … Tyrel Fennel led Kent County with 54 yards on eight carries. … Queen Anne's hosts Easton (4-2) on Thursday at 6 p.m., while Kent County welcomes Washington (0-6).\nCAMBRIDGE-SD 21 WICOMICO 6\nSALISBURY — Senior quarterback Anthony Hughes carried 16 times for 24 yards, but scored a pair of touchdowns Friday as the Vikings remained undefeated at 6-0.\nCOL. RICHARDSON 43 SNOW HILL 6\nSNOW HILL — Quarterback Tarron Hammond ran for two touchdowns and threw for two more Friday as the Colonels evened their record at 3-3.\nHammond had scoring runs of 5 and 3 yards, hit Derrick Mitchell with a 5-yard touchdown pass and connected with Ja'Zir Dickerson for a 45-yard scoring pass.\nDickerson staked Colonel Richardson to an early lead with a 50-yard touchdown run. He later scored on an 8-yard run. Mitchell also rushed for a touchdown, scoring on a 17-yard run. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 13,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
      "explanation": "The article reports on a high school football game, detailing player performance, scores, and coach comments, which aligns directly with the Sports topic."
    },
