  {
    "title": "Smith leads Queen Anne's sweep",
    "date": "2024-10-16",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Smith leads Queen Anne's sweep\n\n October 16, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 780\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n GRASONVILLE — Despite being the two-time defending champion, Nate Smith admitted he's never played his best golf during the annual District 8 championship tournament.The Queen Anne's County High senior continued that trend Tuesday, and even poked fun at himself, noting his strength on this blustery autumn day at Prospect Bay Country Club was hitting his way out of the trees.\n\"I've had a lot of practice,\" Smith said with a slight grin. \"I was basically in the trees every hole so I was pretty used to it. That's the best part of my game.\"\nSmith's mental toughness though may be a notch above his knack for escaping the trees. He certainly showed that quality, as he rallied over the back nine before taking the lead on the second-to-last hole en route to carding a 3-over 75 to win his third consecutive boys individual title by two strokes over Kent County freshman standout Quinten Joiner.\nSmith's third crown also helped Queen Anne's win the district championship for the first time under head coach Brad Niesz, and qualified the Lions team of Smith, Sam Yetman (85), George Cook (92), and girls champion Keira Corcoran (85) for the state tournament. Queen Anne's finished with a 337, two strokes better than runner-up Stephen Decatur.\n\"Just an incredible group of kids that are driven,\" Niesz said. \"Love the game and love to compete.\"\nLeading that group was Smith.\n\"Over the last year the mental part of his game has matured exponentially,\" Queen Anne's head coach Brad Niesz said of Smith. \"He's always been a strong mental player. He's always had all the tools. But his ability to handle adversity on the golf course has been unparalleled.\n\"Golf's a funny game,\" Niesz continued. \"It's basically divided into three parts: off the tee, your approach, and on the green. If you can get all three of those parts clicking you're in business. But it's very rare that that happens.\"\nIt didn't seem to happen for anyone on a course that many think is perhaps the toughest on at least the North Bayside Conference circuit.\nThen there was Joiner, who dropped three birdies on the front nine for a 1-under 35 that gave him a three-stroke lead on Smith at the turn.\n\"Early I was just scrambling for pars,\" said Smith, who had seven pars and two bogeys over the first nine holes. \"I couldn't get anything going. I was putting well, but off the tee I couldn't hit much of anything, or any fairways.\"\nAnd yet the defending champion parred the first three holes on the back nine while Joiner bogeyed two of three, reducing his lead to one. Joiner restored his 3-shot cushion when he birdied 13 as Smith carded a bogey.\nBut back-to-back bogeys by Kent's outstanding freshman allowed Smith to pull even with a par on 14 and his lone birdie of the day on 15. Both made par on 16. Smith took his first lead on the 408-yard, par-4 17th with a par while Joiner had a double bogey.\n\"I kind of got ahead of myself, just got out of my game,\" said Joiner, who was praised for his play and maturity by Niesz. \"That's what happens. You start thinking results, not process.\n\"Putter let me down pretty much the whole way around,\" Joiner added. \"Just struggling on the greens; couldn't really find it. I made a couple good ones. There's just a few … man, it got away out there.\"\nSmith and Joiner both found the trees off the tee on 18. Smith lined his second shot to the left of the water fronting the green. Joiner also delivered an impressive second shot, blasting a ball that was right up against a root, leaving his hands stinging.\nSmith chipped within 8 feet of the cup with his fourth shot, missed a putt that made him wince, before tapping in for bogey and watching Joiner suffer a similar fate, with a miss on a short putt before settling for bogey.\n\"He's a great player,\" Joiner said of Smith. \"He deserves it.\"\nSmith said he was battling nerves, but not just because he was bidding for a third championship.\n\"I was very nervous because I was also playing for my team to go to states,\" Smith said. \"It was very nerve racking.\n\"I just kept grinding,\" Smith said of the back nine. \"Some putts started to fall and my chipping got a little better. I feel like I've got a lot of work to do. I've got to tighten somethings up. Like hitting it straight.\"\nKent Island's Josh David finished third with a 78 to qualify for states, and James M. Bennett's Colton Thomas carded an 83 to finish tied for fifth with Decatur's Jasper Davis. The qualifying cutoff for the boys was 83. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 16,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
