  {
    "title": "Lions win North despite tie with Bucs",
    "date": "2024-10-17",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Lions win North despite tie with Bucs\n\n October 17, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 694\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Queen Anne's County High girls head soccer coach Mike Kern didn't pop out of gear or launch into a tirade.But with his team trailing and doing everything but sticking the ball in the net Wednesday night against Kent Island, Kern was a bit more animated than usual as he pleaded for more.\n\"I just told them we had to play harder,\" Kern said. \"We were knocking on the doorstep the entire first half. I thought we dominated the game. We had more opportunities to score and we just couldn't get it in the net. I just told them they had to play with a little more heart.\"\nIn another physical battle that has become commonplace in this rivalry, Queen Anne's got the equalizing goal it needed, when Shannon Rath buried a penalty kick into the right side of the net in the third minute of the second half in an eventual 1-1 double-overtime tie.\nWhile the Lions and Buccaneers each finished the regular season with identical records — 9-2-1 overall, 6-0-1 North Bayside — Queen Anne's wins the tiebreaker between the two schools based on its 1-0 victory over Kent Island on Sept. 11. Though that first meeting did not count toward the North division, it was the deciding factor since head-to-head competition is the first tiebreaker.\nQueen Anne's faces two-time defending conference champion James M. Bennett (12-0) for the Bayside championship on Monday at Snow Hill High School at 6:45 p.m. It will be the fourth consecutive year the Lions and Clippers decide the conference championship. Bennett beat Queen Anne's, 3-0, on Sept. 30 in Centreville.\n\"I thought Olivia (Ryan) played her best game of the season,\" Kern said of Wednesday's clash with Kent Island. \"So I think we're starting to peak a little bit. We're pretty confident. I think going against (head coach) Ed O'Leary's (Bennett) team, they know us well, we know them well. I think it's going to be a good battle.\n\"I think this is one of the more talented teams we've ever had,\" Kern continued. \"I mean we can play with anybody as far from 18 to 18. We've struggled to get the ball in the back of the net against these tougher teams. But we can defend. Bennett when we played them last time, we lost 3-0, but the score didn't depict the game. We lost on the scoreboard. But we got to get the ball in the back of the net.\"\nQueen Anne's had three direct kicks and three corner kicks in the first 17 minutes against the Bucs, but could not beat Kent Island keeper Taylor Ritz. Then with 15 minutes left in the half, Buccaneers speedster Abbie Mahaffey — with Queen Anne's Rath trying to shield her from the ball and Lions keeper Bea Holthaus moving toward the ball from the opposite direction — managed to chip a shot that floated over Holthaus for a 1-0 Kent Island lead.\n\"I think it was just a miscommunication,\" Kern said of the play. \"Shannon running toward her shielding the ball, and Bea coming out. And I think Abbie just made a good play to poke a foot in there and poke it around her. It was just communication.\"\nKent Island, which stretched its unbeaten streak to nine games after a 1-2 start to the season, held that lead into halftime, despite the Lions swarming near the goal with less than 2 minutes left in the first half in a bid for the tying goal and Ritz away from the net. But the Bucs defense denied Queen Anne's, carrying the 1-0 lead into the break.\nThe Lions put immediate pressure on Kent Island early in the second half, sending a header over the crossbar. Then with almost 3 minutes elapsed, Kara Ringold was fouled just inside the box, setting up Rath, who roped a shot into the right side past Ritz's dive.\nTwo yellow cards against the Bucs gave Queen Anne's two more direct-kick chances, but the first sailed wide right and the second was stopped by Ritz.\nThe Lions came within inches of taking the lead when a shot hit under the crossbar and fell in front of the goal with 9:20 remaining in regulation. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 17,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
      "explanation": "The article reports on a high school soccer game, detailing teams, scores, coaches, and upcoming matches, which aligns directly with the Sports topic."
    },
    "llm_classification_meta": {
      "model": "gpt-oss:120b",
      "llm_failed": false
    },
    "entities_people": "[\"Shannon Rath\", \"Ed O'Leary\", \"Olivia Ryan\"]",
    "metadata_sport": "soccer",
    "metadata_story_type": "game recap",
    "metadata_location": "Centreville, Maryland",
    "metadata_teams": "[\"Queen Anne's County High\", \"Kent Island\"]",
    "metadata_schools": "[\"Queen Anne's County High\", \"Kent Island\"]",
    "entities_quoted_people": "[\"coach Mike Kern\"]",
    "metadata_level_of_play": "high school",
    "metadata_competition_type": "regular season",
    "metadata_outcome": "1-1 tie",
    "metadata_importance": 8,
    "metadata_audience_blurb": "Local high-school sports fans and families; readers interested in Queen Anne's County High athletics"
  },
  {
    "title": "Webb, Stepp score late, lift Sabres over Herons for third straight title",
    "date": "2024-10-17",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Webb, Stepp score late, lift Sabres over Herons for third straight title\n\n October 17, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 824\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — It had gone undefeated in conference play this season, outscoring its six opponents by a 58-1 margin.So it was easy to think Thursday's Eastern Shore Independent Athletic Conference title game was a mere gimme for Saints Peter and Paul High's field hockey team.\nIt was anything but.\nThe two-time defending champ was in a scoreless stalemate at halftime. It nudged ahead in the third, only to be tied 55 seconds into the fourth quarter. But Angie Webb and Jules Stepp scored a little more than 2 minutes apart down the stretch to give the Sabres a 3-1 victory over a stubborn Gunston squad for its third consecutive ESIAC championship.\n\"They came to play,\" Sts. Peter & Paul head coach Debbie McQuaid said of the Herons, who lost both regular-season games to the Sabres by a combined 11-1 score. \"We knew it was going to be hard. Gunston's gotten better. We've gotten better. Both teams have been playing some out-of-conference strong teams. So I expected them to come at me with all they had. And they did.\"\nDespite having a 5-1 advantage in penalty corners in the first half, Sts. Peter & Paul (11-5) was held scoreless in the first half by a conference opponent for the first time all year, as Gunston junior standout Layla Kent seemed to be everywhere at once.\n\"The stress was really high,\" Stepp said. \"We do a prayer before every single game. Then we came together (at halftime) and we prayed again.\"\nThe Sabres earned three more corners in the first seven minutes of the third quarter, but couldn't dent the scoreboard. But SSPP made its fourth corner of the period count, as Webb unloaded a sweeping drive that buzzed by Herons goalie Loreli deGour with 6:52 remaining in the third period for a 1-0 lead.\n\"It was a reverse chip,\" Webb said. \"I saw the (defender) where she was, and I knew I had to pull around her. So I just pulled it to the left and just took the shot. I was aiming for the corner. I was just happy to get the corner in.\"\nJust 55 seconds into the fourth quarter though, Gunston (9-4) pieced together a concerted push that got behind SSPP goalie Pyper Mister and finished with Kate Zottarelli's goal into the left side of the cage.\n\"We've been working so much more on our mental game this season, and we've had so many more games,\" Gunston assistant Jen Matthews said. \"It's just given us a lot more opportunities to connect as a team, work as a team. We really peaked at the right time of the season.\"\nWebb though struck again with 5:18 remaining.\n\"I knew we needed a goal,\" Webb said. \"I didn't want to go into overtime. I knew we needed to get a goal and I just felt the adrenaline rush of wanting to score. Just took a shot when I needed to. We worked well as a team to get it off.\"\nGunston got tagged with a 2-minute card with just under 4 minutes remaining, and the Sabres capitalized, as Webb sent a smash toward the goal that Stepp — in a move that looked like an act of self defense — made a beautiful deflection for a 3-1 lead.\n\"I was so surreal, because Angie cranked a shot off from the top and it was up in the air,\" Stepp said. \"I didn't hear a whistle so at first I thought it was a corner. But she ripped the shot off and it was coming at me so my initial reaction was hit at the ball in some way, shape of form. And it just deflected off right into the goal. It was amazing. I've never done that in my whole career, and it was just perfect to have it happen in our championship game.\"\nDespite losing to the Sabres for a third straight year, Matthews and Gunston head coach Yvonne Gazelle gushed over their team's play.\n\"They did amazing,\" said Gazelle, who in addition to Kent's outstanding all-around play, got a big effort from Grace Shepherd, who is bound for Central Michigan University. \"I'm super proud of these girls.\"\nIt was Sts. Peter & Paul's fourth championship in five years.\n\"The last two goals were simply amazing,\" McQuaid said. \"Happy for Jules and Angie; the finish.\"\nMcQuaid acknowledged her past two championship teams had a tendency to lean heavily on Webb's older sister, Gennie Webb, now playing for Virginia Commonwealth University.\n\"It was not just one person; we relied on her a lot,\" McQuaid said of Gennie Webb. \"This year I have a group of girls, who played as a unit and supported each other. And they were fabulous.\"\nNotes: Kent was named the ESIAC player of the year, and along with teammates Grace Shepherd, Gabi Shepherd and Izzy Labrador, was named to the all-ESIAC first-team. … Sts. Peter & Paul's Webb, Stepp, Reagan Wolters, Lucy Rankin, Kat Murphy, and Claire Bieber all earned first-team honors. SSPP's Caroline Corson was an honorable mention pick. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
