  {
    "title": "Warriors rally three times before edging Lions",
    "date": "2024-10-18",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Warriors rally three times before edging Lions\n\n October 18, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 592\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n It may not have matched the significance of the 2014 playoff between the two schools, but on an emotional level it came close.Mikey O'Connor hit Chase Raab with a 10-yard touchdown pass with 1 minute, 21 seconds remaining, and Zach Barnhart made his second interception with 53 seconds left Thursday night, as Easton High's football team rallied for a 27-26 victory over Queen Anne's County at Lions Stadium in Centreville.\nThe victory strengthened Easton's No. 2 seeding in the approaching Class 2A East Region playoffs behind undefeated and defending 2A state champion Stephen Decatur.\n\"For coach (Al) Waters and the rest of the guys that joined the staff it meant a lot for us to get a win there,\" Easton head coach Matt Griffith said Friday of Waters, his defensive coordinator who was a one-time head coach at Queen Anne's.\n\"But more importantly with that, we had a little bump in the road; two-game losing streak,\" Griffith continued. \"And a young group of kids who are still trying to figure out to be mature on the football field and learn how to win. And they learned how to win last night. We could have folded the tent a few times.\"\nWinners of four straight, Queen Anne's (4-3) jumped to a 14-0 first-quarter lead. DeRodney Smith (15 carries, 101 yards) scored on a 21-yard touchdown run, but Easton's Logan Friend blocked the extra point. Ja'Dereon Thompson then intercepted O'Connor and made an 84-yard touchdown return. Harris ran in the two-point conversion for a two-touchdown lead.\nEaston (5-2) trimmed its deficit to 14-7, when O'Connor (12 of 20, 178 yards, three touchdowns, interception) connected with Colin Mooney for a 27-yard touchdown pass with 4:27 left in the first quarter. Dominic Stanco tacked on the extra point.\nO'Connor's 1-yard touchdown run around left end and Stanco's second point-after kick pulled the Warriors even at 14-14 just 55 seconds into the second quarter.\nBut Queen Anne's responded, as John Connolly (12 carries, 101 yards) scored on an 8-yard run with 3:25 left in the first half for a 20-14 lead. Easton's Nathan Lapp blocked the extra point.\nBarnhart intercepted Lions quarterback Carter Hercher with 30 seconds left in the first half, setting up O'Connor's touchdown pass to Diontaye Copper with 10 seconds to go before halftime. Stanco's PAT gave Easton a 21-20 lead.\nThe Warriors took the opening kickoff and drove 77 yards to the Queen Anne's 1, where they fumbled, the ball recovered by the Lions' Amari Baynard.\nQueen Anne's put together a 97-yard drive that ended with Connolly's 3-yard touchdown run for a 26-21 lead. The Lions' two-point bid was stopped.\nEaston regained the lead at 27-26, when O'Connor hit Raab with a 10-yard touchdown pass in the left side of the end zone with 1:25 left.\nBarnhart then intercepted Hercher a second time with 46 seconds remaining to cement the Warriors' win.\n\"They were poised,\" Griffith said of his team. \"Mikey made some plays. All the receivers made some plays. Dustin Blue was unbelievable (16 carries, season-high 101 yards).\"\nThen there was Barnhart.\n\"He's probably got one of our best football IQs on the team, and defensively makes a big difference out there for us,\" Griffith said of Barnhart. \"The last two weeks he's been playing lights out. He's just going to keep getting better each week.\"\nQueen Anne's linebacker Parker Whiteford set a school single-game record for tackles with 18, breaking the mark set by Kevin Morris in 2000.\nEaston defeated Queen Anne's 21-14 in the Class 2A East Region semifinals in 2014, when Jaelon Moaney hit Dre' Stevens with a 41-yard touchdown pass with 2 minutes left. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 18,
