  {
    "title": "Sabres finish strong on way to crown",
    "date": "2024-10-18",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Sabres finish strong on way to crown\n\n October 18, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 767\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Keely Morgan's goal just 2 seconds before halftime put the home team in front.But it was Eden Zimmerman's goal off Avery McCall's beautiful corner kick that put Saints Peter and Paul High's girls soccer team finally in control.\nThat score gave the top-seeded Sabres an infusion of confidence Friday en route to a 4-1 victory over Gunston in the Eastern Shore Independent Athletic Conference championship.\n\"It feels amazing,\" said McCall, a junior midfielder, who has now been a part of conference title teams in soccer, basketball and lacrosse. \"This is a very special team.\"\nA team that had been bounced in the first round of the ESIAC postseason the past two years. A team that went undefeated in conference play this season and came in as the No. 1 seed. And a team that won its first ESIAC championship since 2019.\nBut the Sabres (10-3) were consistently beaten to the ball by the Herons (6-5-1) in the first half, and halfway through the second half.\n\"We were definitely dominant in the first half and the first 20 minutes of the second half,\" Gunston head coach Tony D'Antonio said. \"And then it was pretty even after that. They found the net and we didn't.\"\nSts. Peter & Paul found the net in the 8th minute, when Emery Zimmerman parked a direct kick into the right side of the net for a 1-0 lead.\nGunston got a pair of corner kicks in the 18th minute, the first skidding through the crease but finding no one on the other end. Sabres keeper Kaylin Palmer made a save on a direct kick with 13:30 left in the first half.\nThe Herons then put together a flurry off a corner with 5 minutes remaining before the break. A corner kick sailed over the Palmer's outstretched fingers and through the crease. Gunston kept possession, striking a ball that hit one of its own players before sending a shot over the crossbar.\nThen with 2:04 left before halftime, Ellie Roberts sent a drive from about 25 yards out that nestled into the far right corner for a 1-1 tie.\n\"It's hard to beat a team three times,\" McCall said of the Sabres' two regular-season wins over Gunston. \"But I think we were mostly in panic mode. They (Gunston) always come out really fast and we're normally a second-half team. So we were very defensive and I think that's what saved us. And then eventually we got the offense together and it all came together.\"\nSts. Peter & Paul got a huge boost just 2 seconds before halftime when Morgan got a ball and tucked it inside the left post for a 2-1 halftime lead.\n\"It was absolutely huge,\" Sabres head coach Nicole Edsall said of Morgan's goal.\nSophomore Emery Zimmerman agreed.\n\"I think that was essential because it really just got us going,\" Emery Zimmerman said. \"It (made us) realize, 'OK, we need to win this. We want to win this. This is ours. We came all this way undefeated (in conference play). This is the time that we need to come out and score.\n\"When they caught up to us and we got back up one more like right off the bat, it really got us going,\" Emery Zimmerman continued. \"Gave us a lot of enthusiasm and momentum.\"\nBut not right away.\n\"They definitely had jitters in the first half,\" Edsall said of her team. \"And when we talked at halftime I said, 'I know this isn't the team that we are. So just kind of brush it off. Let it go. Start the half over and let's play our game.'\"\nPlay evened out midway through the second half. Then with 8:42 left, McCall bent a corner that plopped right in front of Eden Zimmerman, who tapped it in for a 3-1 lead.\n\"I've been struggling on my corners all season,\" McCall said. \"I usually just boot it and hope. Eden is always everywhere and she just found it. It got the curve that it needed and she was just there for it. We've always been a team that struggles to like get it in (on corners). It's always gone right through. But she was just right there where she needed to be.\"\nLess than 2 minutes later, the Sabres spilled into the box before Kendall Kroniser gained possession, spun away from a defender and drilled in a fourth goal.\n\"I'm sure it's great for the seniors,\" Emery Zimmerman said. \"I'm happy for them. I feel over the last year nobody leaving (through graduation) and through this year, we really built up on our skills and built great team chemistry. And this is what happens when you work hard at practice.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 18,
