  {
    "title": "Marks, Queen Anne's run into record book",
    "date": "2024-10-23",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Marks, Queen Anne's run into record book\n\n October 23, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 925\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CAMBRIDGE — Queen Anne's County High's Ben Marks wasn't thrilled with his time, yet still ran into the school record book.So did Queen Anne's County's girls team and Parkside's Logan Newton.\nAnd then there was Kent Island's boys, who added to their historic run Wednesday by winning their 15th team championship in 18 years during the annual Bayside Conference cross country championships at Cambridge-South Dorchester's jungle course.\n\"It's definitely really cool,\" said Marks after becoming the first Queen Anne's runner — male or female — to win back-to-back individual titles in school history. \"I'm extremely blessed and honored to be the Bayside record-holder and all that stuff. I wanted to do something special today, but it just was not the plan for the day.\"\nMarks set a course and meet record last year here, when he clocked a winning time of 15 minutes, 17.94 seconds in his first season running cross country.\nThe senior moved to the front of the pack early yesterday on an unseasonable warm afternoon, broke from the first section of trees with the lead, and never relinquished it en route to covering the 3-mile course in a winning time of 16:06.51 — 45 seconds ahead of runner-up Tekai Drummond of Cambridge-SD.\nBut the defending champion was far from pleased.\n\"Today was not necessarily my best day to say the least,\" Marks said. \"I went out with intention and it just didn't go the way I wanted it to.\n\"Good start, but it didn't feel amazing,\" Marks continued. \"I don't have excuses. I guess the woods slowed me up a little bit today; nobody there with me pushing. So after a certain point in the race I was like, 'OK, this is not going right at all. Let's check the box and focus on a little more training before I head into the more important (regional and state) races.'\"\nThe race for second may have been the best duel of the meet, as Cambridge-SD's Drummond and Mardela's Connor McIntyre charged down the stretch side by side, with Drummond (16:51.28) crossing the finish line just ahead of McIntyre (16:51.64).\nKent Island won its third straight boys crown, totaling 51 points to second-place Stephen Decatur's 82. But with Bucs' No. 1 runner Gunner Hansard unsure if he was going to run because of an ankle injury, second-year head coach Andrew McIntire stressed the importance of a pack mentality to his team prior to taking its place on the starting line.\n\"It was a last-minute decision and he decided to run,\" McIntire said of Hansard, who was Kent Island's second runner to finish, placing ninth in 17:39.94. \"I told them before the race, 'We're going to need a close gap between one and five today.'\"\nThe Bucs did just that, as its top five runners finished within 47 seconds of each other.\n\"That's about as good as it's going to get,\" McIntire said. \"We've always run in a bunch. We have a lot of great runners and they like to run together, and it paid off. And it's paid off all season long.\"\nLeading the way was senior Giovanni Aguirre, who was approached by Hansard before the race.\n\"When he looked at me and he was like, 'Hey my ankle's really bad. I need you to be able to take my spot,\" Aguirre said of Hansard. \"And I managed to get up there. It was pretty fulfilling knowing I didn't let my team down. I had to beat one runner, and he almost got me at the end.\"\nAguirre finished fifth (17:19.20) edging Decatur's Braydon Bell (17:19.36). Following Aguirre and Hansard across the finish line for Kent Island were Caleb Heater (10th, 17:41.42), Cole Fury (12th, 17:52.58) and Jacob Keever (15th, 18:06.70).\nThough its top five runners were separated by less than 4 minutes, Queen Anne's girls finished in a tight enough bunch to win their first team title since 2007 with 51 points, edging Decatur by one.\n\"This group of girls have worked so hard,\" Queen Anne's head coach Kim Agee said. \"And when they get in a race we talk about what they need to do beforehand, and they get out there and just fight for it. It's been so impressive. I've loved watching them.\"\nLeading the charge was junior Faith Gordon, who was among the leaders for a good portion of the race before Parkside's Newton and Snow Hill's Allie Suarezapecheche pulled away to finish first and second, respectively.\n\"Usually before my races I kind of dread them,\" Gordon said. \"And then after them I'm like, 'That was really fun.' Today I felt really excited. I was like, 'I have a good feeling about this today.' I'm kind of beating myself up that I didn't get first place, but I know I did my best. I think I could have run faster.\"\nGordon finished third in 20:31.03, almost a full 30 seconds behind Newton (20:01.04), who became the first runner in Parkside's history — male or female — to win back-to-back individual titles at Baysides. Suarezapecheche was second in 20:20.37.\nHelping Queen Anne's to the championship were Addison Holden (8th, 21:12.82), Riley Coleman (13th, 23:07.67); RJ Ensor (23:16.67) and Emmalyn Dutton (21st, 24:12.90).\nPlacing in the top 10 were Cambridge-SD's Camryn Russum (5th, 20:50.76) and North Caroline's Brooke Sullivan (7th, 21:11.36).\nFinishing in the top 10 on the boys side were Cambridge-SD's Josiah Harris (4th, 17:11.27) and North Caroline's Darly Ortega-Miguel (8th, 17:31.14). Cambridge-SD (97 points) was third in the boys field followed by North Caroline (4th, 114), Queen Anne's (5th, 129), and Easton (6th, 149).\nNorth Caroline (81) was third in the girls competition followed by Kent Island (97), Parkside (108) and Cambridge-SD (119). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
