  {
    "title": "Orrell nets equalizer, game-winner to lift Easton over Rams in double overtime",
    "date": "2024-10-24",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Orrell nets equalizer, game-winner to lift Easton over Rams in double overtime\n\n October 24, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 614\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n SALISBURY — It was a Warrior's cry.\"The story's not done,\" declared a member of Easton High's field hockey team before breaking the pregame huddle.\nThe Warriors did indeed extend their 2024 story Thursday afternoon. And as usual — at least when it comes to chapters with big games — they needed more than the usual amount of time to finish and deliver a happy ending.\nOlivia Orrell scored the equalizer late in the fourth period, then knocked in the game-winner 1 minute, 52 seconds into the second overtime yesterday, lifting Easton to a 2-1 victory over No. 2 seed Parkside in a Class 2A East II semifinal.\nWinners of six straight, the third-seeded Warriors (10-3) advance to Tuesday's 2A East II final at top-seeded Stephen Decatur (11-3).\n\"These girls never give up,\" said Easton head coach Kim Neff, whose team has played beyond regulation four times this season, losing a shootout to two-time Bayside champion Queen Anne's and beating perennial conference heavyweights Pocomoke and Kent Island in OT before yesterday's double-overtime thriller. \"And as exhausted as they were, they never ever give up.\"\nBut Easton could have.\nAfter a scoreless first half, the Warriors earned four penalty corners in the third period but couldn't beat Rams goalie Eden Hirsch. Easton continued applying pressure.\nThen with 7:20 left in regulation, Parkside's Ava Holland converted a pass from Mady Bleile for a 1-0 Rams lead.\nNeff immediately called time.\n\"I called time because I knew they were going to get in their own heads with that goal,\" Neff said of her team. \"I knew I had to bring them back together as as unit and tell them we had seven minutes (in regulation). That's plenty of time to score and get ahead. And sure enough, they took that and within three minutes they were able to put one in the goal.\"\nEaston surged into the circle and earned another corner. Hirsch made an initial save on the play. But Ryan Quicke gained possession and got the ball to Orrell, who tucked into the right corner of the cage with 3:52 remaining in regulation.\n\"When they scored the first goal, it put a drive in us to like dig deep,\" Orrell said. \"Coach said we had it and she knew we had the energy. We just had the discipline. And the discipline I think as a team really got us through the game.\"\nEaston's defense kept it a 1-1 tie through the first 10-minute overtime.\nFreshman goalie Taylor Bowen (five saves) kicked away a sweeping backhand from Bleile 1:05 into the extra session. The Rams earned back-to-back corners but were denied, with Addie Knox making a clear on the second corner. Easton's Bis Johnson then swept away a ball inches from the goal line with 2 minutes remaining.\nHirsch answered, making a big save just 15 seconds later, forcing the game into a second OT.\nThe Warriors got two corners early, and capitalized on the second, as Orrell continuously hit at the ball before sneaking it into the back of the cage for the winner.\n\"Too many,\" Orrell answered when asked how many times she hit the ball before scoring. \"We were really determined. We just got it in. It was great.\"\nThe Rams, who shut out Easton earlier in the season, finish 9-4.\n\"I think it was a great game from both sides,\" Parkside head coach Stephanie Cason said. \"It just didn't fall in our favor.\"\nKENT ISLAND 4 NORTH CAROLINE 0\nSTEVENSVILLE — Willow Yost scored two goals and set up a third as second-seeded Kent Island blanked the No. 3 seed Bulldogs.\nThe second-seeded Buccaneers (9-3-1) played at top-seed Queen Anne's (11-1-1) in the region final on Tuesday.\nEllie Burnside and Parker Tengwall each scored once for Kent Island. Goalie Brynja Petursson made seven saves. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
