  {
    "title": "Burnham has Vikings seemingly in all the right places",
    "date": "2024-10-25",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Burnham has Vikings seemingly in all the right places\n\n October 25, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 467\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Rob Burnham deflects praise quite nicely.\"We got great kids. I'm not a good coach,\" Cambridge-South Dorchester High's first-year head football coach said. \"All I've done is put people in places to be successful. I haven't done anything fancy.\"\nWell, after Thursday night's 42-0 road victory at Washington, the Vikings have a fancy 8-0 record — the first time they've been unbeaten this deep into a season since 2000.\nDespite his self evaluation, Burnham has proven to be a pretty good head coach, and has certainly put his personnel in the right spots offensively, as Cambridge-SD has outscored its last five opponents, 187-14.\nLeading that charge has been junior running back Sterling Perkins, who has rushed for 902 yards and 10 touchdowns, and senior quarterback Anthony Hughes, who has 733 yards and a team-high 12 touchdowns on the ground, and has completed over 50% of his passes for 355 yards and seven touchdowns — two of those coming Thursday against the Jaguars.\nBut the Vikings have been equally, if not more impressive, on defense.\n\"The defense is definitely the strength of our team without question,\" Burnham said of a unit that has four players — KaMarii Stanley, Christen Dockins, Antonio Blueford and Tabrion Watts — with over 40 tackles apiece.\nHard to argue that considering Cambridge-SD has surrendered just one offensive touchdown over its last five games — that coming on an 8-yard run by Wicomico — and has shut out three of its last five opponents, including its last two.\n\"We've done pretty well defensively holding people,\" Burnham said. \"Our kids play fast. We tackle well and drive the pile backwards. And we run fast to it.\"\nThat unit figures to face its stiffest test of the season next Friday when longtime rival Easton (5-2) comes to Cambridge for the regular-season finale for both teams.\n\"Easton brings a quality opponent to Cambridge next week,\" Burnham said. \"They have some talent on offense that's pretty special and we have to be able to contain that. Our kids are going to be ready to play.\"\nBurnham liked the way his team started for the most part against winless Washington. The Vikings scored on their first possession, committed a turnover, then scored the next three times they had the ball while building a 35-0 halftime lead.\nHughes was an efficient 4 of 5 passing for 65 yards and two touchdowns before being relieved by Nathan Twilley, who also had a scoring toss. Blueford, Ra'Quan Cornish and Tamyron Evans each had touchdown receptions, and William Jackson and Watts had one rushing touchdown apiece.\nStill, Burnham said there's work to be done offensively.\n\"We have things we need to clean up offensively,\" Burnham said. \"We're not anywhere near where I want to be offensively.\n\"Overall we blocked well. We finished well,\" Burnham said of Thursday's win. \"But we have not played a complete game yet.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 25,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
