  {
    "title": "Rival clashes feature various degrees of confidence",
    "date": "2024-10-29",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Rival clashes feature various degrees of confidence\n\n October 29, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 784\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n They each come into rivalry week with varying degrees of confidence.After getting shutout by defending Class 2A state champion Stephen Decatur on Oct. 11, Kent Island High's football team rides a two-game win streak into Friday's annual clash with county rival Queen Anne's.\nThe Lions come into Friday having won five of six, their latest, a 21-20 victory over Parkside eight days after losing a one-point heartbreaker to Easton.\nStreaking Colonel Richardson readies for its annual duel with North Caroline having outscored its last four opponents, 203-20.\nAnd though they are still searching for their first win of the season, the Bulldogs fell just two points shy of Kent Island but have senior star Zymear Smith back and running.\n\"I can see some progress that we've made,\" said Heath Wilkins, who became North Caroline's head coach just four weeks ago. \"I wish I had the team for more time, but I don't, so this is what we've got.\"\nFor the second time in Wilkins' three games as head coach, the Bulldogs (0-8) held a halftime lead last Friday. Corey Bunce's 4-yard touchdown run and Caden Deavers' two-point conversion run gave North Caroline an 8-0 lead on Kent Island, which had a field-goal attempt blocked before the half.\nBut the Bucs (5-3) rebounded in the second half, as Nathan Green scored on runs of 14 and 38 yards to help push Kent Island to a 16-8 lead.\nCaroline pulled within 16-14, when Bunce connected with tight end Declan Egan for a short touchdown pass in the fourth quarter. But the Bucs stopped North Caroline short on the two-point try, then picked up a couple of first downs over the final 4 minutes to melt the clock and seal the win.\n\"I think we've taken some steps,\" said Kent Island first-year head coach Zach Davis, who got a 16-carry, 123-yard rushing effort from Jaxson Hess against the Bulldogs. \"We've been a streaky team; win three, lose three, and then we've won two.\n\"This team has been a work in progress, figuring out how do we have success because a lot of these guys are new starters,\" Davis added. \"We bring back a ton of starters next year. But we're a young football team. Those growing pains showed in the middle of the season. We're getting better and that's what you want to see.\"\nQueen Anne's head coach Brian Mooney has learned a lot about pain this season after losing a slew of starters to injuries, and then falling to Easton, 27-26, when Mikey O'Connor hit Chase Raab with a late touchdown pass on a fourth-and-five.\n\"The Easton game was a gut punch,\" Mooney said.\nBut it was the Lions delivering the gut punch last Friday by way of a 21-20 victory over Parkside.\nDown 7-0, Queen Anne's (5-3) pulled even on John Connolly's 28-yard touchdown run in the second quarter. The Rams moved back in front in the third quarter, but again Connolly (season-high 27 carries, 156 yards rushing) answered, scoring on a 3-yard run. But Parkside maintained a slim lead when the Lions missed the point-after attempt.\nParkside extended its lead to 20-13 on a 70-yard touchdown pass. The Lions again looked poised to respond, only to fumble the ball away inside the Rams' 5-yard line. But Queen Anne's senior linebacker Parker Whiteford came through with one of the season's biggest plays when he made a tackle for a safety, drawing the Lions with 20-15.\nAided by Ja'Dereon Thompson's 33-yard completion to Jamison Woodward, Queen Anne's drove 60 yards, with Thompson scoring on a 4-yard run with a little over a minute to play giving the Lions a 21-20 victory.\n\"It was a nail-biter for sure,\" Mooney said. \"It's huge. A confidence booster. Just again the resiliency. But we're going to scratch and claw just like we have all year.\"\nThough is took a little while, Colonel Richardson appears to have found its groove.\nJa'Zir Dickerson carried 10 times for 175 yards and three touchdowns, and Derrick Mitchell had 100 yards on 11 touches during last Friday's 44-0 rout of Kent County, the Colonels' fourth straight win.\n\"We coach them each week to stay true to themselves and follow the system that we run,\" said Colonel head coach James Jackson, his team now 5-3. \"Our backs I feel like are finally starting to follow our linemen, not trying to kick out too soon, or over-run the hole.\"\nWhile the Colonel's running attack has impressed over the past four weeks, so has its defense, notching a pair of shutouts.\n\"Can we be better? Yes,\" Jackson said. \"I think we need better play from our two d-tackles. Our d-ends have to contain a little better. Our linebackers, we've got to flow a little better. But the last four weeks they've been bent but they haven't been broken.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 29,
