  {
    "title": "One is enough for Queen Anne's",
    "date": "2024-10-30",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "One is enough for Queen Anne's\n\n October 30, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 711\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CENTREVILLE — Neither one needed a lesson on the importance of scoring first.\"The first team that scores is more likely to win than the second,\" Kent Island High head field hockey coach Laura Harding said.\n\"Getting that early goal, it sets the tone,\" Queen Anne's County head coach Shana Corder said. \"And I knew going into this game, I knew both teams were going to come out to win it in regulation.\"\nQueen Anne's and Kent Island played to a scoreless, double-overtime tie Oct. 19 before each team scored twice in a shootout in their regular-season finale.\nIt didn't take that long Tuesday afternoon.\nHaley Cole's goal on a corner just 7 minutes in proved enough, as the Lions blanked Kent Island, 1-0, for their second straight Class 2A East Region I championship.\nQueen Anne's (12-1-1) is seeded third in the 2A state tournament and will host No. 6 seed Centenniel (11-3) on Thursday at 4 p.m. in a quarterfinal.\n\"We're successful on our corners because our defense is so amazing,\" said Corder, whose team has won back-to-back Bayside Conference and regional titles for the first time in the program's history. \"And it pays off for us. And all you need is one to go in. And honestly going forward, this is how the state tournament's going to go. One goal is going to get it.\"\nOne goal was all that was needed on this beautiful autumn afternoon.\nQueen Anne's earned two corners in the game's first 5 minutes, then got a third with 7:20 remaining in the first quarter. Jordan Bilbo sent an entry pass to Ellee McLean, who quickly sent the ball back Bilbo. Positioned to the right of the goal, Bilbo centered a pass to an unmarked Cole about five yards from the cage. Cole didn't hesitate, quickly sweeping shot under the lunge of Bucs goalie Brynja Petursson and into the left side of the cage for a 1-0 lead.\n\"We've been working really hard on corner execution, and every practice it's been getting better and better,\" Cole said. \"And I got the perfect pass from Jordan and happened to be the one. It was really a team effort. We were all in there to get it done and earn the corner. I just happened to be the one lucky to tap it in.\"\nThat was plenty for Queen Anne's defense.\nSince yielding a goal to Crisfield on Oct. 7, the Lions haven't surrendered a goal over their last six games — not counting the 1 vs. 1 against the Bucs in the regular-season finale. Corder again played Cassidy Alexander (four saves) and Caroline Kimball (two saves) two quarters each Tuesday. And even though Sydnie Harris — one of the new mainstays on a rebuilt defense — was not 100%, she played tough minutes along with linemates Sydnee Carr and Mel Gill. Corder dropped forward Aly Edwards back on defense when Harris needed a breather.\n\"I just knew if we scored first I knew we could do it because my defense is amazing, and they're peaking,\" Corder said of a unit that has now logged 10 shutouts this season. \"They are peaking right now. They've done well all season.\n\"I had to put one of my forwards back to defense,\" Corder said of Edwards. \"But the kids listen to what we tell them in practice and they know everybody's role. So we're just able to be successful even being sick. I'm super proud of the girls.\"\nHarding was also proud of her defense, which limited an opponent to one goal or less for the 11th time this season. Petursson made seven saves, including a stop McLean's penalty stroke with 7:45 left in the second quarter, and made another save when the Bucs were down a man for 2 minutes in the third quarter.\n\"We made the save and we battled back on all the corners down here in the first half,\" said Harding, whose team stopped Queen Anne's on 10 of its 11 corners.\nKent Island (9-4-1) almost pulled even with 2:50 remaining, when a drive in the circle missed connecting with Ellie Burnside at the post.\n\"They have a good defense,\" Harding said of Queen Anne's. \"And they've got a great goalie. It's going to be a big battle again next year, too. I think we'll be right back here again next year.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 10,
    "day": 30,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
      "explanation": "The article reports on a high school field hockey game, focusing on scores, coaches, and player performance, which aligns directly with the Sports topic."
    },
