  },
  {
    "title": "QA's Marks runs off with 2nd straight regional crown",
    "date": "2024-11-01",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "QA's Marks runs off with 2nd straight regional crown\n\n November 1, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 615\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Ben Marks has been in races this cross country season where he's been challenged.The Queen Anne's County High senior has also been in races this autumn where half a mile in, the only one pushing him was himself.\nThursday at the Fair Hill Race Track in Elkton, Marks separated himself from the field early on the way to winning his second straight Class 2A East Region boys individual title in 17 minutes, 01.04 seconds.\n\"He's very strong,\" Queen Anne's head coach Kim Agee said of Marks during a phone interview Friday. \"He's looking good. His biggest goal is obviously states. So he's just focused on that. He knows he has some really big competition there, and he knows that he can run with them. So he has just been really focused and training for that moment; for those hills.\"\nMarks will bid to become the school's first state cross country champion next Saturday, when he competes in the 2A state championship at Hereford High in Parkton at 1:30 p.m. Marks ran a 15.48.90 at last year's state meet, finishing second to Arundel's Eric Penkala, who won in 15:47.80.\nMarks was in control early Thursday and finished over a full minute ahead of Fallston's Nicholas Spangler, who was runner-up in 18:01.16.\n\"A few times he uses a (regular season) dual meet as a speed practice,\" Agee said when Marks isn't challenged. \"Sometimes he will pace himself for a bit with the group and then he'll move out and move ahead. He is super aware that he needs that competition there. So he often in the race tries to get into a mental place to push himself.\"\nKent Island was the only boys team to qualify for states, finishing third with 91 points behind champion C. Milton Wright (83) and Fallston (85).\nPacing the Bucs was Gunner Hansard, who placed fifth overall in 18:16.16. Rounding out Kent Island's top five were Gio Aguirre (13th, 18:57.87), Caleb Heater (16th, 19:14.01), Hudson Scarlett (24th, 19:39.31) and Jacob Keever (33rd, 20 06.24).\nThe top six teams in 2A qualified for states. North Caroline (7th, 180) finished seven points behind North Harford. Queen Anne's (183) was eighth and Easton (184) ninth. Easton's Will Orndoff (9th, 18:28.77) and North Caroline's Darly Ortega-Miguel (13th, 18:52.51) qualified as individuals, cracking the top 20.\nQueen Anne's Faith Gordon placed fourth (22:08.93) in the 2A girls competition, leading the Lions to third place (97 points). The top five girls teams qualified for states. Completing Queen Anne's top five were Addison Holden (14th, 23:43.87), Anna Schwinger (26th, 26:08.21), Emmalyn Dutton (33rd, 26:40.28) and Linda Vail (34th, 26:46.48).\n\"These girls, I love them,\" Agee said. \"They have some real depth on their team. We have 10 girls that are considered our varsity girls and they push each other. And if one of them is down in a race somebody picks up their space. They work so hard to get ahead of their competition, or race them at the end.\"\nNorth Caroline also qualified for states, finishing fifth with 119 points. The Bulldogs were led by Brooke Sullivan, who was eighth in 22:32.08. North Caroline's top five included Kelly Beauchemin (20th, 25.16.83), Shelby Andrew (24th, 25.46.03), Emmy Brown-Varon (31st, 26:35.51) and Shelby Rust (36th, 27.03.42).\nKent Island's Olivia Redgraves also qualified, finishing 16th in 24:21.59.\nCambridge-South Dorchester finished fourth in the Class 1A boys competition to earn a spot at Hereford next week. Tekai Drummond (7th, 18:18.14) led the Vikings followed by Preston Adkins (10th, 18:39.34), Jaasiel Molock (21st, 19:56.58), Joshia Harris (36th, 20:57.54) and Cincere Gibson (42nd, 21:47.63).\nCambridge-SD's girls also qualified, with Camryn Russum finishing 12th in 23:39.29. The Vikings' top five included Isabelle Hughes (21st, 26.099.99), Avery Shorter (28th, 27.11.67), Hailey Mills (32nd, 30:19.34) and Olivia Shiftler (35th, 31:51.88). \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 1,
