  {
    "title": "QA’s, Easton hoping for a Shore thing",
    "date": "2024-11-05",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "QA’s, Easton hoping for a Shore thing\n\n November 5, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 841\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n They battled each other in one of the season's more memorable duels, playing 80 scoreless minutes before settling things in 1v1s.That was Oct. 1, when Queen Anne's County High's field hockey team outlasted Easton in a shootout, part of a 14-game Lions' unbeaten streak.\nThe Warriors have not lost since that clash, reeling off eight straight victories.\nWednesday, 36 days later after that marathon, Queen Anne's and Easton prepare to play in the Class 2A state semifinals at Glen Burnie High School, not against each other, but hoping to possibly create the first all-Eastern Shore state final since 1997.\n\"How great would it be?\" Queen Anne's longtime head coach Shana Corder asked rhetorically. \"I am rooting for Easton and Queen Anne's to win this one. I think that would be an awesome setup.\"\nWarrior head coach Kim Neff seconded that notion.\n\"That would be so wild,\" Neff said of an all-Shore final that hasn't happened since Pocomoke defeated Kent County in the 1997 Class 1A championship. \"That would be so cool.\"\nOf course, Corder and Neff each know that dream match doesn't happen unless their respective teams get past a pair of unbeatens tonight.\nThird-seeded Queen Anne's (13-1-1) goes first, when it plays three-time defending state champion and No. 2 seed Glenelg (15-0) at 5:30 p.m. Fourth-seeded Easton (12-3) follows in a meeting with No. 1 seed Manchester Valley (15-0) at 7:30 p.m.\n\"OK. So we're not undefeated,\" Corder said. \"We're the underdog.\"\nThe Lions may have thought they would be wearing that underdog tag through at least part of this season after graduating a number of standouts off last year's 15-1 team that posted 14 shutouts and reached the state semifinals. Corder honestly thought this would be a rebuild season, since the entire defense from a year ago — with the exception of goalie Cassidy Alexander — was gone.\nBut after a season-opening loss at Pocomoke, Corder changed her position.\n\"I think they felt, 'Oh, we're not going to be as good,'\" Corder said. \"'We are not as good as this team.' But I was like, 'We are as good. You can't set yourself up to think you're a failure.' I think losing that game was the best blessing we've gotten all year.\n\"I saw their faces and they weren't used to losing, all my returners,\" Corder continued. \"And I felt like they didn't like to lose. So I thought that gave them the drive to want to win. I felt that was our turning point.\"\nSince then the Lions, behind their one-two goalie punch of Caroline Kimball and Alexander, and a rebuilt defense, have gone unbeaten, which included winning their second straight Bayside Conference and regional championships — a first in program history — and recording 11 shutouts.\n\"I feel every team we play has given us their best game because we did so well (last year),\" Corder said. \"They understand pressure. They've been here before. All the returners know what this feels like. Hopefully they already felt the pressure. We've had 1v1 games, overtime, blah, blah, blah. I'm just hoping they are able to rise to the occasion Wednesday.\"\nThough Easton didn't have a 2023 season that compared to Queen Anne's, Neff was filled with optimism at the outset of this season.\n\"I knew we were capable of getting this far,\" Neff said. \"A lot of these girls I've coached since they were in kindergarten, and I know their potential. It was the most athletic group that I have seen (during preseason tryouts). I think them playing some travel ball together and being on rec teams together, they know each other well, which helps.\"\nBut Easton's record was sitting at 4-3 after the 1v1 loss to Queen Anne's. The Warriors moved to 5-3 with a shutout of Kent County.\nThen came a 3-2 overtime victory over Pocomoke.\n\"We have a long history with Pocomoke,\" Neff said. \"And Pocomoke tends to get the victories. So I think that game, and going into overtime, and doing what they did really solidified them. Like I knew and I had the confidence in them. But I think it solidified the confidence in them, like, 'We can do this.'\"\nSince that victory, Easton has defeated perennial Bayside heavyweight Kent Island 1-0 in overtime. The Warriors defeated Parkside 2-1 in overtime in a 2A East Region II semifinal, then beat Stephen Decatur 2-1 with 11 seconds remaining in regulation in the region final. Easton continued its trend for the dramatic last Thursday, defeating Hereford in a 1v1 in a state quarterfinal, placing the Warriors in the state semifinals for the first time since 2021.\n\"I told them, 'You've earned your spot there,'\" Neff said. \"'We're all good teams. We made it to the top four. We're not looking at records because every day's a clean slate for every team. We haven't seen them (Manchester Valley). They haven't seen us. And so we're going in and we're playing our game. They're ready.\"\nNotes: Wednesday's winners will meet for the state final on Saturday at Stevenson University at 11 a.m.. … Third-seeded Pocomoke plays No. 2 seed Fallston Wednesday in a 1A state semifinal at Crofton High. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
