  {
    "title": "Blue, O'Connor lead Easton",
    "date": "2024-11-08",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Blue, O'Connor lead Easton\n\n November 8, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 978\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n EASTON — Matt Griffith delivered a familiar coaching lament.\"We still haven't played a full game,\" Easton High's head football coach said. \"And if we can ever figure that out before the season ends we're going to be OK.\"\nWell, the Warriors figured out one thing Thursday night — how to win a home playoff game.\nDustin Blue and Mikey O'Connor each rushed for three touchdowns, and Jordan Horsey had an interception and returned a second takeaway for a touchdown, as Easton won a playoff game on its home turf for the first time since 1993 with a 62-36 victory over Parkside in a Class 2A East Region first-round game.\nThe third-seeded Warriors (7-3) advance to the second round, where they will play at No. 2 seed Kent Island (7-3) at a date yet to be determined.\n\"We had more turnovers than they did,\" said Parkside head coach Brendan Riley, whose team committed three turnovers and had a punt blocked, leading to 24 Easton points.\nThe Warriors had four turnovers in a 37-30 loss to the Rams on Oct. 11. Thursday they had none.\n\"They came into our house and beat us,\" O'Connor said of the Oct. 11 loss. \"So we had like a chip on our shoulder. And the fact that we haven't won a home playoff game in a long time.\"\nThere were plenty of other reasons why Easton notched its first home playoff win since a 35-15 victory over Smithsburg in a 1993 Class 1A quarterfinal.\nBlue was a battering ram, carrying 24 times for 125 yards — both season highs — and three touchdowns, and also caught four passes for 33 yards and another TD.\n\"Dustin and the o-line. I believe that group and Dustin really controlled the game,\" O'Connor said. \"They carried us.\"\nO'Connor wasn't too shabby either. The senior quarterback carried 18 times for 92 yards and three touchdowns, and completed 13 of 19 passes for 165 yards and a touchdown.\n\"Everything is centered on Mikey,\" Riley said. \"I think he does a good job of commanding what they do.\"\nHorsey intercepted Parkside quarterback Chris Morandi on the fourth play from scrimmage, setting the Warriors up at the Rams' 5-yard line. Four plays later, Blue crashed through the middle for a 1-yard touchdown. Dominic Stanco, who was 8 for 8 on extra-point attempts, and booted field goals of 24 and 36 yards, tacked on the PAT for a 7-0 Easton lead with 8:39 left in the first quarter.\nThe sixth-seeded Rams (3-7), who had not won since their win at Easton, pulled within 7-6, when Morandi connected with Tavian Dashiell for a 79-yard touchdown pass with 3:47 to go in the first quarter. The Warriors maintained the lead though, when Logan Friend blocked the extra point.\nEaston answered with an 11-play, 65-yard march that ended with Blue blasting in from the 1 with 25 seconds remaining in the first quarter.\n\"He ran the ball unbelievable tonight,\" Griffith said of Blue. \"Very hard. Very committed. We knew coming in we wanted to pound the rock with him. He did exactly what we asked him to do: ball security. He was able to pick us up short yards and stuff. And he extended some big runs in the middle, taking advantage of some of the alignments they gave us.\"\nLess than 4 minutes into the second quarter, Rams senior running back Karontez Bratten (24 carries, 240 yards) scored on a 2-yard run. James Harmon's two-point run tied the game at 14-all.\nBut Friend gave the Warriors another short field when he blocked a punt. Parkside's punter kicked the ball out of bounds, giving Easton a safety. But Griffith opted to give up the points in favor of getting the ball at the Rams' 2-yard line. O'Connor scored on a 2-yard run with 5:20 left, then watched Stanco drill a 24-yard field goal as time ran out in the first half with Easton up 24-14.\nThe Warriors widened their lead to 31-14 on their first possession of the second half, as O'Connor rolled left and dumped a short pass to Blue for a 12-yard touchdown.\nBut Bratten, who had five touchdowns and rushed for 248 yards on 23 touches in the first meeting, broke free for a 59-yard touchdown jaunt and ran in the two-point conversion, shaving Easton's lead to 31-22 with 6:45 to go in the third.\n\"Is he a senior,\" Griffith asked of Bratten. \"I hope he is. I hope he's graduating and moving out of the country or something. He's a dude. He's good. We were in a lot of the right places and he just made us miss. We'd hit him and he'd bounce off. Unbelievable.\"\nEaston came right back though, as Blue, who bounced off defenders all game, scored on an 18-yard run around the right side, extending the Warrior lead to 38-22 with 4:12 still left in the third.\n\"Before the game, I expected the ball 30 times,\" Blue said. \"But just doing it for the seniors. I just love 'em so much, and I just want to keep playing with them on and on. So I'm just going to put it all on the field every single week.\"\nParkside wouldn't go away, as Harmon's 6-yard touchdown run and two-pointer trimmed the Rams' deficit to 38-30 with 2:50 left in the third.\nThe Warriors though took control from there, scoring the next 24 points.\nO'Connor's 1-yard touchdown run made it 45-30 with 1:17 left in the third. Kyree Acree intercepted Morandi on the first play of the fourth quarter, setting up Stanco's 36-yard field goal for an 18-point cushion. O'Connor was sprung for a 17-yard touchdown run, moving Easton to a 55-30 lead.\nHorsey then ended Parkside's next possession, when he stripped the ball from Bratten and raced 41 yards for a touchdown and a 62-30 lead.\nBratten added a 40-yard touchdown run with 2:12 left for the final score.\nEaston's 62 points were the most it scored in a playoff game in at least 46 years. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
