  {
    "title": "Thomas, Colonels romp into second round",
    "date": "2024-11-08",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Thomas, Colonels romp into second round\n\n November 8, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 718\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n AMERICAN CORNER — James Jackson had no explanation.\"This is new to me,\" Colonel Richardson High's head football coach said of the seemingly new-found speed of Bradley Thomas.\nThomas also struggled to provide any further insight.\n\"I don't know,\" Thomas said of his surprising higher gear. \"All my brothers are linemen so I have no clue where it came from. I guess I was gifted differently.\"\nThomas ran for a pair of touchdowns, including a 75-yard sprint late in the first half, and also returned a fumble for a touchdown Friday night to help the Colonels rout Snow Hill, 51-6, in the first round of the Class 1A East Region playoffs.\nFourth-seeded Colonel Richardson (6-4) advances to next week's second round, where it will travel to unbeaten No. 1 seed Cambridge-South Dorchester (9-0).\n\"I told them in the locker room, 'It's hard beating a team twice,'\" said Jackson, whose team defeated the Eagles 43-6 in Snow Hill on Oct. 11. \"'We got to go out, we got to score quickly. And then don't let up.' When we scored on that first drive I reminded the offensive linemen, 'Hey, we're not done, so don't go out on our next drive and lay an egg.'\"\nThe Colonels didn't, scoring on all four of their first-half possessions for a 32-0 halftime cushion. Colonel also scored the only two times it had the ball in the second half before Thomas' 9-yard fumble return for a touchdown made it 51-0 with with 5 minutes remaining.\nFifth-seeded Snow Hill (5-5) avoided a shutout, when Justin Shipley returned a kickoff 95 yards down the visitors' sideline for a touchdown in the closing minutes.\nWinners of five of their last six games, Colonel pieced together a 13-play, 70-yard scoring drive to open the game that included an 18-yard burst on fourth down by Thomas, and ended with quarterback Tarron Hammond's 5-yard touchdown run. Thomas ran in the two-point conversion for an 8-0 lead with 4 minutes, 44 seconds left in the first half.\nThe Colonels blew the game open with a 24-point second quarter. Derrick Mitchell scored on a 16-yard run and ran in the two-point try for a 16-0 lead on the first play of the quarter. Thomas bulled in from a yard out and Collin Brewer powered in the two-point run to make it 24-0 with 7:13 left before halftime.\n\"He started the year as a backup quarterback and fullback,\" Jackson said of Thomas. \"And then we were like, 'You know what? Maybe we should try Bradley at some wing.' He's a bruiser. We got two finesse running backs so we decided we need a pounder and he became that pounding back. Brings back that (Camron) Gondeck that we used to have (at running back).\"\nSnow Hill drove to the Colonel 11 on its next drive, but fumbled on a fourth-and-one. Three plays later, Thomas found open space on the left side and romped 75 yards for a touchdown with his jubilant head coach chasing him down the sideline.\n\"I was excited for him,\" Jackson said smiling. \"This is something you don't see with him. Usually he'll give you 10 to 15 yards, but when he breaks a long one it's exciting. I thought he really ran out of gas the last five yards, but he made it in.\n\"Tonight the way he ran the football, it was good to see,\" Jackson continued. \"The long runs he had tonight were impressive. He's not a speed guy. When I get in the locker room I'm going to ask him was he running scared, or has he found some speed somewhere.\"\nThomas finished with 168 yards on 10 carries.\n\"I just saw green,\" Thomas said of his 75-yard scoring dash. \"I was like, 'Just keep going. Don't fumble the ball. Just keep going.' And I kept going.\"\nHammond's 26-yard touchdown pass to Carter Tubitt, and Morgan Demir's point-after kick with 5:30 remaining in the third quarter pushed Colonel's lead to 39-0, and started the running clock.\nThomas then intercepted quarterback Dominic Kuipers on Snow Hill's next play from scrimmage, stirring Colonel's biggest sideline celebration of the night. The Colonels were back in the end zone two plays later, with Dominque Caven sweeping 28 yards around right end ffor a 45-0 lead.\nThomas capped Colonel's scoring two plays later when he grabbed a fumble and ran nine yards for his third touchdown of the game. \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
