  {
    "title": "Stakes raised in 2nd meeting between CSD, Colonel",
    "date": "2024-11-13",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Stakes raised in 2nd meeting between CSD, Colonel\n\n November 13, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 772\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n It was more of an announcement than analysis.\"That just wasn't us,\" Colonel Richardson High head football coach James Jackson said of his team's 23-0 loss at Cambridge-South Dorchester on Sept. 27.\nJackson wasn't disrespecting or discrediting the Vikings, though his reaction was certainly understandable considering a week earlier, the Colonels put up 33 points while flirting with an upset of Kent Island. Since that loss to the Vikings — the only time they've been shut out all year — the Colonels have won five of six in lopsided fashion.\nThe latest of those romps came last week during a 51-6 rout of Snow Hill, which propelled the fourth-seeded Colonels (6-4) into Friday's 6 p.m. rematch at top-seeded Cambridge-SD (9-0) in a Class 1A East second-round playoff game.\n\"It's always tough to beat a team twice in the same season,\" Vikings first-year head coach Rob Burnham said. \"I expect coach Jackson to have a solid game plan. I expect his kids to come out and play hard. And I expect the game to be more competitive than it was last time. And it's our job to make sure that it's not as competitive.\"\nColonel had one turnover and about half a dozen fumbled snaps from center in that Week 4 loss. But Jackson noted his team's biggest problem that first meeting was \"from our (offensive) line not knowing who to block. So correcting them, that changed a lot.\"\nThose changes have helped running back Derrick Mitchell gain a team-high 800 yards and score five touchdowns on 94 carries. Ja'Zir Dickerson, back after missing the last two games, has rushed for 774 yards and 11 touchdowns on 72 touches.\n\"After the Cambridge game, the running backs got a better understanding of hitting the holes,\" Jackson said. \"You can't overrun the holes and you can't assume just because you see the flash of a jersey that the hole is clogged. You've got to stay patient and run behind your linemen.\"\nThere has also been the emergence of Bradley Thomas (41-425, 3 TDs), who carried 10 times for 168 yards and two touchdowns during last week's first-round playoff against Snow Hill.\n\"We didn't use Bradley much at wing when we played them last time,\" Jackson said. \"I think having Bradley this game will give us a little more power.\"\nColonel also has quarterback Tarron Hammond, who has run for six touchdowns, and completed 19 of 37 passes for 662 yards, nine TDs and no interceptions.\nAnd while Colonel has improved, it still faces one of the Bayside Conference's most athletic and fastest defenses.\n\"I'm glad we don't have to block ourselves,\" Burnham said of his defense, which like Colonel logged three shutouts this season. \"Our kids run fast across the board and they pursue to the football. And we preach it in practice and we drill it. And we try to be technicians and do the little things well.\"\nSophomore K'Marion Hall, who was injured in the first Colonel game, returns to the defensive line, and will play tight end for an offense that has the potential to score on any down from anywhere.\n\"Well, those kids that run fast for us on defense also run fast for us on offense,\" Burnham said.\nLeading the offense is Anthony Hughes, who has been a problem for everyone he's faced this season. The senior quarterback, who also plays outside linebacker, has carried 108 times for 957 yards and 15 touchdowns, and completed 34 of 64 passes for 413 yards and seven touchdowns. Then there is Sterling Perkins Jr., who has rushed for 1,047 yards and 11 touchdowns on 105 touches.\n\"The first thing we've got to do is limit their explosive plays,\" Jackson said. \"We can't give up the big runs. You can't let a kid catch a 7-yard slant and turn it into a 50-yard run.\"\nJackson is also well aware of how Hughes and Perkins can shed and break tackles in an instant.\n\"You got to wrap up,\" Jackson said. \"And if you got him wrapped up, we can't assume that one person is going to bring either one of them down. We talk about that all the time: gang tackling. And don't assume someone's down. Somebody's got to come in and clean up.\"\nBurnham, who's never coached a team that had a first-round bye in the playoffs, said his team is healthy and doesn't expect the Vikings to be rusty.\n\"We want to score as fast as we can,\" Burnham said. \"But they are not going to roll over for us. They have a bunch of seniors and they don't want it to be their last game. And we have a bunch of seniors that don't want it to be their last game.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 13,
