  {
    "title": "North Dorchester's deepest run ends",
    "date": "2024-11-13",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "North Dorchester's deepest run ends\n\n November 13, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 557\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n GAMBRILLS — Carol Hubbard knows it takes time to build programs.In the spring of 2023, Hubbard guided North Dorchester High's softball team to its first Class 1A state championship.\nHubbard guided the Eagles to yet another first this year, leading the volleyball team to a Class 1A state semifinal berth for the first time in the program's 11-year history.\nThat bid for a state crown ended though Tuesday night at Arundel High School, as second-seeded Boonsboro swept No. 6 seed North Dorchester, 25-12, 25-6, 25-13 in that 1A state semifinal.\nThe Warriors (16-3) will play fifth-seeded Clear Spring (14-5) at noon on Saturday for the 1A state championship at Harford Community College's APG Federal Credit Union Arena.\n\"It was a huge accomplishment for them to get to this point,\" said Hubbard, whose team had a five-match win streak ended, finishing the year 12-7. \"This is the farthest that any team has gone in our program.\"\nNorth Dorchester scored the first point of the night, and stayed within two to three points of the Warriors early. But a four-point service run from Larissa Blair, that included a pair of kills from Rosslyn Briggs, helped give Boonsboro separation in Game 1 at 15-6. Another Briggs kill and an ace from Aly Phillips extended the Warriors lead to 19-8. Back-to-back errant returns by the Eagles closed the 25-12 set.\n\"Our game plan coming in is to always serve tough,\" Boonsboro seven-year head coach Shelby Wallace said. \"But we had a few select styles of serve that we wanted to execute today and I think we did that. Mostly the deep float and some short serves where appropriate.\"\nThose service combinations fueled Boonsboro's most dominant set of the match, as Devin Glines served 10 straight points before Blair reeled off a five-point run in Game 2.\n\"Serve-receive struggled,\" Hubbard said of her team. \"And then when we did get a decent pass we weren't able to capitalize on it on the front row. So it was just kind of a snowball. I think the front row was getting frustrated with the back row some, and then the back row would finally get (the ball) up and they were getting frustrated with the front row.\"\nA service error and back-to-back points off Katelyn Hopkins' serve gave North Dorchester a 4-3 edge in Game 3. Boonsboro rallied to a 9-5 lead. Brooke Outten answered, coupling a kill with consecutive aces to draw the Eagles within 9-8.\nBut Boonsboro steadily began widening its lead. A Warrior kill and two return errors bumped the gap to 13-8. Brooke Iager then delivered back-to-back aces to open a five-point service spree that gave Boonsboro a 19-9 lead. North Dorchester kept its deficit under 10 before Anna Jackson's kill and two errant returns capped the 25-13 Game 3 win.\n\"The third set early on we had a little run there and evened it up,\" Hubbard said. \"I tip my hat to them. They are a strong team.\n\"As far as an all-around balanced team, I would say they're the best that we've seen,\" Hubbard added of Boonsboro, which advanced to its first state final since 2007. \"They didn't have a weak spot. They were strong all the way around. You play some teams and they have a really strong outside hitter, or a really strong middle hitter. I would say they were as well-rounded a team as we've faced.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 13,
