  {
    "title": "Warriors, Bucs set for round two",
    "date": "2024-11-14",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Warriors, Bucs set for round two\n\n November 14, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 994\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Last Friday's 41-0 rout of James M. Bennett, was the first time Kent Island High head football coach Zach Davis had played an opponent twice in one season.Matt Griffith has seen the same team a handful of times during his two stints as Easton High's head coach. During that span, Griffith has watched his team lose to a 2A rival in the regular season then beat that same team in the postseason. He's also been on the flip side of that scenario.\nSo it's no surprise heading into Friday's 6 p.m. Class 2A East Region second-round playoff clash with the Buccaneers at Andy Schipul Stadium, that Griffith hasn't placed much stock in the Warriors' 43-24 victory over Kent Island on Sept. 27.\n\"Game one doesn't mean anything except they know a little bit about us, and we know a little bit about them,\" Griffith said. \"That's it.\"\nSecond-seeded Kent Island (7-3) enters the game on a four-game win streak. The third-seeded Warriors (7-3) opened the season with a four-game win streak, and arrive for this second encounter having won three of their last four.\n\"I think myself and my coaches have done a nice job of keeping them level, knowing that we're playing a really good football team that's coached very well, and that there's going to be adjustments and changes,\" Griffith said. \"We have to be able to respond to that and be able to play our brand of football. And hopefully we have a better night than they do.\"\nAdjustments figure to be coming from both sides.\nKent Island will look to contain Easton quarterback Mikey O'Connor, who rushed for 250 yards and three touchdowns, and passed for 146 yards and two touchdowns in the first game. The Warriors will again hope to limit the Bucs' two-pronged running attack of Jaxson Hess, who has carried 157 times this season for 987 yards and nine touchdowns, and Nate Green (101-825-9).\nAs far as wholesale changes?\n\"You have some wrinkles, but you can't change who you are,\" said Davis, who spent nine seasons coaching in West Virginia before taking over at Kent Island this year. \"You can't put in a whole new playbook. But you have some things that they haven't seen on tape and just hope your guys play well, get them ready to play.\"\nAfter losing 52-0 to defending 2A state champion Stephen Decatur, Kent Island has reeled off four straight wins, outscoring its opponents 122-35, which has led Davis to think his team has found its identity.\n\"We've embraced what kind of football works for us,\" Davis said. \"We are blue collar. We play old-school football. That's the style of football I prefer to play; I think our staff prefers to play. That's what we want to do. We want to be hard-nosed, have a hard edge. And we want to get the game to the fourth quarter because that's where we feel we are the best.\"\nOffensively, that means running the ball with Hess and Green.\n\"Kent Island has some really good running backs,\" Griffith said. \"Number 11 (Green) is fast and very strong and athletic. Twenty-two (Hess) is what I consider one of the better running backs in the Bayside (Conference) as well. He hits the hole hard. He's a powerful runner.\n\"What they do they do very well,\" Griffith added. \"We're going to be challenged and have to tackle well, and do our jobs up front to give our skill guys opportunities to make plays for us.\"\nEaston also has to be concerned with quarterback Gavan Henry (38 carries, 142 yards, 7 touchdowns), who completed 35 of 94 for 607 yards and seven touchdowns — two coming against the Warriors — and his top target Jackson Towers (8 receptions, 207 yards, 2 touchdowns).\nO'Connor figures to again be the focal point for the Bucs' defense. The senior has thrown for 1,761 yards and 19 touchdowns, and rushed for 1,005 yards and 16 TDs. He also has an array of targets with Chase Raab (519 yards, 8 touchdowns), Colin Mooney (388-4), Diontaye Copper (339-5), Carter Mooney (304-1) and Zach Barnhart.\nBut the Warriors were aided greatly in last Thursday's 62-36 first-round win over Parkside by junior running back Dustin Blue (579 yards), who last week ran for 125 yards and three touchdowns, and also had a touchdown reception.\n\"I think Mikey and Dustin, they offset each other and complement each other very well,\" Griffith said. \"And I think that's part of what's taken place now. Mikey might have done some things earlier in the season that people want to take away now, and that allows Dustin to be able to showcase his ability. He's always been a very good running back for us and he's going to get some more opportunities based on what teams do against us.\"\nGriffith has called his offensive line a work in progress throughout the season, but has seen improvement. He knows that unit will again have to play well in Friday's round two.\n\"We're going up against a really good defensive front,\" Griffith said. \"They're going to have to have a really good game if we're going to be successful. Defensively, they're going to give us multiple looks, they're going to bring pressure. We've got to control the football. And we've got to take what they give us and not force things. One of the things that we had a lot of success with the first time is we didn't get silly trying to force things.\"\nExecution and limiting turnovers and penalties have been stressed by both coaching staffs since the preseason. But Davis thinks if he and the Bucs are to even the slate against Easton they will have to stay true to their identity.\n\"We got to be able to run the football and stop the run,\" Davis said. \"We rush for over 200 yards and we win. That's what we're going to try to do on offense. On defense, if we can hold them to 100 yards or less rushing, we're going to win. That's what we want to do.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 14,
