  {
    "title": "STATE CHAMPS",
    "date": "2024-11-16",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "STATE CHAMPS\n\n November 16, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 1085\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n BALTIMORE — Mike Kern's belief never wavered.From the beginning of the season, Queen Anne's County High's girls head soccer coach said his team could compete for a state title. Kern said it after wins. He seemed more emphatic after losses. He continued saying it one narrow postseason victory after another.\n\"We just have to put the ball in the back of the net,\" Kern said.\nKara Ringold did that twice early Saturday afternoon as Queen Anne's shut out C. Milton Wright, 2-0, to win the Class 2A state championship at Loyola University's Ridley Athletic Complex.\nIt was the first state title in the history of Queen Anne's girls soccer program, and the first by a Bayside Conference school since the Maryland Public Secondary School Athletic Association began holding the girls state championship in 1989.\n\"At the beginning of the season our goal was to get to a state championship,\" said Kern, who has been the Lions head coach for 24 years. \"To win it, this is like a bonus. We knew we had the team.\"\nWhether it was early-game jitters or the strong winds that whipped across the field, neither team mounted much offense through the first 12 minutes. C. Milton Wright (12-5), which was playing into the wind during the first half, had two early chances, the first coming on a corner kick 5 minutes in that produced no shot. Then with 28:30 left in the half, Emma Jankowiak broke in alone only to have Queen Anne's Finley Patchett angle in to take the ball away.\n\"I think the first half, playing into the wind, that was really difficult,\" Mustangs head coach Andrew Harrell said. \"It was probably the toughest conditions we've played with all season. We've played with a little bit of drizzle, we've played with cold, we played with hot. But the wind is something we've never played with. And it's definitely a game-changer, especially in the first half. Playing into the wind, it's like you're playing against an extra person.\"\nQueen Anne's (14-3-1) ended the scoreless duel more than 14 minutes later.\nShannon Rath struck a beautiful ball on a direct kick toward the center of the goal that C.M. Wright's Nani Milich headed. The ball drifted toward the right post. Mustangs goalie Alix Bramble moved to her left in an attempt to knock the ball away. But Queen Anne's Morgan Scott headed the ball back across the crease to an ummarked Ringold, who got her right foot on the ball for a tap-in and a 1-0 lead with 14 minutes, 47 seconds left in the first half.\n\"Shannon hit a great ball and then Morgan got a head on it and I just hit it right in,\" Ringold said.\nThe Lions, who won their state quarterfinal and semifinal on penalty kicks, scored in regulation for the first time since their 2-0 victory over Kent Island on Oct. 29 in the Class 2A East Region II final.\n\"I think it gave us all a lot of comfort because we have been trying to get that goal in regulation,\" Queen Anne's senior midfielder Olivia Ryan said. \"And we ended up getting another one. But we wanted to at least get one. So it gave us a lot of comfort and we all could relax a little bit, but still have the momentum to keep going.\"\nThe fifth-seeded Mustangs got a corner kick with 51 seconds left in the first half, and then back-to-back corners in the opening 4 minutes of the second half, but put nothing on Lions sophomore keeper Bea Holthaus, who notched her 12th shutout of the season.\n\"Our goalie saved our butt a lot of times this season,\" Ryan said of Holthaus, who after giving up a goal in the regional semifinals posted four straight shutouts to end the season.\nHaving a stellar defense also helped.\n\"A lot of us have played together since we were very little so we kind of know each other's habits,\" senior center back Shannon Rath said. \"That definitely helps a lot. Our communication is what gets us through. We are constantly talking, tracking down runners. That's just the only way we know how to do it.\"\nTen minutes after the Mustangs' second corner of the half, Queen Anne's put together another score.\nMia Barnette came down left wing and touched the ball to Taylor Napier, who pushed toward the middle of the field and dished to Ringold. With two defenders in front of her, and a third closing in from the left, Ringold settled the ball then quickly drove a left-footed shot inside the right post for a 2-0 lead with 26:05 left in the game.\n\"We were just on an attack and then we had really good passing on the left side, and Taylor (Napier) played me a great ball and I just took a touch around the defender and hoped for the best, and it went in,\" Ringold said. \"it was just one of the best moments of my life.\n\"Kara was in the right place at the right time that first goal,\" Kerr said. \"But that second goal, she earned that goal. There was a lot of things that led up to it. But when she got the ball she had to do a couple things there to get a good shot off and put it far post, low.\"\nC.M. Wright got a shot off from outside the box that Holthaus caught in the midsection with 13:39 left, then sent a corner kick through the box and out of bounds with 6:37 left. The Mustangs didn't build a serious scoring threat the rest of the game.\n\"I think when you get to this stage, you watch enough game film to come up with a game plan for the other team,\" Harrell said. \"But what you can't game plan for is the work ethic and the hard work and that kind of stuff. And I think Queen Anne's brought a ton of intensity and a lot of hard work today. They beat us to balls for the majority of the game. That was one of our halftime talks; we got to do better getting to the ball first.\"\nIt was Queen Anne's first state finals appearance since 2003, when it lost 2-0 to Loch Raven.\n\"I felt like last year we had the team,\" Kern said. \"We did get some injuries at the end of the year and things didn't go our way. But we had a good core of the group back and thought this was going to be the year for us. Everything just kind of fell into place for us.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
