  {
    "title": "Blueford, Vikings rise up to get past stubborn Colonels",
    "date": "2024-11-20",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Blueford, Vikings rise up to get past stubborn Colonels\n\n November 20, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 533\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Colonel Richardson High head football coach James Jackson didn't think Cambridge-South Dorchester was 23 points better than his team in the fourth week of the season.The Colonels certainly proved that last Friday night, as they battled the top-seeded and undefeated Vikings down to the wire in a Class 1A East Region second-round playoff. But with 1 minute, 15 seconds remaining, senior wide receiver Antonio Blueford made a leaping grab in the back of the end zone on Anthony Hughes' pass for the winning touchdown, lifting Cambridge-SD to a 14-7 victory.\nThe top-seeded Vikings (10-0) will host three-time defending Class 1A state champion Fort Hill (4-7) on Friday at 6:30 p.m. in a 1A state quarterfinal. The Sentinels, who forfeited five victories this season for using an ineligible player, outscored their two regional opponents by a combined 103-20.\n\"Even though our season ended, the way we played, and Cambridge was the number one undefeated team (in the 1A East Region), I couldn't be more proud of our guys the way they stepped up,\" said Jackson, whose team lost 23-0 to the Vikings during the regular season. \"We showed we shouldn't have lost that game by 23 points. We should have been in that game, but we didn't play to our potential. So when we play to our potential I feel like we can play with anybody.\"\nCambridge-SD took a 7-0 lead in the first quarter when Hughes' broke up the middle for a touchdown run and Collin Bair tacked on the extra point.\nColonel (6-5) answered in the second quarter, when Ja'Zir Dickerson (18 carries, 82 yards) ran off right tackle for a touchdown run inside run 10-yard line. Morgan Demir added the extra point.\nAn interception by Colonel's Jay Wilkins kept the game tied at 7-7.\nIt remained that way until late in the fourth quarter, when Cambridge-SD drove inside Colonel's 5-yard line. The Vikings were backed up after getting tagged with an unsportsmanlike conduct penalty. But Hughes then dropped back and arced a pass that Blueford jumped and came down with despite good coverage.\n\"It was just a great ball,\" Blueford said. \"I just went up and got it and the game was on the line. I knew it was going to be a good pass. I just had to come down with it.\"\nIt was the lowest point total of the season for the Vikings.\n\"We had opportunities,\" Cambridge-SD head coach Rob Burnham said. \"Right before the half we got a short field and got inside the 5 and then ended up kicking a field goal off the left upright and missing it. Then early in fourth quarter we got inside the 20 and threw an interception.\n\"They played well and tackled well and took some things away that they gave us last game,\" Burnham added of Colonel. \"We took some things away that we gave them last game. They played better offensively than they did the first time. A win's a win and we'll take it.\"\nT.J. Wenger had 14 tackles for Colonel, Chatydrick Pickrom had 13, Collin Brewer nine, and Chris Rockwell eight.\n\"I thought our defense held up,\" Jackson said. \"Even on the last play we were in perfect position. On that play Blueford just made a great catch.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 20,
