  {
    "title": "Easton playing with familiar tag",
    "date": "2024-11-21",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Easton playing with familiar tag\n\n November 21, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 736\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Underdog.Easton High's football team started the season with that tag when it was ranked 10th in a preseason poll.\nThe Warriors steadily shedded that label through a 6-3 regular season, won two Class 2A East Regional playoffs, and are one of three Bayside Conference teams still playing along with defending 2A state champion Stephen Decatur and undefeated Cambridge-South Dorchester, the No. 1 seed in Class 1A.\nNow, as it heads into Friday's Class 2A state quarterfinal at second-seeded Milford Mill (9-1) at 6:30 p.m., No. 7 seed Easton (8-3) returns to its familiar role.\n\"We know we're the underdog,\" Warrior head coach Matt Griffith said. \"And we love it. And while we're happy with a lot of things we've done this year, we're not done. We have a lot of work still to do.\n\"Yes, we're playing a very good football team,\" Griffith said of the Millers, who won the 2A state title two years ago then lost in last year's state semifinals to eventual state champ Decatur. \"Yes, they're very athletic. Yes, they're big. Yes, they're fast. We're focused on not who's the toughest, but who can be the toughest the longest. We need to be able to take their punches early and be able to respond. And if we can do that I like our chances, if we can keep it close at the end of the game.\"\nIf the Warriors are to keep it close, they will have to limit a Millers team that scored 32 or more points in eight of their nine victories this season.\nLeading the way is junior quarterback Owen Newberns, who has completed 88 of 140 passes for 1,586 yards, 18 touchdowns and just four interceptions. Easton's secondary figures to be challenged by receivers Romero Ison (33 receptions, 534 yards, five touchdowns) and Derrell Pierson (23-497-6). Then there's running back Damon Ferguson, who has carried 72 times for 975 yards and 12 touchdowns.\n\"Each team is different because they have a different formula for winning,\" Milford Mill head coach Reggie White said. \"It's really cool to coach these different types of teams. One year you may have a running team. One year you may have a passing team. One year you might have a ferocious defense.\n\"So this year's team, we've got some guys who are very, very athletic,\" White continued. \"We've got some guys who can break a game open. It's the little nuances that you try to lean on during the game to help you win.\"\nThe Millers also have the biggest offensive line the Warriors have faced all year,\n\"Their center is like 6-(foot)-6, 320,\" Griffith said. \"They run tight zone and outside zone really, really well. They got a really fast running back. Their quarterback is super athletic and he can throw the deep ball to receivers who are really athletic.\"\nEaston has a really athletic quarterback of its own in senior Mikey O'Connor, who has completed 123 of 228 passes for 1,908 yards, 21 touchdowns and 14 interceptions, and also run for 1,247 yards and 18 touchdowns on 186 carries.\n\"The first thing has to start with the signal-caller, their quarterback,\" White said when asked his biggest concerns with Easton. \"He's a lefty. He's different. He has it. And I know about the O-line. They might be young, but they'll fight you.\"\nThat young front will look to open avenues for not only O'Connor, but junior running back Dustin Blue, who has carried 107 times for 632 yards and seven touchdowns.\nThe Warriors also have a fleet of receivers, which include Chase Raab (24 receptions, 519 yards, 8 touchdowns) and Diontaye Copper (21-382-6), who had the winning touchdown catch in last Friday's 28-21 second-round win over Kent Island. O'Connor's other targets are Colin Mooney (32-452-5), Carter Mooney (16-304-1) and Zach Barnhart (10-115-1).\n\"They're very athletic on defense,\" Griffith said of Milford Mill. \"They're overly aggressive at times, which we're hoping we can take advantage of, and try to be a little patient in how we do things. Try to make them uncomfortable. No one's really made them uncomfortable this year except Loyola-(Blakefield), who beat them.\"\nThe Warriors, who have won four of their last five, also have to continue protecting the football. They have committed just one turnover in their last two games.\nWhite also noted keeping mistakes to a minimum.\n\"I think our biggest obstacle is when we look in the mirror; it's ourselves sometimes,\" White said. \"We're just trying to eliminate mental errors and play a whole game.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 11,
    "day": 21,
