  {
    "title": "Sentinels end Vikings' unbeaten run",
    "date": "2024-11-22",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Sentinels end Vikings' unbeaten run\n\n November 22, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 709\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CAMBRIDGE — It was a great run.And for one half, it looked like top-seeded Cambridge-South Dorchester High's football team might extend that run, as it carried momentum and a six-point lead into the locker room.\nBut Fort Hill shut down the Vikings in the second half en route to a 30-12 come-from-behind victory Friday night in a Class 1A state quarterfinal played in a steady, wind-driven rain.\n\"Kids worked really hard. Coaches worked really hard,\" said Cambridge-SD head coach Rob Burnham, whose team finished the season 10-1. \"Didn't end the way we wanted it to end. It hurts. It really does.\"\nEighth-seeded Fort Hill (5-7) took a 6-0 lead with just under 8 minutes left in the first quarter, when Jabril Daniels broke loose down the visitors' sideline for a 54-yard touchdown run. Jacob Bone's point-after kick was no good.\nThe Sentinels carried that 6-0 lead into the second quarter. But Cambridge-SD recovered a second Fort Hill fumble at the Vikings 46. Cambridge-SD quarterback Anthony Hughes hit Majestic Jones for a 20-yard gain to the Sentinels' 31. Six plays later, Hughes found Ka'Marii Stanley for an 8-yard touchdown pass. Fort Hill blocked Collin Bair's point-after attempt, leaving the score tied at 6-all with 4:44 remaining in the first half.\nThe three-time defending state champions lost a third fumble on their next possession that Hughes returned to the Fort Hill 15. The Sentinels defense stiffened. But on fourth-and-four, Hughes hit Antonio Blueford for a 9-yard touchdown pass and a 12-6 lead. Fort Hill was called for pass interference on the two-point attempt. Hughes tried running the two-point conversion in, but was stopped short.\n\"We had opportunities in the first half and we capitalized,\" Burnham said.\nDespite being held to its lowest first-half point total in these playoffs, Fort Hill didn't panic.\n\"We weren't playing our style of football,\" Fort Hill head coach Zack Alkire said. \"Lot of guys were down, probably feeling a bit sorry for themselves and we just tried to remind them that we're only down six points. It only takes one play to get going. And we got that one play early and we didn't stop from there.\"\nCambridge-SD took the second-half kickoff and gained a first down on an 18-yard carry by Sterling Perkins Jr. The Vikings went backwards from there, a low snap from center resulted in a 7-yard sack of Hughes on third down. The Vikings attempted to salvage a second low snap from center on the ensuing punt, but turned the ball over on downs, giving Fort Hill possession at the Cambridge-SD 16.\nDaniels worked his way through the left side before reaching the ball across the goal for a 15-yard touchdown run. Bone kicked the extra point to give the Sentinels a 13-12 led with 9:24 to go in the third quarter.\nA short kickoff return and an 8-yard sack of Hughes forced another Vikings' punt, giving Fort Hill another short field at the 35. The Sentinels moved 34 yards before Daniels scored on a 1-yard run on fourth-and-goal. Daniels' two-point run extended Fort Hill's lead to 21-12 with 2:13 left in the third quarter.\n\"Jabril's a tough player,\" Alkire said. \"Jabril, he's all heart, he's all emotion. He was trying to put too much on his shoulders (in the first half). He was able to control himself a little better in the second half. Can't say enough great things about him. He's the leader of our team for sure.\"\nFort Hill widened its lead to 28-12 with 8:25 remaining when Braelyn Younger caught a 9-yard touchdown pass from Nash Cassell with 8:25 left.\nOn the ensuing kickoff, Cambridge-SD's Willie Jackson slipped at the 1-yard line. On the next play, Fort Hill's Noah House shot through the line and tackled Hughes for a safety and a 30-12 lead.\n\"Defense was great,\" Alkire said. \"Defense was really good all night long. We gave up two cheap ones, but for the most part I thought we did a nice job all night long on defense.\"\nFort Hill killed almost 5 minutes of clock before turning the ball over on downs to the Vikings with 3:05 left. Hughes hit Perkins for a gain down to the Fort Hill 30. But the Vikings were flagged for an ineligible man downfield.\n\"We didn't make plays when we needed to,\" Burnham said. \"They did.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
