  {
    "title": "Seahawks on record pace",
    "date": "2024-11-27",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Seahawks on record pace\n\n November 27, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 867\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n Stephen Decatur High made history last year when it became the first Bayside Conference school to win a state football championship in 26 years.Not quite one year later, the Seahawks are making yet another historic bid. But don't expect head coach Jake Coleman to get overly excited just yet.\n\"I don't think being on the cusp of history means a whole lot,\" Coleman said. \"It just means you ain't done it. It's kind of like when people say, 'You've got potential.'\"\nThe top-seeded Seahawks (12-0) can put themselves in position to challenge for a second straight state title match Friday, when they host No. 4 seed Westlake (10-2) in a Class 2A state semifinal at 6:30 p.m. in Berlin.\nA win would also give defending-champion Decatur at chance to challenge for a second consecutive title, something that hasn't been done since Coleman slipped on his helmet and fastened his chin strap for Cambridge-South Dorchester in 1995 and '96.\nColeman thought his team had the chance to contend for a second straight state title this season, despite the graduation of his son and starting quarterback Brycen Coleman, and fellow standouts Gavin Solito and Careen Bolden. Decatur's head coach also knew Brycen's successor behind center, junior Johnny Hobgood, needed support from this year's senior cast.\n\"Last year on the offensive side you had an extra special player obviously with Brycen,\" said Coleman, whose son now plays flex tight end at Vanderbilt. \"And he had some guys around him that he was able to lift up and show them the way.\"\nSo before this season began, Coach Coleman told seniors and Division I commits Davin Chandler (Vanderbilt), Trybe Wise (Georgetown) and Ethan Bradshaw (Army), as well as his offensive line, it was their turn to provide support.\n\"I told them, 'Johnny's got really, really big shoes to fill,\"' Jake Coleman said. \"'So I don't want to hear that the ball was not perfect. I don't want to hear any excuses. We got to play like guys with experience and we got to start playing like Division I athletes, too.' And they really have. And they've given Johnny the pocket that's clean. He's thrown balls and they've made plays. And really it's been exactly what I knew was possible for this group.\"\nHobgood has been outstanding, completing 107 of 157 passes for 1,824 yards, 30 touchdowns and just two interceptions. He's also run for four touchdowns.\n\"Johnny Hobgood is playing as well offensively as any player in Bayside history,\" Coleman said. \"And the thing you won't see within those stats is he's played probably 90 percent of the games this year only playing two quarters, because we've been running the clock on people.\"\nHobgood injured his leg during the Seahawks' 2A East Regional first-round victory over North Caroline. He sat out Decatur's 19-14 second-round win over Wicomico, but returned for last Friday's 37-0 state-quarterfinal rout of Walkersville, completing 8 of 13 for 93 yards and four touchdowns.\n\"Johnny throws the ball on time,\" Coleman said. \"He's accurate. He can make plays with his legs when he needs to. But Johnny is that pro-style quarterback, where he throws on time and he's accurate, and he goes through his reads and his process.\"\nThen there's that supporting cast.\nChandler has 29 receptions for 676 yards and eight touchdowns. Wise has snagged 28 passes for 512 yards and a team-high 10 TDs. Evan Copeland has 24 catches for 323 yards and seven touchdowns. And Zakhari Baker has totaled 26 grabs for 420 yards and seven touchdowns.\n\"Zakhari Baker, he starts the season off with a kickoff return for a touchdown and I don't think he's slowed down since,\" Coleman said. \"He has just made explosive play after explosive play all season long. We have other good wide receivers, but Zahkari Baker has just been amazing this year.\"\nColeman said the same thing of running back Vernon DeShields, who's carried 97 times for 803 yards and 10 touchdowns.\nDecatur also had a big hole to fill at inside linebacker with Solito's graduation. But that was the lone loss on a defense that returned its other 10 starters, and put together a string of five straight shutouts this year. Filling Solito's void have been linebackers Peyton Travers and Kyle Mumford.\nThat group prepares for its toughest test of the season in Westlake, which is led by senior running back Devin Cox (180 carries, 1,554 yards, 29 touchdowns) and junior quarterback William Drakeford Jr. (103 of 164, 1,169 yards, 14 touchdowns, 4 interceptions).\n\"Super fast,\" Coleman said of the Wolverines, who have shut out six of their last eight opponents. \"They are probably the fastest defense that we have seen.\n\"Offensively, they're a power football team,\" Coleman added. \"They got a really good running in (Cox), and he's tough to tackle. They've got an athletic dual threat quarterback. They've got weapons on the outside. It's exactly who you expect to see out a 10-2 semifinal team. They're definitely a team that has the ability to win it.\"\nSo does Decatur.\n\"This is right where I thought we'd be, is being contender,\" Coleman said. \"Last year we were young. Now, we have experience. They're senior laden and they've been through the fire. Now, we just want to get ourselves back to Annapolis (site of the state title game).\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
