  {
    "title": "Decatur bids for second straight state title in rematch with Huntingtown",
    "date": "2024-12-06",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Decatur bids for second straight state title in rematch with Huntingtown\n\n December 6, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 994\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n His team was undefeated and still wearing the tag of defending state champion.And though Stephen Decatur High trailed 14-0 at halftime in last Friday's Class 2A state semifinal, Seahawks head coach Jake Coleman said he felt no stress.\n\"I was pretty calm. Having to win a game when your son's a senior and it's the state championship, that was pressure,\" said Coleman, whose son Brycen quarterbacked Decatur to last year's 2A state crown.\n\"We came out very determined,\" Coleman said of last Friday's semifinal against Westlake. \"I told them, 'Hey, we just got to get that first score.'\"\nBut then the Wolverines widened their advantage to 20-0 with 3 minutes, 34 seconds left in the third quarter.\n\"I just told the guys, 'Keep fighting. Keep coaching,'\" Coleman said. \"Yeah, we were at the precipice of breaking, but we didn't. And we kept saying, 'Hey, get one score and then get one stop.' And as soon as we got the score, it was like, you could feel something changing on the field. Our guys were like, 'Oh, OK. We're still good.'\"\nThe Seahawks proved to be real good, reeling off the game's final 27 points for a 27-20 overtime victory that launched them into Saturday's Class 2A state title game against Huntingtown (10-2) at 3:30 p.m. at Navy-Marine Corps Memorial Stadium in Annapolis.\nA win Saturday would make Decatur (13-0) only the fourth football team in Bayside history to win back-to-back state titles, joining James M. Bennett (1982-83), Kent County (1989-90) and Cambridge-South Dorchester (1995-96).\n\"So now we have the challenge,\" Coleman said of the rematch with Huntingtown, which Decatur defeated 21-13 in last year's title game. \"I told my seniors that are returning state champions, I was like, 'Hey. Are you satisfied? You got your ring. We got other guys that don't have a ring. I know they're going to be ready and they want it. And in practice this week, in the game, I don't know when, but you've got to answer that bell. Is this something you want?\n\"'You want to go back-to-back?'\" Coleman continued. \"'Because I can tell you this, Huntingtown has been thinking about you just like you were thinking about Milford Mill (which eliminated Decatur in the 2022 state semifinals, then lost to the Seahawks in last year's semis), and how you were waiting for the opportunity to go and beat them. Everything they've done has been for this moment. So they got the edge and now you've got to answer that question. Do you want it more than they want it? Do you want to go back-to- back?' We'll find out Saturday.\"\nOf course, it wasn't looking like there would be a rematch with Westlake holding a 20-0 lead deep into last Friday's third quarter. That's when Coleman got into his defense.\n\"I told them, 'No excuses, man,'\" Coleman said. \"'This offense has been making it easy for us all year. It's our time to step up.' When we got the (first) score, we said, 'Hey, let's get 'em three-and-out. And if we get three-and-out, I promise you that offense is going to score.' And they did. We went three-and-out, and boom. We go right down field like we have done on many teams this season.\"\nDecatur's comeback began when junior quarterback Johnny Hobgood connected with Zakhari Baker for a 38-yard touchdown pass with 34 seconds left in the third, and Blake Wallace booted the extra point, pulling the Seahawks within 20-7.\nThe defending champs pared their deficit to 20-14 with 6:15 left in the game on Nate Tapley's 2-yard touchdown run and Wallace's PAT.\n\"Once we got it to 20-14, I could just tell our guys were so determined,\" Coleman said. \"I saw Johnny Hobgood transform. He goes from the first half really struggling, and then I saw him all of a sudden start to get that look. Everything just slowed down. Our offensive line starting handling them a little bit better.\"\nDavin Chandler caught a 10-yard touchdown pass from Hobgood with 1:03 left, but Wallace missed the extra point, leaving the game knotted at 20-all and forcing overtime.\nWestlake opted to go on defense first. Hobgood scored on an 8-yard run and Wallace converted the PAT to make it 27-20 before Decatur's defense stopped the Wolverines on their OT possession.\nNow, comes Saturday's rematch.\n\"They're a premier program,\" Coleman said of the Huntingtown. \"They're running the ball a little bit more than last year. Last year they were a little bit more balanced run to pass. This year a little bit heavier in the run in the games that we've seen. They certainly can pass unquestionably, and they are great on defense.\"\nThe Hurricanes' ground game is led by senior Landon Cawley (101 carries, 670 yards, 13 touchdowns) and juniors Gabriel Copeland (93-666-8) and Brody Whittington (53-542-5). Senior quarterback Dominic Hickman has completed 63 of 116 passes for 920 yards, six touchdowns and six interceptions.\nSenior defensive end Evan Powell, a West Virginia University commit, is part of a defensive unit that will look to contain the Seahawks' run and passing game.\nHobgood has connected on 120 of his 190 attempts this season for 1,973 yards, 32 touchdowns and just two interceptions. His receiving fleet includes Chandler (36 receptions, 762 yards, 9 touchdowns), who Wednesday signed with Vanderbilt, and Trybe Wise (29-521-10), who signed with Georgetown. The Canes also have Baker (28-457-8) to contend with, and a running attack led by Vernon DeShields (106 carries, 855 yards, 10 touchdowns).\n\"You look across the board, all the teams that are playing in the state championship, all of them can run the ball,\" Coleman said. \"All of them have good defenses. All of them have quarterbacks that most of the time are dual-threat kids. And they got playmakers. They are good in all three phases and they're well coached. And Huntingtown is no different.\n\"And I know that they are hungry,\" added Coleman of the Hurricanes, who are making their fourth state championship appearance. \"(They) haven't been able to win one yet. It's going to be a great challenge.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
    "content_source": "full_document",
    "year": 2024,
    "month": 12,
    "day": 6,
    "llm_classification": {
      "topic": "Sports",
      "explanation": "The article focuses on a high school football team's state championship run, detailing games, coaches, and players, which aligns directly with the Sports category."
    },
