  },
  {
    "title": "Vikings' Hughes adds to big year",
    "date": "2024-12-19",
    "author": "Bill Haufe",
    "content": "Vikings' Hughes adds to big year\n\n December 19, 2024 | Star Democrat, The (Easton, MD)\n\n Author/Byline: Bill Haufe | Section: Sports \n \n 608\n Words \n\n Read News Document\n\n CAMBRIDGE — It's already been quite a school year for Cambridge-South Dorchester High's Anthony Hughes.As a first-year starting quarterback, Hughes led the Vikings' football team to an undefeated regular season while earning the Bayside Conference Player of the Year.\nThe senior earned yet another milestone Thursday night, scoring 10 points to reach 1,000 for his career during Cambridge-SD's 73-62 victory over Queen Anne's County.\n\"It felt great because all my teammates encouraged me to get it,\" said Hughes, who scored on a tip-in with under 3 minutes remaining for his 1,000th-career point. \"They knew I wanted it. I felt like they wanted to get it more than I wanted it myself to get it.\"\nIt may have been the toughest 10 points Hughes has scored during his basketball career.\n\"Kind of felt like I wanted it more than I thought,\" Hughes admitted. \"I was putting too much pressure on myself.\"\nHughes scored on a 3-pointer with 4 minutes, 38 seconds remaining in the first quarter, but wouldn't score the remainder of the first half. It didn't matter though, as Cambridge-SD (5-0 overall, 3-0 North Bayside) went on a 16-4 tear to close the first half for a 37-24 halftime lead.\nSemaj Pinder (17 points) had seven points in the run, starting it with a 3-pointer. Thomas Wilson Jr. (18 points) netted five points, including a 3-pointer during the closing blitz, and Koby Ennals (game-high 20 points) added four points, including a dunk.\n\"We got back to ourselves,\" Vikings head coach Shawn Tucker said. \"Defense first. That's our motto. And Thomas Wilson Jr. stepped up and hit some big-time shots and that ballooned the lead for us. We were just playing CSD basketball the right way.\"\nA lay-in from Jimmy Holthaus (team-high 19 points) and an Amari Baynard 3-pointer pulled Queen Anne's (4-2, 2-2) within 37-29 in the opening 46 seconds of the second half.\nBut Wilson buried a trey, scored a layup, hit another 3, and converted a pair of free throws, widening the Vikings' lead to 47-33. Hughes then scored a bucket and made 1 of 2 from the foul line to make it 50-35 with 2:42 left in the third quarter.\nThe Vikings extended their lead to as much as 60-41 at the end of the third quarter.\nBut as Cambridge-SD was focusing on trying to get Hughes his milestone on his home floor, its offense began to sputter some, allowing the Lions to slowly close their deficit, as a Chance Hegedus bucket and two Ayden Hicks free throws capped a 14-5 run that drew Queen Anne's within 65-55 with 3:41 left.\nBut Ennals scored on a drive before Hughes reached his milestone with a tip-in, bumping the Vikings' lead back to 69-55.\n\"This is a milestone,\" Tucker said. \"We know who we are. We didn't want it (the opportunity for Hughes to score his 1,000th point at home) to slip. We missed some free throws at the end, but those are some things we can correct. The most important thing was for him to have his night, and we were going to do everything in our power for him to get it tonight. And most importantly, we got the W.\"\nBut not before Queen Anne's made things a bit more interesting, trimming its deficit to 70-63 on Holthaus' layup with 17.7 seconds left. The Lions would get no closer though, as their three-game win streak was ended.\n\"I told them, 'We won the second half,'\" said Queen Anne's head coach Jeff Hollis, who got 16 points from Baynard and 14 from Hegedus. \"We just can't have a second quarter like we did. They outscored 23-8 in the second quarter and that was just too much to come back from.\" \n\n © Copyright © 2024 Star Democrat, Chesapeake Publishing Group (Adams Publishing/APGMedia). All rights reserved.",
